window.CHAPTERS = [{"id": "readme", "path": "README.md", "part": "Overview", "title": "📖 Start Here — Curriculum & Study Plan", "md": "# 🧩 Low-Level Design Mastery — Java Edition\n\n> Clean, complete, from-scratch notes to take you from **zero → pro** in LLD / object-oriented design,\n> with Java code, UML & Mermaid diagrams, real interview case studies, tips & tricks, and **interview Q&A in every chapter**.\n\n---\n\n## 📱 Mobile App (Android)\n\nThese notes ship as a **fully offline Android app** — all 32 chapters, rendered Mermaid class/sequence/state diagrams (pinch-zoom), search, dark mode, and reading progress. No permissions, no network.\n\n**➡️ Download the APK from the [Releases page](../../../releases/latest)** (tag `lld-v*`) and install it on any Android 8.0+ phone.\n\nThe app lives in [`app/`](app) (WebView reader) and is built + published automatically by [GitHub Actions](../.github/workflows/lld-release.yml) on every `lld-v*` tag.\n\n---\n\n## 📖 How to use these notes\n\n1. **Read in order** — chapters build on each other (foundations → patterns → concurrency → machine coding → case studies).\n2. **Every chapter ends with** ✅ *Key Takeaways / Outcome*, 💡 *Tips & Tricks*, and 🎯 *Interview Q&A* — revise those before interviews.\n3. **Case studies include \"say this\" scripts** — the exact narration that scores in interviews, not just the design.\n4. **Diagrams are Mermaid** — class, sequence, and state diagrams render directly on GitHub and in the app.\n5. **Code is Java** — runnable, minimal, interview-calibrated.\n6. Finish with the **Interview Prep** section: the 45-minute framework, the cheat sheet, and the 60-question rapid-fire bank.\n\n---\n\n## 🗺️ Curriculum\n\n### Part 1 — OOP Foundations (`01-foundations/`)\n| # | Chapter | What you'll master |\n|---|---------|--------------------|\n| 1.1 | [OOP in Java](01-foundations/01-oop-in-java.md) | The four pillars as habits, composition over inheritance, equals/hashCode, immutability, records |\n| 1.2 | [SOLID Principles](01-foundations/02-solid-principles.md) | Each principle as smell → refactor, with Java code and the cheat table |\n| 1.3 | [Beyond SOLID](01-foundations/03-design-principles.md) | DRY, KISS, YAGNI, Law of Demeter, cohesion & coupling |\n| 1.4 | [UML for Interviews](01-foundations/04-uml-for-interviews.md) | Class boxes, the 5 arrows, sequence diagrams, diagram-first workflow |\n\n### Part 2 — Design Patterns (`02-design-patterns/`)\n| # | Chapter | What you'll master |\n|---|---------|--------------------|\n| 2.1 | [Creational](02-design-patterns/01-creational-patterns.md) | Singleton (done right), Factory, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype |\n| 2.2 | [Structural](02-design-patterns/02-structural-patterns.md) | Adapter, Decorator, Facade, Composite, Proxy, Flyweight, Bridge |\n| 2.3 | [Behavioral I](02-design-patterns/03-behavioral-patterns-1.md) | Strategy, Observer, Command, State — the interview big four |\n| 2.4 | [Behavioral II](02-design-patterns/04-behavioral-patterns-2.md) | Template Method, Chain of Responsibility, Iterator, Mediator, Memento, Visitor |\n| 2.5 | [Choosing Patterns](02-design-patterns/05-choosing-patterns.md) | The problem → pattern map, winning combos, the anti-checklist |\n\n### Part 3 — Concurrency (`03-concurrency/`)\n| # | Chapter | What you'll master |\n|---|---------|--------------------|\n| 3.1 | [Java Concurrency Essentials](03-concurrency/01-java-concurrency-essentials.md) | synchronized, volatile, atomics/CAS, locks, executors, deadlock |\n| 3.2 | [Concurrent Collections](03-concurrency/02-concurrent-collections.md) | ConcurrentHashMap, BlockingQueue, semaphores, per-key locking |\n| 3.3 | [Thread-Safe LLD](03-concurrency/03-thread-safe-lld-patterns.md) | The 5 moves: immutability, per-entity locks, CAS, confinement, expiry |\n\n### Part 4 — Machine Coding (`04-machine-coding/`)\n| # | Chapter | What you'll master |\n|---|---------|--------------------|\n| 4.1 | [The Playbook](04-machine-coding/01-machine-coding-playbook.md) | The 90-minute budget, build order, demo runner, walkthrough |\n| 4.2 | [Clean Code for LLD](04-machine-coding/02-clean-code-for-lld.md) | Naming, small methods, exceptions, Optional, API shape |\n| 4.3 | [Object Modeling](04-machine-coding/03-object-modeling.md) | Entities vs VOs, aggregates, repositories, state machines, typed IDs |\n\n### Part 5 — Case Studies: Real Interview Problems (`05-case-studies/`)\n| # | Design… | Core concepts exercised |\n|---|---------|-------------------------|\n| 5.1 | [Parking Lot](05-case-studies/01-parking-lot.md) | The template problem: strategies, per-spot locking, factory, extensions |\n| 5.2 | [Elevator System](05-case-studies/02-elevator-system.md) | LOOK scheduling, State, Strategy, confinement concurrency |\n| 5.3 | [Vending Machine](05-case-studies/03-vending-machine.md) | State pattern, change-making, money safety |\n| 5.4 | [LRU Cache](05-case-studies/04-lru-cache.md) | HashMap+DLL, eviction Strategy, TTL, thread-safety ladder |\n| 5.5 | [Tic-Tac-Toe & Snake-Ladder](05-case-studies/05-board-games.md) | Clean fundamentals, turn management, injected dice, win detection |\n| 5.6 | [Chess](05-case-studies/06-chess.md) | Piece polymorphism, layered legality, simulate-undo, scoping |\n| 5.7 | [Splitwise](05-case-studies/07-splitwise.md) | Split strategies, exact money math, balance sheets, debt simplification |\n| 5.8 | [Movie Ticket Booking](05-case-studies/08-movie-ticket-booking.md) | HOLD→PAY→CONFIRM, seat races, TTL holds, idempotent payments |\n| 5.9 | [Ride Hailing](05-case-studies/09-ride-hailing.md) | Trip state machine, CAS driver matching, pricing decorators |\n| 5.10 | [Logging Framework](05-case-studies/10-logging-framework.md) | Appenders, async queues, overflow policy, never-throw discipline |\n| 5.11 | [Rate Limiter](05-case-studies/11-rate-limiter.md) | Token bucket + 3 rivals, atomic check-consume, distributed story |\n| 5.12 | [Pub-Sub Queue](05-case-studies/12-pub-sub-queue.md) | Log+offsets, consumer groups, delivery semantics, DLQ |\n| 5.13 | [In-Memory File System](05-case-studies/13-in-memory-file-system.md) | Composite, path resolution, recursive ops, symlink cycles |\n\n### Part 6 — Interview Prep (`06-interview-prep/`)\n| # | Chapter | What you get |\n|---|---------|--------------|\n| 6.1 | [The Interview Framework](06-interview-prep/01-interview-framework.md) | The 45-minute script, phrase bank, seniority calibration, failure modes |\n| 6.2 | [The Cheat Sheet](06-interview-prep/02-pattern-problem-cheatsheet.md) | Problems × patterns × traps table, universal seams, memory snippets |\n| 6.3 | [Rapid-Fire Q&A Bank](06-interview-prep/03-qa-bank.md) | 60 questions with crisp 15-second answers |\n| 6.4 | [Tips & Red Flags](06-interview-prep/04-tips-and-red-flags.md) | The 10 behaviors, the red-flag list, 4-week plan, night-before checklist |\n\n---\n\n## 🧭 Suggested study plan (4 weeks)\n\n```\nWeek 1  → Part 1 + Part 4 (foundations & modeling — the grading rubric)\nWeek 2  → Part 2 (patterns) + reproduce the Ch 2.5 map from memory\nWeek 3  → Part 3 (concurrency) + case studies 5.1–5.5\nWeek 4  → Case studies 5.6–5.13 + all of Part 6 + one recorded 45-min mock\n```\n\n**Golden rule:** after each chapter, close the notes and *design it aloud from scratch* (or code the kata). If you can't narrate it, re-read. LLD is learned by *designing*, not by reading.\n\n---\n\n## 🔗 Companion\n\nPairs with the [System Design Mastery (HLD)](https://github.com/maheshsreerama/System-Design) notes — same style, same app, one level up the stack. Ch 6.2 Q5 maps exactly which LLD ideas reappear in HLD interviews.\n"}, {"id": "01-foundations/01-oop-in-java", "path": "01-foundations/01-oop-in-java.md", "part": "Part 1 · OOP Foundations", "title": "1.1 OOP in Java — The Four Pillars, Done Right", "md": "# 1.1 OOP in Java — The Four Pillars, Done Right\n\n> Every LLD interview is secretly an OOP exam. Interviewers rarely ask \"what is encapsulation?\" — they watch whether your *code* encapsulates. This chapter turns the pillars from definitions into habits.\n\n---\n\n## The four pillars — as behaviors, not definitions\n\n| Pillar | Definition (1 line for the interview) | What it looks like in your code |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Encapsulation** | Data + behavior together; internals hidden behind a small API | `private` fields, no setters unless needed, invariants enforced in methods |\n| **Abstraction** | Expose *what*, hide *how* | Interfaces (`PaymentStrategy`), callers never see implementation details |\n| **Inheritance** | *is-a* reuse of interface + implementation | `abstract class Vehicle` → `Car extends Vehicle` — use sparingly |\n| **Polymorphism** | One call site, many behaviors at runtime | `fee.calculate(ticket)` dispatches to hourly/flat/weekend strategy |\n\n**The interview one-liner:** \"I encapsulate state, expose behavior through interfaces, and use polymorphism instead of `if/else` chains. I inherit only for true is-a relationships — otherwise I compose.\"\n\n---\n\n## Encapsulation — protect your invariants\n\nBad (anemic — data class + logic elsewhere):\n\n```java\nclass Wallet { public double balance; }          // anyone can corrupt it\n// elsewhere: wallet.balance -= amount;          // no validation, duplicated everywhere\n```\n\nGood (invariant lives *inside* the object):\n\n```java\npublic class Wallet {\n    private long balanceInPaise;                  // money as long, never double!\n\n    public void debit(long amount) {\n        if (amount <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"amount must be positive\");\n        if (amount > balanceInPaise) throw new InsufficientBalanceException(amount, balanceInPaise);\n        balanceInPaise -= amount;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Rules that impress:**\n- No setters by default. Ask: \"does anyone *need* to mutate this?\"\n- Never return mutable internals: return `List.copyOf(items)` or an unmodifiable view.\n- Money = `long` minor-units or `BigDecimal`. Saying \"never double for money\" earns instant credibility.\n\n## Abstraction — program to interfaces\n\n```java\npublic interface ParkingSpotAssignmentStrategy {\n    Optional<ParkingSpot> findSpot(ParkingLot lot, Vehicle vehicle);\n}\n// callers depend on the interface; NearestSpotStrategy / RandomStrategy are swappable\n```\n\n**When interface vs abstract class?**\n- **Interface**: pure capability/contract, multiple implementations expected, no shared state. Default choice.\n- **Abstract class**: shared *state* or a skeletal implementation (Template Method, Ch 2.4). A class can extend only one — spend that budget wisely.\n\n## Inheritance — the sharpest knife, use least\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Vehicle {\n        <<abstract>>\n        -String licensePlate\n        -VehicleType type\n        +getType() VehicleType\n    }\n    Vehicle <|-- Car\n    Vehicle <|-- Bike\n    Vehicle <|-- Truck\n```\n\nInheritance is fine for closed, stable hierarchies (vehicle types, chess pieces). It breaks when:\n- The hierarchy grows on two axes → class explosion (`ElectricSportsCar`, `PetrolSportsCar`…) → use **composition/Bridge/Strategy**.\n- You inherit to *reuse code* rather than to *be* the parent → violates LSP (Ch 1.2).\n\n**Litmus test to say aloud:** \"Is a `Car` always a `Vehicle` in every context this code runs? Yes → inherit. Am I just borrowing methods? → compose.\"\n\n## Composition over inheritance — the default\n\n```java\n// A vehicle HAS an engine; engines vary independently of vehicle kinds.\npublic class Car {\n    private final Engine engine;        // composition: swap Electric/Petrol freely\n    public Car(Engine engine) { this.engine = engine; }\n    public void start() { engine.ignite(); }\n}\n```\n\nComposition wins because: no fragile base class, testable via mocks, behavior swappable at runtime, and hierarchies stay one level deep.\n\n## Polymorphism — kill the if/else chain\n\n```java\n// ❌ what interviewers penalize                     // ✅ what they reward\ndouble fee(Ticket t) {                               interface FeeStrategy { long fee(Ticket t); }\n    if (t.type == CAR) ...                           Map<VehicleType, FeeStrategy> strategies;\n    else if (t.type == BIKE) ...                     strategies.get(t.type()).fee(t);\n    else if (t.type == TRUCK) ...\n}\n```\n\nAdding a new vehicle type: left = edit every switch in the codebase; right = add one class + one map entry (Open/Closed, Ch 1.2).\n\n---\n\n## Java essentials interviewers probe\n\n**`equals`/`hashCode` — always together, on identity fields only:**\n\n```java\npublic final class SpotId {\n    private final int floor; private final int number;\n    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {\n        if (this == o) return true;\n        if (!(o instanceof SpotId s)) return false;\n        return floor == s.floor && number == s.number;\n    }\n    @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(floor, number); }\n}\n```\n\nBreak the contract and `HashMap`/`HashSet` silently misbehave — a classic bug they ask about.\n\n**Immutability — your default for value objects:**\n\n```java\npublic record Money(long amountPaise, Currency currency) {   // records: free equals/hashCode/toString\n    public Money add(Money other) {\n        if (!currency.equals(other.currency)) throw new CurrencyMismatchException();\n        return new Money(amountPaise + other.amountPaise, currency);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nImmutable objects are thread-safe for free, cacheable, and safe as map keys. Use `record` for value objects (Java 16+), `enum` for fixed sets (`VehicleType`, `OrderStatus`).\n\n**`enum` with behavior — a mini pattern:**\n\n```java\npublic enum VehicleType {\n    BIKE(1), CAR(1), TRUCK(2);                 // spots each occupies\n    private final int spotsNeeded;\n    VehicleType(int n) { this.spotsNeeded = n; }\n    public int spotsNeeded() { return spotsNeeded; }\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Encapsulation = invariants enforced inside the object; no naked fields, no reflex setters.\n- Program to interfaces; abstract classes only for shared state/skeletons.\n- Inheritance for true is-a in stable hierarchies; **composition is the default**.\n- Polymorphism (strategy map) replaces `if/else`-on-type everywhere.\n- Value objects: immutable `record`s with proper `equals/hashCode`; money is never `double`.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- In machine coding, start every entity `private final` and relax only when forced — reviewers notice.\n- Narrate the litmus tests aloud (\"this is has-a, so I'll compose\") — the interviewer is grading your *reasoning*, not just the result.\n- If you write a `switch` on a type enum, immediately say \"I'd refactor this to Strategy if more variants come\" — it defuses the follow-up.\n- Records + enums + `Optional` return types make your interview code look senior with zero extra effort.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Composition vs inheritance — when do you choose which?**\n> Inheritance only for a true, permanent is-a relationship in a closed hierarchy where I want polymorphic dispatch (Chess pieces, vehicle categories). Composition everywhere else: it avoids the fragile-base-class problem, lets behavior vary at runtime, keeps testing easy, and survives requirement changes on multiple axes. Practical signal: if I'm inheriting *to reuse code* rather than to *be substitutable* for the parent, that's composition's job.\n\n**Q2. Interface vs abstract class in Java — real differences?**\n> Interface: contract only, multiple inheritance of type, fields are implicitly constants, since Java 8 can carry default methods. Abstract class: can hold state, constructors, and non-public members; single inheritance. Design answer: interfaces define *capability* (Comparable, PaymentStrategy); abstract classes provide a *partial implementation* for a family (Template Method). Default to interfaces — you can always add a skeletal abstract class implementing the interface later.\n\n**Q3. Why must equals and hashCode be overridden together?**\n> The contract: equal objects must have equal hash codes. Hash collections first bucket by hashCode, then confirm with equals. Override only equals → two \"equal\" objects land in different buckets → `set.contains()` returns false, duplicate map keys. Override only hashCode → lookups find the bucket but equals rejects the match. Either way, silent data corruption.\n\n**Q4. What makes a class immutable, and why care in LLD?**\n> final class (or no subclassing of mutators), all fields private final, no setters, defensive copies of mutable inputs/outputs, `this` not leaked from the constructor. Benefits: thread-safe with zero locking, safe as `HashMap` keys, freely shareable/cacheable, and reasoning stays local. In interviews, immutable value objects (Money, SpotId, Position) eliminate whole classes of concurrency questions before they're asked.\n\n**Q5. Is Java pass-by-value or pass-by-reference?**\n> Strictly pass-by-value — but for objects, the value passed is the reference. So a method can mutate the object it receives, but reassigning the parameter never affects the caller. This is why defensive copies matter for encapsulation: handing out your internal `List` reference hands out write access.\n"}, {"id": "01-foundations/02-solid-principles", "path": "01-foundations/02-solid-principles.md", "part": "Part 1 · OOP Foundations", "title": "1.2 SOLID Principles — With Java Refactors", "md": "# 1.2 SOLID Principles — With Java Refactors\n\n> SOLID is the grading rubric of LLD interviews. You won't be asked to recite it — but every design choice you make will be silently scored against it. Learn each principle as **smell → refactor**.\n\n---\n\n## S — Single Responsibility Principle (SRP)\n\n> A class should have **one reason to change** — one actor whose requirements affect it.\n\n**Smell:** a `ParkingLotService` that finds spots *and* computes fees *and* prints receipts *and* writes to DB. Pricing team, receipt team, and DBA all force edits to one class.\n\n```java\n// ❌ God class                                  // ✅ One job each\nclass ParkingService {                           class SpotAllocator   { ... }   // assignment rules\n    park(); calcFee(); printReceipt();           class FeeCalculator   { ... }   // pricing rules\n    saveToDb();                                  class ReceiptPrinter  { ... }   // formatting\n}                                                class TicketRepository{ ... }   // persistence\n```\n\n**How to use it live:** when your class hits a second responsibility, say *\"I'll split this — fee logic changes for different reasons than allocation logic.\"* That sentence is worth more than the split itself.\n\n**Caution:** SRP ≠ one method per class. Over-splitting into 30 two-line classes is a red flag too. Group by *reason to change*.\n\n## O — Open/Closed Principle (OCP)\n\n> Open for **extension**, closed for **modification** — add features by adding code, not editing tested code.\n\n**Smell:** `switch (vehicleType)` inside fee calculation. Every new type reopens the file.\n\n```java\npublic interface FeeStrategy { long fee(Duration parked); }\n\npublic class HourlyFee implements FeeStrategy {\n    private final long ratePerHour;\n    public HourlyFee(long r) { ratePerHour = r; }\n    public long fee(Duration d) { return Math.max(1, d.toHours()) * ratePerHour; }\n}\n// New \"weekend surge pricing\"? New class + registration. Zero edits to existing code.\n```\n\nMechanisms in Java: interfaces + polymorphism (Strategy), abstract hooks (Template Method), composition (Decorator).\n\n**Caution to say aloud:** \"I apply OCP on the axis that's *likely to change* — abstracting everything upfront is speculative generality.\"\n\n## L — Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP)\n\n> Subtypes must be usable **anywhere** the base type is expected — without surprises.\n\n**The classic trap (know it cold):** `Square extends Rectangle` — `setWidth(5)` on a Square silently sets height too; code that worked on Rectangle breaks. Also: `penguin.fly()` throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`.\n\n**Rules:** subclasses may *weaken* preconditions and *strengthen* postconditions, never the reverse; never throw new exception types the base doesn't; never no-op an inherited method.\n\n```java\n// ❌ Penguin forced to lie                       // ✅ model the capability separately\nclass Bird { void fly() {...} }                  interface Flyable { void fly(); }\nclass Penguin extends Bird {                     class Sparrow extends Bird implements Flyable {...}\n    void fly() { throw new UOE(); }              class Penguin extends Bird {...}   // simply not Flyable\n}\n```\n\n**Real interview relevance:** in Chess, if `Piece.possibleMoves()` returns legal moves, *every* piece must honor that contract — no piece may return \"call a different method for me.\"\n\n## I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)\n\n> No client should be forced to depend on methods it doesn't use. **Many small interfaces > one fat one.**\n\n```java\n// ❌ fat interface                               // ✅ role interfaces\ninterface Machine {                               interface Printer { void print(Doc d); }\n    void print(Doc d);                            interface Scanner { void scan(Doc d); }\n    void scan(Doc d);                             class OldPrinter implements Printer { ... }\n    void fax(Doc d);                              class AllInOne implements Printer, Scanner { ... }\n}\n```\n\nISP is LSP's prevention: fat interfaces are *why* implementations end up throwing `UnsupportedOperationException`.\n\n## D — Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)\n\n> High-level policy shouldn't depend on low-level detail; **both depend on abstractions**.\n\n```java\n// ❌ policy welded to detail                       // ✅ inverted\nclass OrderService {                                class OrderService {\n    private final MySqlOrderDao dao =                   private final OrderRepository repo;   // interface\n        new MySqlOrderDao();                            private final NotificationSender sender;\n    private final SmtpMailer mailer =                   OrderService(OrderRepository r, NotificationSender s) {\n        new SmtpMailer();                                   this.repo = r; this.sender = s;   // injected\n}                                                   }   }\n```\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph High-level policy\n        OS[OrderService]\n    end\n    subgraph Abstractions\n        OR[«interface» OrderRepository]\n        NS[«interface» NotificationSender]\n    end\n    subgraph Low-level detail\n        MY[MySqlOrderRepository]\n        MEM[InMemoryOrderRepository]\n        SMTP[EmailSender]\n    end\n    OS --> OR\n    OS --> NS\n    MY -.implements.-> OR\n    MEM -.implements.-> OR\n    SMTP -.implements.-> NS\n```\n\n**Machine-coding payoff:** inject an `InMemoryRepository` in tests, a real one in prod — no framework needed, constructor injection is enough. *\"I'll constructor-inject the repository interface so the design is testable\"* is a top-tier sentence.\n\n---\n\n## SOLID cheat table\n\n| Principle | One-line pitch | Smell that violates it | Fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SRP | One reason to change | God class, \"and\" in class description | Split by actor/concern |\n| OCP | Extend, don't edit | `switch` on type, editing old code per feature | Strategy / polymorphism |\n| LSP | Subs behave like base | UnsupportedOperationException, no-op overrides | Rethink hierarchy; capability interfaces |\n| ISP | Small role interfaces | Clients implementing dummy methods | Split interface by client |\n| DIP | Depend on abstractions | `new MySqlDao()` inside business logic | Inject interfaces (constructor) |\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- SOLID is the *why* behind design patterns: Strategy = OCP, Template = OCP+LSP, Adapter = DIP, role interfaces = ISP.\n- Apply on demand — abstract the axes that change; don't build cathedrals of interfaces speculatively.\n- Constructor injection of interfaces gives DIP + testability with zero frameworks.\n- LSP is about **behavioral** substitutability, not just compiling — contracts, not signatures.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Narrate principles by *consequence*, not name-dropping: \"new pricing rules shouldn't touch tested code\" beats \"I'm applying OCP.\"\n- If asked \"where does your design violate SOLID?\" — have an answer ready. Knowing your trade-offs impresses more than claiming purity.\n- The `switch` you keep: a `switch` over an `enum` that's *sealed by nature* (chess piece types) is acceptable — say you chose it consciously for locality.\n- SRP question to self-check any class in 5 seconds: \"describe it without using 'and'.\"\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Isn't SOLID overkill for a 90-minute machine coding round?**\n> The principles cost nothing when applied at the right altitude: constructor injection, strategy for the one axis the problem statement says will vary, small interfaces. What I skip under time pressure: speculative abstractions for axes nobody mentioned. I'd say: design the extension point they *hinted at* (\"support new fee rules later\") and keep the rest simple.\n\n**Q2. Give a real LSP violation you've seen and the fix.**\n> `ReadOnlyList extends ArrayList` overriding `add()` to throw. Any code holding a `List` breaks at runtime. Fix: composition — wrap the list and expose only read methods (`Collections.unmodifiableList` does exactly this), or model capability with a separate `ReadableList` interface. General fix pattern: if a subclass must disable parent behavior, the hierarchy is wrong — extract the narrower contract upward.\n\n**Q3. How do OCP and Strategy pattern relate?**\n> Strategy is OCP's most common implementation: variability is captured behind an interface, each variant is a class, and the consuming code is closed to modification. Adding a variant = adding a class + registering it (map, factory, or DI config). The `switch` statement is the \"modification\" that Strategy deletes.\n\n**Q4. DIP vs dependency injection vs IoC container — untangle them.**\n> DIP is the *principle*: depend on abstractions. Dependency injection is a *technique* for satisfying it: dependencies are handed in (constructor/setter) instead of constructed inside. An IoC container (Spring) is a *tool* that automates injection. You can honor DIP with plain `new` at the composition root and no framework — which is exactly what I do in machine coding rounds: a `main()` that wires implementations into constructors.\n\n**Q5. When would you deliberately break SRP?**\n> When cohesion beats separation: a small immutable value object computing its own derived values (Money.add), simple CRUD where splitting creates ceremonial layers, or a prototype where requirements are still moving. The senior answer: SRP violations are debt — acceptable if taken knowingly, logged aloud (\"I'm keeping fee calc inline for now; first thing I'd extract\"), and cheap to pay back because the code is small.\n"}, {"id": "01-foundations/03-design-principles", "path": "01-foundations/03-design-principles.md", "part": "Part 1 · OOP Foundations", "title": "1.3 Beyond SOLID — DRY, KISS, YAGNI, Law of Demeter & Cohesion", "md": "# 1.3 Beyond SOLID — DRY, KISS, YAGNI, Law of Demeter & Cohesion\n\n> SOLID gets the headlines, but interviews are often decided by these quieter principles — especially **KISS and YAGNI**, because over-engineering is the #1 reason strong coders fail LLD rounds.\n\n---\n\n## DRY — Don't Repeat Yourself (but don't over-DRY)\n\nEvery piece of **knowledge** should have one authoritative home. Note: *knowledge*, not *text*.\n\n```java\n// ❌ the rule \"15 min grace period\" lives in 3 places\nif (minutes <= 15) return 0;         // in FeeCalculator\nif (parked < 15) waiveFee();         // in ExitGate\nGRACE = 15;                          // in a random constant file\n\n// ✅ one home\npublic class GracePeriodPolicy {\n    private static final Duration GRACE = Duration.ofMinutes(15);\n    public boolean isWithinGrace(Duration parked) { return parked.compareTo(GRACE) <= 0; }\n}\n```\n\n**The senior nuance — accidental duplication:** two code blocks that *look* identical but represent different business rules (e.g., \"max 4 passengers per cab\" vs \"max 4 items per order\") must **stay separate** — merging them couples unrelated concepts that will diverge. Say: *\"same shape, different knowledge — I won't unify these.\"*\n\n## KISS — Keep It Simple\n\nThe simplest design that satisfies stated requirements wins. Complexity must be *pulled in* by requirements, never *pushed in* by cleverness.\n\n| Over-engineered (red flag) | Simple (hired) |\n|---|---|\n| `AbstractVehicleFactoryProvider` for 3 vehicle types | `enum VehicleType` + one factory method |\n| Event bus inside a single-process Tic-Tac-Toe | Direct method call `board.place(move)` |\n| Custom thread pool for a single-user CLI app | Single-threaded; note where locks would go |\n| 6 layers (controller/service/manager/helper/impl/dao) | Entity + service + repository |\n\n**Interview script:** *\"I'll start with the simplest model that meets the requirements and flag the extension points; tell me if you want me to build any of them out.\"* This sentence prevents both over- and under-engineering.\n\n## YAGNI — You Aren't Gonna Need It\n\nDon't build for imagined futures. But — critical for interviews — **YAGNI applies to code, not to design seams.**\n\n- ❌ Building multi-floor, multi-gate, valet parking when asked for a single-lot MVP.\n- ✅ Putting fee calculation behind an interface (1 minute of work) because pricing *always* changes.\n\nRule of thumb: **abstractions are cheap, implementations are expensive.** Add the seam, skip the speculative implementation.\n\n## Law of Demeter — talk to friends, not strangers\n\nA method should only call methods on: its own class, its fields, its parameters, objects it creates. **One dot of behavior, not a train of gets.**\n\n```java\n// ❌ train wreck — knows Wallet internals 3 levels deep\norder.getCustomer().getWallet().getBalance().isGreaterThan(total);\n\n// ✅ tell, don't ask\ncustomer.canPay(total);          // Customer internally consults its wallet\n```\n\nWhy it matters: the train wreck couples `Order` to the *shape* of Customer→Wallet→Balance; any refactor breaks it. \"Tell, don't ask\" also moves logic next to the data it uses (encapsulation, SRP).\n\n**Exception:** fluent builders and streams (`list.stream().filter().map()`) are fine — each call returns the same conceptual object.\n\n## Cohesion & Coupling — the two dials\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    A[High cohesion<br/>class does one thing,<br/>all parts used together] -->|aim here| GOOD((⭐ Good design))\n    B[Low coupling<br/>few, narrow, abstract<br/>dependencies between classes] -->|aim here| GOOD\n    C[Low cohesion<br/>Utils / Manager / Helper<br/>grab-bags] -->|refactor| A\n    D[High coupling<br/>concrete refs, deep call chains,<br/>shared mutable state] -->|refactor| B\n```\n\n- **Cohesion** (within a class): do its fields and methods form one concept? A `Utils` class is zero cohesion.\n- **Coupling** (between classes): how many things break if this class changes? Reduce with interfaces (DIP), events (Observer), and Demeter.\n\nAlmost every principle in this part is a strategy for **high cohesion + low coupling** — say this and you've shown the map, not just the streets.\n\n## Supporting cast (know in one line each)\n\n- **Principle of Least Astonishment**: `getX()` never mutates; `add()` never removes. Name = contract.\n- **Fail fast**: validate at the boundary, throw early with context — don't let bad state travel.\n- **Separation of concerns**: domain logic never knows about I/O, formatting, or persistence details.\n- **Encapsulate what varies** (GoF's meta-principle): find the axis of change, put an interface on it — this single sentence generates half the patterns in Part 2.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- DRY is about knowledge, not text; keep look-alike code separate when it encodes different rules.\n- KISS/YAGNI: complexity must be justified by a requirement; seams are cheap, speculative features are not.\n- Law of Demeter: tell, don't ask; one dot of behavior; no get-chains into other objects' guts.\n- Every principle serves the same goal: **high cohesion, low coupling**.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Over-engineering fails more candidates than under-engineering. When unsure, build simple + *narrate* the extension: \"if we later need X, this interface is where it plugs in.\"\n- Spot your own train wrecks while coding and fix them live — interviewers love visible self-correction.\n- Never name a class `Manager`, `Helper`, `Util`, or `Processor` in an interview without a defense ready — they're cohesion smells.\n- \"Encapsulate what varies\" is the answer skeleton for *any* \"how would you design X to support Y later?\" follow-up.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Your interviewer says \"just hardcode it, it's an interview.\" Do you?**\n> For genuinely fixed facts, yes — a chess board is 8×8, hardcode it. For stated variability (fee rules, vehicle types), I put in the interface but implement only one variant. The skill being tested is *calibration*: hardcode invariants, seam the variants, and say which is which.\n\n**Q2. How is the Law of Demeter different from just \"encapsulation\"?**\n> Encapsulation hides *my* internals; Demeter stops me from exploiting *others'* internals. A getter-chain respects encapsulation technically (all getters are public) but still couples me to the object graph's shape. Demeter pushes behavior to where the data lives: instead of pulling wallet data out of Customer to make a decision, I ask Customer to make the decision.\n\n**Q3. When is duplication better than abstraction?**\n> When the two copies represent different business knowledge that will evolve independently, when the abstraction would need flags/conditionals to serve both callers (a config-flag abstraction is worse than two honest copies), or when it's too early to see the right shape — wrong abstractions cost more than duplication (\"prefer duplication over the wrong abstraction\"). I deduplicate on the third occurrence, when the pattern is proven.\n\n**Q4. What does \"tell, don't ask\" change in a Splitwise design?**\n> Ask-style: service pulls `group.getMembers()`, `member.getBalances()`, loops and mutates balances externally — logic smeared across the service, Group is a dumb bag. Tell-style: `group.addExpense(expense)` and the Group updates its own balance sheet, enforcing invariants (split sums to total) internally. Result: invariants have one enforcement point, and the service layer shrinks to orchestration.\n\n**Q5. How do you keep cohesion high as requirements grow mid-interview?**\n> Each new requirement gets routed to the class that owns that knowledge — or triggers a split. \"Add discount coupons\" lands in the pricing area, not in `Order`; if `FeeCalculator` starts holding both rates and coupons, I split `CouponPolicy` out. The habit: after every added feature, re-ask \"can I still describe each class without 'and'?\" — the moment I can't, refactor before adding more.\n"}, {"id": "01-foundations/04-uml-for-interviews", "path": "01-foundations/04-uml-for-interviews.md", "part": "Part 1 · OOP Foundations", "title": "1.4 UML for Interviews — Class & Sequence Diagrams That Communicate", "md": "# 1.4 UML for Interviews — Class & Sequence Diagrams That Communicate\n\n> You need ~20% of UML to ace 100% of interviews: class boxes, 5 relationship arrows, and a basic sequence diagram. The goal is *communication speed*, not standards compliance.\n\n---\n\n## The class box\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class ParkingLot {\n        -String id\n        -List~Floor~ floors\n        -SpotAssignmentStrategy strategy\n        +parkVehicle(Vehicle v) Ticket\n        +unpark(Ticket t) Receipt\n    }\n```\n\nConvention: `-` private, `+` public, `#` protected; fields on top, methods below. In an interview, **only show the members that matter for the discussion** — a 20-field box is noise.\n\n## The five relationships that matter\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Vehicle { <<abstract>> }\n    Vehicle <|-- Car : inheritance\n    class Flyable { <<interface>> }\n    Flyable <|.. Drone : implements\n    ParkingLot \"1\" *-- \"many\" Floor : composition\n    Car \"1\" o-- \"1\" Engine : aggregation\n    ExitGate ..> FeeStrategy : dependency\n```\n\n| Arrow | Name | Meaning | Say it as |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `<|--` | Inheritance | is-a (class) | \"Car **is a** Vehicle\" |\n| `<|..` | Realization | implements interface | \"Drone **implements** Flyable\" |\n| `*--` | Composition | owns; part dies with whole | \"Floors **can't exist without** the lot\" |\n| `o--` | Aggregation | has; part outlives whole | \"Engine **can be reused** elsewhere\" |\n| `..>` | Dependency | uses temporarily (param/local) | \"ExitGate **uses** a FeeStrategy\" |\n\n**Honest truth to say if asked:** composition vs aggregation rarely changes code — both are fields. What matters is *ownership & lifecycle*: who creates it, who cleans up, can it be shared? Plain association (`-->`) is fine when you don't want to commit.\n\n**Multiplicities:** `\"1\" -- \"0..*\"` etc. Use them where they carry a rule (\"a Spot holds at most one Vehicle\") and skip elsewhere.\n\n## Sequence diagrams — for the \"walk me through a flow\" moment\n\nThe highest-leverage diagram in LLD interviews. When asked \"what happens when a car enters?\", draw this instead of talking:\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    actor D as Driver\n    participant G as EntryGate\n    participant L as ParkingLot\n    participant S as SpotAssignmentStrategy\n    participant T as TicketRepository\n    D->>G: arrive(vehicle)\n    G->>L: parkVehicle(vehicle)\n    L->>S: findSpot(vehicle)\n    S-->>L: Optional~Spot~\n    alt spot available\n        L->>L: spot.assign(vehicle)\n        L->>T: save(ticket)\n        L-->>G: Ticket\n    else lot full\n        L-->>G: throw LotFullException\n    end\n```\n\nReading it aloud *is* your design narration: every arrow is a method call you're committing to. If an arrow feels awkward to name, the design is wrong — the diagram just caught a bug for free.\n\n## Interview workflow — diagram before code\n\n1. **Nouns → candidate classes** from the problem statement (parking lot, floor, spot, ticket, gate, vehicle).\n2. **Verbs → methods/relationships** (park, unpark, calculate fee, assign).\n3. Draw boxes + arrows, **talk while drawing** — get the interviewer nodding *before* writing Java.\n4. Only then code — the diagram becomes your to-do list of classes.\n\nThis ordering is the single biggest process difference between candidates who pass and fail: code-first candidates get lost in refactors; diagram-first candidates walk a straight line.\n\n## Common notation in these notes (and how to sketch fast on a whiteboard)\n\n- Interface: box with `<<interface>>`; abstract: italics or `<<abstract>>`.\n- Enum: box with `<<enumeration>>` listing values — great for states (`TicketStatus: ACTIVE, PAID, LOST`).\n- On a physical whiteboard: solid arrow for is-a, diamond for has-a, dashed for uses. Nobody will grade arrowhead shapes — they grade whether relationships are *correct*.\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class TicketStatus {\n        <<enumeration>>\n        ACTIVE\n        PAID\n        LOST\n    }\n    class Ticket {\n        -String id\n        -Instant entryTime\n        -TicketStatus status\n    }\n    Ticket --> TicketStatus\n```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Master exactly: class box, inheritance, realization, composition/aggregation, dependency, multiplicity — plus sequence diagrams.\n- Diagram-first, code-second: nouns → classes, verbs → methods, then walk one end-to-end flow as a sequence.\n- Show only decision-relevant members; a diagram is an argument, not an inventory.\n- If a sequence arrow is hard to name, you found a design flaw early — cheapest possible fix.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Start every case study by drawing the class diagram skeleton from memory — Part 5 chapters all include one to internalize.\n- When the interviewer questions a relationship, annotate the diagram rather than defending verbally — \"you're right, Ticket shouldn't own Payment; let me flip that arrow.\"\n- Keep one flow diagrammed end-to-end rather than five flows half-done.\n- Say multiplicities as business rules: \"one spot, at most one vehicle — I'll enforce that in `Spot.assign()`.\"\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Composition vs aggregation — does the distinction actually matter?**\n> Conceptually: composition = exclusive ownership + shared lifecycle (Floor dies with ParkingLot); aggregation = has-a with independent lifecycle (Driver has a Vehicle; the vehicle outlives the trip). In Java both are just fields, so what matters is what the distinction *implies*: who constructs the part, whether it can be shared across owners, and who's responsible for cleanup. I mention it when lifecycle is a real design question and don't split hairs otherwise.\n\n**Q2. The interviewer asks for a class diagram of your half-coded design. What do you include?**\n> The 5–8 classes that carry the design's ideas: the aggregate root, the interfaces (strategies/repositories), the entities with state machines, and the relationships with multiplicities that encode business rules. I omit getters, DTOs, and exceptions. Then I offer one sequence diagram of the core flow — that pair answers 90% of follow-ups before they're asked.\n\n**Q3. When do you reach for a sequence diagram over a class diagram?**\n> Class diagram = static structure: what exists and how it relates — I draw it once, upfront. Sequence diagram = dynamic behavior: who calls whom in what order for one scenario — I draw it per interesting flow (\"book a seat\", \"car exits and pays\"), especially when the flow crosses many objects or has branches (payment fails, lot full). If the interviewer asks \"then what happens?\", that's my cue to switch to sequence.\n\n**Q4. How do you show a design pattern in UML quickly?**\n> By its silhouette: Strategy = context box → interface → 2+ implementations (realization arrows); Observer = subject with a `*` association to a listener interface; Decorator = class that both implements and wraps the same interface (an arrow to itself via the interface); Factory = creator with a dashed \"creates\" dependency to products. Naming the silhouette while drawing (\"this triangle-of-three is Strategy\") transfers the idea in seconds.\n\n**Q5. Where should associations be navigable — both ways?**\n> Default to one direction: `Order → Customer` (order knows its customer) without `Customer → List<Order>` unless a use case needs it — bidirectional links double the coupling and create consistency burdens (both sides must stay in sync). When I do need reverse lookup, I often prefer a repository query (`orderRepository.findByCustomer(id)`) over an in-memory back-pointer, and say so.\n"}, {"id": "02-design-patterns/01-creational-patterns", "path": "02-design-patterns/01-creational-patterns.md", "part": "Part 2 · Design Patterns", "title": "2.1 Creational Patterns — Singleton, Factory, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype", "md": "# 2.1 Creational Patterns — Singleton, Factory, Abstract Factory, Builder, Prototype\n\n> Creational patterns answer one question: **who calls `new`, and how do we stop that decision from leaking everywhere?** In interviews you'll actually use three: Factory Method, Builder, and (carefully) Singleton.\n\n---\n\n## Singleton — one instance, globally reachable\n\n**Use in interviews:** `ParkingLot`, `VendingMachine`, ID generators, config. **But** always add: *\"in production I'd prefer a DI container managing one instance — Singleton hardcodes a global and hurts testability.\"* That caveat is half the marks.\n\n```java\n// The recommended version: enum — serialization-safe, reflection-safe, thread-safe by JVM guarantee\npublic enum IdGenerator {\n    INSTANCE;\n    private final AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong();\n    public long nextId() { return counter.incrementAndGet(); }\n}\n\n// The version they ask you to write: lazy + thread-safe (double-checked locking)\npublic class ParkingLot {\n    private static volatile ParkingLot instance;          // volatile is NOT optional\n    private ParkingLot() {}\n    public static ParkingLot getInstance() {\n        if (instance == null) {                           // fast path: no lock\n            synchronized (ParkingLot.class) {\n                if (instance == null) instance = new ParkingLot();  // second check inside lock\n            }\n        }\n        return instance;\n    }\n}\n\n// The clever version: holder idiom — lazy, thread-safe, no locks (JVM class-init guarantees)\npublic class Config {\n    private Config() {}\n    private static class Holder { static final Config INSTANCE = new Config(); }\n    public static Config getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }\n}\n```\n\n**Guaranteed follow-ups:** Why `volatile`? (Prevents reordered publication — another thread could see a non-null but half-constructed object.) Why check twice? (First check skips locking after init; second closes the race between two threads passing the first check.)\n\n## Factory Method — centralize the `new`\n\n**Problem:** `new Car()` / `new Bike()` scattered through the code = shotgun surgery when construction changes.\n\n```java\npublic class VehicleFactory {\n    public static Vehicle create(VehicleType type, String plate) {\n        return switch (type) {\n            case CAR   -> new Car(plate);\n            case BIKE  -> new Bike(plate);\n            case TRUCK -> new Truck(plate);\n        };  // exhaustive over enum — compiler forces update on new type\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe one `switch` your design is allowed: creation knowledge lives in exactly one place. Everything downstream works with the `Vehicle` abstraction (OCP everywhere else).\n\n**Classic (GoF) form** — subclasses decide the product; useful with Template Method:\n\n```java\nabstract class NotificationService {\n    protected abstract Notifier createNotifier();          // factory method — the hook\n    public void notify(User u, String msg) { createNotifier().send(u, msg); }  // template\n}\nclass EmailNotificationService extends NotificationService {\n    protected Notifier createNotifier() { return new EmailNotifier(); }\n}\n```\n\n## Abstract Factory — families of related products\n\nUse when products must be **consistent with each other** (all-dark widgets, all-Mac widgets).\n\n```java\npublic interface UIFactory {           // one factory = one consistent family\n    Button createButton();\n    Checkbox createCheckbox();\n}\npublic class DarkThemeFactory implements UIFactory {\n    public Button createButton() { return new DarkButton(); }\n    public Checkbox createCheckbox() { return new DarkCheckbox(); }\n}\n// client code is theme-blind:\nUIFactory f = themeIsDark ? new DarkThemeFactory() : new LightThemeFactory();\n```\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class UIFactory { <<interface>>\n        +createButton() Button\n        +createCheckbox() Checkbox\n    }\n    UIFactory <|.. DarkThemeFactory\n    UIFactory <|.. LightThemeFactory\n    DarkThemeFactory ..> DarkButton : creates\n    LightThemeFactory ..> LightButton : creates\n```\n\n**Interview line:** \"Factory Method creates *one* product; Abstract Factory creates a *matched family*.\"\n\n## Builder — many parameters, some optional, immutable result\n\n**Problem:** telescoping constructors (`new Pizza(true, false, true, null, 3, ...)`) — unreadable, error-prone.\n\n```java\npublic class Order {\n    private final String id;                 // required\n    private final List<Item> items;          // required\n    private final Address address;           // optional\n    private final Coupon coupon;             // optional\n\n    private Order(Builder b) {               // private: Builder is the only door\n        this.id = b.id; this.items = List.copyOf(b.items);\n        this.address = b.address; this.coupon = b.coupon;\n    }\n\n    public static Builder builder(String id) { return new Builder(id); }\n\n    public static class Builder {\n        private final String id;\n        private final List<Item> items = new ArrayList<>();\n        private Address address; private Coupon coupon;\n\n        Builder(String id) { this.id = Objects.requireNonNull(id); }\n        public Builder addItem(Item i) { items.add(i); return this; }\n        public Builder shipTo(Address a) { address = a; return this; }\n        public Builder withCoupon(Coupon c) { coupon = c; return this; }\n        public Order build() {\n            if (items.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException(\"order needs items\");\n            return new Order(this);           // validation in ONE place, result immutable\n        }\n    }\n}\n// Order o = Order.builder(\"o-1\").addItem(pizza).shipTo(home).build();\n```\n\nThree wins to narrate: readable named steps, **cross-field validation at `build()`**, immutable product. Use for: `Order`, `User`, `HttpRequest`, `Pizza`, any 4+ param object.\n\n## Prototype — clone instead of construct\n\nRare in interviews; know the idea: when construction is expensive or you need many near-copies, copy an existing instance (`copy()` method returning a deep copy). Java tip: prefer **copy constructors** over `Cloneable` (broken by design — say why: no constructor call, shallow by default, checked exception).\n\n```java\npublic GameState copy() {                    // deep copy for game-tree exploration / undo\n    return new GameState(board.copy(), new ArrayList<>(moves), currentPlayer);\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Choosing quickly\n\n| Situation in the problem | Pattern |\n|---|---|\n| \"There must be exactly one lot / machine / logger\" | Singleton (+ DI caveat) |\n| Creating variants of one abstraction from a type/enum | Factory Method |\n| Consistent families (themes, cloud providers, DB dialects) | Abstract Factory |\n| 4+ constructor params, optionals, want immutability | Builder |\n| Undo/redo, snapshots, expensive construction | Prototype (copy constructor) |\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Factory = the one allowed `switch`; everything after construction is abstraction-only.\n- Builder = readability + one validation point + immutability; know the static-inner-class form cold.\n- Singleton: enum or holder idiom preferred; DCL needs `volatile`; always mention the DI alternative.\n- Abstract Factory = families; Prototype = copy constructors, not `Cloneable`.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- In machine coding, write the factory the moment you have 2+ subtypes — retrofitting later wastes minutes.\n- Records don't need builders under ~4 params — say \"record is enough here\" to show calibration.\n- If you write DCL, preempt with \"volatile prevents seeing a half-constructed instance\" before they ask.\n- Never Singleton entities (Ticket, Order) — only true process-wide services. Singletons holding mutable state need thread-safety (Part 3).\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why is Singleton called an anti-pattern, and when is it still right?**\n> Costs: hidden global dependency (call sites don't declare they use it), hard to substitute in tests, carries mutable global state, and complicates concurrency. Still right for: genuinely process-wide, stateless-or-thread-safe services — config, metrics, ID generation — ideally with the *container* enforcing single-instance rather than the class itself. In interviews I implement it when the domain says \"the system has one X,\" and note I'd inject it rather than call `getInstance()` deep in the code.\n\n**Q2. Walk through why double-checked locking is broken without `volatile`.**\n> `instance = new ParkingLot()` is three steps: allocate, run constructor, publish reference — and the compiler/CPU may reorder publish before construct. Thread B can then pass the outer null-check and use a half-initialized object. `volatile` inserts the barrier making publication happen-after construction. Alternatives that avoid the subtlety entirely: holder idiom (class-init lock does it) or enum.\n\n**Q3. Factory Method vs Abstract Factory vs simple static factory?**\n> Static factory: one method mapping input → concrete product; my default in interviews. GoF Factory Method: creation deferred to *subclasses* via an abstract hook — used when a framework/template owns the flow but not the product. Abstract Factory: an interface producing a *family* of related products that must match (dark button + dark checkbox). Escalate only as the problem demands: most interview problems need just the static factory.\n\n**Q4. Doesn't the factory's `switch` violate Open/Closed?**\n> Technically yes — and that's the accepted trade: I concentrate the violation in one cohesive place so the other 95% of the code is closed. Java's exhaustive `switch` over enums turns \"forgot to update\" into a compile error, which is a feature. If variants multiply or come from plugins, I upgrade to a registration map (`Map<Type, Supplier<Vehicle>>`) — then adding a type touches zero existing lines.\n\n**Q5. When Builder vs constructor vs record?**\n> ≤3 required params, no optionals → constructor or record. Optionals/defaults, cross-field validation, or step-wise assembly (test-data builders!) → Builder. Mutable JavaBean setters are the option I rule out: they allow half-initialized objects and thread-unsafe publication. Bonus senior point: Builder pairs beautifully with immutability — the builder is the only mutable phase, `build()` freezes it.\n"}, {"id": "02-design-patterns/02-structural-patterns", "path": "02-design-patterns/02-structural-patterns.md", "part": "Part 2 · Design Patterns", "title": "2.2 Structural Patterns — Adapter, Decorator, Facade, Composite, Proxy, Flyweight, Bridge", "md": "# 2.2 Structural Patterns — Adapter, Decorator, Facade, Composite, Proxy, Flyweight, Bridge\n\n> Structural patterns are about **wiring objects together** without welding them. The interview stars: Decorator (pricing/toppings), Composite (file systems, org trees), Adapter (third-party gateways), Facade (your service layer — you already use it).\n\n---\n\n## Adapter — make an alien interface fit yours\n\n**Trigger:** integrating third-party/legacy code whose interface doesn't match your abstraction (payment gateways are *the* interview example).\n\n```java\npublic interface PaymentProcessor {                     // YOUR domain interface\n    PaymentResult charge(Money amount, PaymentMethod m);\n}\n\npublic class StripeAdapter implements PaymentProcessor {\n    private final StripeClient stripe;                  // THEIR sdk\n    public StripeAdapter(StripeClient s) { this.stripe = s; }\n\n    @Override public PaymentResult charge(Money amount, PaymentMethod m) {\n        StripeCharge c = stripe.createCharge(            // translate: units, models, errors\n            amount.amountPaise(), amount.currency().code(), toStripeSource(m));\n        return c.succeeded() ? PaymentResult.success(c.id())\n                             : PaymentResult.failure(mapError(c.errorCode()));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nYour domain never imports `com.stripe.*` — swapping Razorpay in = one new adapter. This is DIP with a passport.\n\n## Decorator — stack behavior at runtime\n\n**Trigger:** combinatorial add-ons — coffee toppings, pizza extras, notification channels, streams. The decorator **is** the interface and **has** the interface:\n\n```java\npublic interface Coffee { String desc(); long cost(); }\n\npublic class Espresso implements Coffee {\n    public String desc() { return \"Espresso\"; } public long cost() { return 120; }\n}\n\npublic abstract class CoffeeDecorator implements Coffee {\n    protected final Coffee inner;                        // wraps another Coffee\n    protected CoffeeDecorator(Coffee c) { inner = c; }\n}\npublic class WithMilk extends CoffeeDecorator {\n    public WithMilk(Coffee c) { super(c); }\n    public String desc() { return inner.desc() + \" + milk\"; }\n    public long cost() { return inner.cost() + 30; }\n}\n\n// Coffee order = new WithMilk(new WithMocha(new Espresso()));  // any combo, no class explosion\n```\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Coffee { <<interface>>\n        +desc() String\n        +cost() long\n    }\n    Coffee <|.. Espresso\n    Coffee <|.. CoffeeDecorator\n    CoffeeDecorator o-- Coffee : wraps\n    CoffeeDecorator <|-- WithMilk\n    CoffeeDecorator <|-- WithMocha\n```\n\n**The math to say:** n add-ons via subclassing = 2ⁿ classes; via decorator = n classes. Java's own `BufferedInputStream(new FileInputStream(f))` is the canonical citation.\n\n## Facade — one simple door to a messy subsystem\n\n**Trigger:** a use case touches 4+ subsystems; callers shouldn't choreograph them.\n\n```java\npublic class OrderFacade {\n    private final InventoryService inventory;\n    private final PaymentProcessor payments;\n    private final ShippingService shipping;\n    private final NotificationService notifier;\n\n    public OrderConfirmation placeOrder(Cart cart, PaymentMethod pm) {\n        inventory.reserve(cart.items());\n        PaymentResult pr = payments.charge(cart.total(), pm);\n        if (!pr.ok()) { inventory.release(cart.items()); throw new PaymentFailedException(pr); }\n        Shipment s = shipping.schedule(cart);\n        notifier.orderPlaced(cart.customer(), s);\n        return new OrderConfirmation(s.trackingId());\n    }\n}\n```\n\nEvery `XxxService` you naturally write **is** a facade — name the pattern when you do it. Facade *simplifies* (new interface over many); Adapter *translates* (your interface over one alien).\n\n## Composite — treat one and many uniformly\n\n**Trigger:** tree structures — file systems, org charts, UI widgets, nested comments, menu categories.\n\n```java\npublic interface FileSystemNode {\n    String name(); long size();\n}\npublic class FileNode implements FileSystemNode {                 // leaf\n    private final String name; private final long bytes;\n    public long size() { return bytes; }\n}\npublic class DirectoryNode implements FileSystemNode {            // composite\n    private final String name;\n    private final List<FileSystemNode> children = new ArrayList<>();\n    public void add(FileSystemNode n) { children.add(n); }\n    public long size() { return children.stream().mapToLong(FileSystemNode::size).sum(); }  // recursion\n}\n```\n\nClient asks `root.size()` and never cares if it's a file or a 10-level tree. Design question they'll poke: does `add()` belong on the interface? Putting it there = uniformity but leaves must throw (LSP hit); keeping it on `DirectoryNode` = safety but clients must type-check. **Say the trade-off, pick safety.**\n\n## Proxy — same interface, controlled access\n\nSame interface as the real object, but the proxy adds: lazy loading (virtual proxy), access control (protection proxy), caching, rate-limiting, or remoteness (RPC stubs).\n\n```java\npublic class CachingProductProxy implements ProductCatalog {\n    private final ProductCatalog real;                     // same interface — drop-in\n    private final Map<String, Product> cache = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    public Product byId(String id) {\n        return cache.computeIfAbsent(id, real::byId);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Proxy vs Decorator (favorite question):** same shape, different intent — decorator *adds behavior the client wants*, proxy *controls access the client doesn't see* (and often manages the real object's lifecycle). Spring AOP/`@Transactional` = dynamic proxies; Hibernate lazy-loading = virtual proxies.\n\n## Flyweight — share the heavy immutable part\n\n**Trigger:** millions of objects with repeated intrinsic state. Split: **intrinsic** (shared, immutable — glyph shape, tree texture, chess piece type) vs **extrinsic** (per-instance — position).\n\n```java\n// Chess needs 32 pieces on the board but only 12 distinct piece-type objects ever.\npublic enum PieceType { /* KING, QUEEN ... with movement rules */ }   // flyweights\nrecord PlacedPiece(PieceType type, Color color, Square at) {}          // extrinsic: position\n```\n\nJava citation: `Integer.valueOf(-128..127)` returns cached instances; `String` interning. Mention memory math (\"1M cache entries sharing 100 metadata objects\") and move on.\n\n## Bridge — two independent axes of variation\n\n**Trigger:** a hierarchy multiplying across two dimensions (`Shape × RenderApi`, `Notification × Channel`). Split into abstraction + implementor and compose:\n\n```java\npublic abstract class Notification {              // axis 1: kind of message\n    protected final MessageSender sender;          // bridge to axis 2: channel\n    protected Notification(MessageSender s) { sender = s; }\n    public abstract void send(User u);\n}\npublic interface MessageSender { void deliver(String to, String body); }  // email/sms/push\n// UrgentNotification × SmsSender, DigestNotification × EmailSender — n+m classes, not n×m\n```\n\nBridge is \"composition over inheritance\" formalized. If you catch yourself naming a class `EmailUrgentNotification`, say \"two axes — I'll bridge them.\"\n\n---\n\n## Choosing quickly\n\n| Smell in the problem | Pattern |\n|---|---|\n| Third-party SDK's interface ≠ yours | Adapter |\n| Optional add-ons stack in any combination | Decorator |\n| One use case orchestrates many subsystems | Facade |\n| Tree / part-whole, uniform operations | Composite |\n| Control/loading/caching around a real object | Proxy |\n| Millions of similar objects, memory pressure | Flyweight |\n| Class names concatenating two dimensions | Bridge |\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Adapter translates, Facade simplifies, Proxy controls, Decorator enriches — same wrapping shape, four intents. Interviewers test *intent*, not shape.\n- Decorator kills 2ⁿ subclass explosions; Composite makes recursion disappear behind an interface.\n- Flyweight = share immutable intrinsic state; Bridge = split two independently-varying axes.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Cite the JDK: streams (Decorator), `Collections.unmodifiableList` (Proxy), `Integer.valueOf` (Flyweight), NIO `Files` (Facade) — one citation each = instant depth signal.\n- In pricing problems (coffee/pizza), *start* with Decorator; it's the expected answer and unlocks the follow-ups.\n- When wrapping anything, keep the wrapper implementing the same interface — that's what makes all four wrap-patterns composable.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Decorator vs inheritance for coffee add-ons — quantify the win.**\n> With inheritance, every combination is a class: 5 toppings → 2⁵ = 32 classes, and \"double milk\" is impossible. With Decorator: 1 base + 5 decorators = 6 classes, combinations composed at runtime, duplicates allowed, order visible in construction. Cost: many small objects and stack-like debugging — acceptable price, and the same trade the JDK made for streams.\n\n**Q2. Proxy and Decorator look identical in UML. How do you tell them apart in a codebase?**\n> Ask what the wrapper knows and why it exists. Decorator: client composes it deliberately to *add* visible behavior; wrappers stack; the wrapped object is handed in. Proxy: client usually doesn't know it's there; it *controls* access (lazy-init, auth, caching, remote) and often creates/owns the real subject itself; typically one layer. Rule of thumb: found in business code composing features → decorator; found at infrastructure boundaries → proxy.\n\n**Q3. Design an in-memory file system's size calculation. Which pattern and why?**\n> Composite. `FileSystemNode` interface with `size()`; `File` leaf returns bytes; `Directory` holds `List<FileSystemNode>` and sums recursively. Uniformity means `du`-style logic is one polymorphic call — no instanceof, no separate traversal code. I'd keep `add/remove` on `Directory` only (type safety over uniformity) and note the LSP reasoning. Full build-out in Ch 5.13.\n\n**Q4. Where does Adapter sit in a clean architecture?**\n> At the boundary, pointing inward: the domain defines the port (`PaymentProcessor`), adapters in the infrastructure layer implement it by translating models, units, and error taxonomies of each vendor. Domain code has zero vendor imports; vendor swap = new adapter + config change. This is the hexagonal \"ports and adapters\" naming — the pattern and the architecture are the same idea at two scales.\n\n**Q5. When is Flyweight actually worth it?**\n> When profiling (or arithmetic) shows object count × intrinsic-state size matters: text editors (a glyph object per character is absurd), game boards, map tiles, connection metadata. Preconditions: the shared state must be immutable, and identity must not matter (`==` comparisons break). If those don't hold — or the count is thousands, not millions — it's premature optimization; modern JVMs are fine with thousands of small objects, and I'd say so.\n"}, {"id": "02-design-patterns/03-behavioral-patterns-1", "path": "02-design-patterns/03-behavioral-patterns-1.md", "part": "Part 2 · Design Patterns", "title": "2.3 Behavioral Patterns I — Strategy, Observer, Command, State", "md": "# 2.3 Behavioral Patterns I — Strategy, Observer, Command, State\n\n> These four carry more interview weight than all other patterns combined. **Strategy** appears in nearly every case study; **State** owns vending machines and elevators; **Observer** owns notifications; **Command** owns undo and schedulers.\n\n---\n\n## Strategy — swap the algorithm\n\n**Trigger words in the problem:** \"different ways to…\", \"pricing rules\", \"matching logic\", \"payment methods\", \"eviction policy\".\n\n```java\npublic interface DriverMatchingStrategy {\n    Optional<Driver> match(RideRequest req, List<Driver> available);\n}\npublic class NearestDriverStrategy implements DriverMatchingStrategy { ... }\npublic class HighestRatedStrategy implements DriverMatchingStrategy { ... }\n\npublic class RideService {\n    private final DriverMatchingStrategy matching;                 // injected → swappable\n    public RideService(DriverMatchingStrategy m) { matching = m; }\n}\n```\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class RideService { -DriverMatchingStrategy matching }\n    class DriverMatchingStrategy { <<interface>> +match(req, drivers) Optional~Driver~ }\n    RideService o-- DriverMatchingStrategy\n    DriverMatchingStrategy <|.. NearestDriverStrategy\n    DriverMatchingStrategy <|.. HighestRatedStrategy\n    DriverMatchingStrategy <|.. SurgeAwareStrategy\n```\n\nModern Java note: a strategy with one method can be a lambda — `FeeStrategy hourly = d -> d.toHours() * 20;`. Say it; still name the interface (the *name* is the documentation).\n\n**Strategy is your OCP answer** to every \"what if a new X rule comes?\" follow-up. Selection lives in a factory/map: `Map<PlanType, FeeStrategy>`.\n\n## Observer — one event, many reactions, zero coupling\n\n**Trigger:** \"notify\", \"when X happens, Y and Z should…\", price alerts, seat availability, game move → UI/logger/bot.\n\n```java\npublic interface OrderListener { void onOrderPlaced(Order o); }\n\npublic class OrderService {\n    private final List<OrderListener> listeners = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();  // safe iteration\n    public void register(OrderListener l) { listeners.add(l); }\n\n    public void place(Order o) {\n        repo.save(o);                                              // core logic first\n        for (OrderListener l : listeners) {\n            try { l.onOrderPlaced(o); }                            // one bad listener\n            catch (Exception e) { log.warn(\"listener failed\", e); } // must not break the rest\n        }\n    }\n}\n// InventoryUpdater, EmailConfirmation, AnalyticsTracker — each a listener; adding one touches nothing\n```\n\n**Design points that score:** publisher knows only the interface; exceptions isolated per listener; `CopyOnWriteArrayList` so listeners can (un)register during notification; mention sync vs async delivery (\"for slow listeners I'd hand events to an executor — that's the road to pub-sub, Ch 5.12\").\n\n## Command — reify an action into an object\n\n**Trigger:** undo/redo, task queues/schedulers, transactional macros, hotkey remapping, text editors.\n\n```java\npublic interface Command { void execute(); void undo(); }\n\npublic class InsertTextCommand implements Command {\n    private final Document doc; private final int pos; private final String text;\n    public void execute() { doc.insert(pos, text); }\n    public void undo()    { doc.delete(pos, text.length()); }\n}\n\npublic class CommandInvoker {                                  // history = undo/redo for free\n    private final Deque<Command> undoStack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    private final Deque<Command> redoStack = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    public void run(Command c) { c.execute(); undoStack.push(c); redoStack.clear(); }\n    public void undo() { if (!undoStack.isEmpty()) { Command c = undoStack.pop(); c.undo(); redoStack.push(c); } }\n    public void redo() { if (!redoStack.isEmpty()) { Command c = redoStack.pop(); c.execute(); undoStack.push(c); } }\n}\n```\n\nThe insight to narrate: **once an action is an object, you can store, queue, log, replay, and invert it.** Invoker knows nothing about documents — only `Command`. `Runnable` is Command without undo.\n\n## State — behavior that changes with internal state\n\n**Trigger:** the entity's *response to the same event* depends on its mode — vending machine, elevator, order lifecycle, ATM, media player, Tic-Tac-Toe turn handling.\n\n```java\npublic interface MachineState {\n    MachineState insertCoin(VendingMachine m, long amount);\n    MachineState selectItem(VendingMachine m, String code);\n    MachineState dispense(VendingMachine m);\n}\n\npublic class IdleState implements MachineState {\n    public MachineState insertCoin(VendingMachine m, long amt) {\n        m.addBalance(amt);\n        return new HasMoneyState();                       // transition = return next state\n    }\n    public MachineState selectItem(VendingMachine m, String c) {\n        m.display(\"Insert coin first\"); return this;      // invalid event: explicit, not crash\n    }\n    public MachineState dispense(VendingMachine m) { m.display(\"Nothing to dispense\"); return this; }\n}\n\npublic class VendingMachine {\n    private MachineState state = new IdleState();\n    public void insertCoin(long amt) { state = state.insertCoin(this, amt); }  // delegate everything\n    public void selectItem(String c) { state = state.selectItem(this, c); }\n}\n```\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Idle\n    Idle --> HasMoney : insertCoin\n    HasMoney --> Dispensing : selectItem (paid enough)\n    HasMoney --> HasMoney : insertCoin\n    Dispensing --> Idle : dispense + change\n    HasMoney --> Idle : cancel / refund\n```\n\n**Why not an enum + switch?** For 2–3 states with trivial transitions, enum+switch is fine — say so. State pattern wins when each state has real behavior: no `if(state==X && event==Y)` matrix, invalid transitions are impossible to forget (every state must answer every event), and adding a state = adding a class.\n\n**State vs Strategy (asked constantly):** identical class structure. Strategy: *client* picks the algorithm, variants don't know each other, rarely changes mid-flight. State: *the object itself* transitions between states, states know their successors, changes constantly at runtime.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Strategy = pluggable algorithm chosen from outside; the universal OCP answer.\n- Observer = decoupled fan-out; isolate listener failures; know the async upgrade path.\n- Command = action as object → queues, logs, undo/redo via two stacks.\n- State = event handling delegated to a state object returning the next state; draw the state diagram first.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Before coding a state machine, **draw the state diagram and get a nod** — coding transitions from an agreed picture is mechanical.\n- In Observer, firing listeners *after* persisting is a correctness point — say it (\"no emails for orders that failed to save\").\n- Undo needs commands to capture *inverse data* (deleted text, previous value) at execute-time — mention this; it's the trap in undo questions.\n- If you hear \"the same button does different things depending on…\" — that's State, verbatim.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. State vs Strategy — same UML, so what's actually different?**\n> Intent and lifecycle. Strategy: interchangeable algorithms for one task; chosen by the client/config; strategies are peers unaware of each other; typically fixed for the object's lifetime. State: modes of one object where behavior *and allowed events* differ; the current state decides the next state (they know each other); transitions happen constantly, driven from inside. Quick test: who changes the implementation at runtime? Outside → Strategy. The object itself → State.\n\n**Q2. Your Observer publisher must not miss events, and a listener is slow. Options?**\n> Separate delivery from computation: publisher appends to a queue per listener and returns; a worker drains each queue (this is exactly a mini pub-sub broker). Slow listener then delays only itself; backpressure = bounded queue + policy (block producer / drop oldest / drop listener with alert). If in-process and simple must stay: executor per listener or a shared pool with per-listener ordering keys. And I'd note \"no missed events across restarts\" needs persistence — the Outbox idea from HLD.\n\n**Q3. Implement undo for a text editor. Why Command over Memento?**\n> Command stores *deltas* (inverse operations): `InsertText.undo()` deletes the inserted range — O(op) memory, natural redo, and an audit log for free. Memento snapshots *whole state* — O(document) per step, but trivially correct even when operations aren't invertible. Real editors mix: commands for typing, periodic snapshots as checkpoints (bounding replay cost). I'd default to Command with the two-stack invoker and mention snapshot checkpoints as the scaling valve.\n\n**Q4. How does the State pattern prevent illegal transitions, versus a status enum?**\n> With an enum, every handler must remember to check `if (status == PAID)` — forgetting one check silently allows `ship()` on an unpaid order. With State, `UnpaidState` simply has a `ship()` that raises/ignores by *design*: the compiler forces every state to answer every event, so the full event×state matrix is enumerated — nothing falls through. The state diagram becomes executable documentation.\n\n**Q5. Where would you use Strategy + Factory together?**\n> Whenever the strategy is picked from data: `PaymentMethod` enum → `PaymentStrategyFactory.for(method)` returns UPI/Card/Wallet strategy from a pre-built map. The factory is the single place that knows all variants; consumers see only the interface; adding a variant = one class + one map entry. This combo answers \"how does the right algorithm get chosen?\" — the question Strategy alone leaves open.\n"}, {"id": "02-design-patterns/04-behavioral-patterns-2", "path": "02-design-patterns/04-behavioral-patterns-2.md", "part": "Part 2 · Design Patterns", "title": "2.4 Behavioral Patterns II — Template Method, Chain of Responsibility, Iterator, Mediator, Memento, Visitor, Null Object", "md": "# 2.4 Behavioral Patterns II — Template Method, Chain of Responsibility, Iterator, Mediator, Memento, Visitor, Null Object\n\n> The supporting cast. Chain of Responsibility powers the Logger and ATM questions; Template Method appears in any \"common workflow, varying steps\" problem; the rest you need at *recognition* depth.\n\n---\n\n## Template Method — fixed skeleton, pluggable steps\n\n**Trigger:** several workflows share the same step sequence but differ in specific steps (report generation, data pipelines, game loops, onboarding flows).\n\n```java\npublic abstract class DataExporter {\n    // the template: final so subclasses can't break the sequence\n    public final void export(Query q, Path out) {\n        List<Row> rows = fetch(q);\n        byte[] bytes = format(rows);          // varying step\n        write(out, bytes);\n        afterExport(out);                     // hook: optional override, default no-op\n    }\n    protected List<Row> fetch(Query q) { /* shared impl */ return db.run(q); }\n    protected abstract byte[] format(List<Row> rows);          // must vary\n    protected void afterExport(Path out) {}                    // may vary\n}\npublic class CsvExporter extends DataExporter {\n    protected byte[] format(List<Row> rows) { /* csv */ }\n}\n```\n\n**Hollywood principle:** \"don't call us, we'll call you\" — the parent drives, children fill blanks. Modern alternative to mention: pass steps as lambdas/strategies instead of subclassing (\"Template via composition\") — fewer classes, no inheritance lock-in. JDK citation: `AbstractList`, servlet `service()`→`doGet/doPost`.\n\n## Chain of Responsibility — pass the request down a pipeline\n\n**Trigger:** logger levels, ATM cash denominations, approval workflows (manager → director → VP), middleware/filters, support-ticket escalation.\n\n```java\npublic abstract class LogHandler {\n    protected final LogLevel level;\n    protected LogHandler next;                                 // the chain link\n    protected LogHandler(LogLevel l) { level = l; }\n    public LogHandler setNext(LogHandler n) { next = n; return n; }\n\n    public void handle(LogLevel msgLevel, String msg) {\n        if (msgLevel.ordinal() >= level.ordinal()) write(msg); // handle...\n        if (next != null) next.handle(msgLevel, msg);          // ...and/or pass on\n    }\n    protected abstract void write(String msg);\n}\n// ConsoleHandler(INFO) → FileHandler(WARN) → AlertHandler(ERROR)\n```\n\nTwo flavors — **say which you're building**: (a) *first-match-wins* (ATM dispenser, support escalation — stop after handling); (b) *process-and-continue* (loggers, servlet filters — everyone gets a look). Client holds only the head; chain assembly is configuration.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    C[Client] --> H1[ConsoleHandler ≥ INFO] --> H2[FileHandler ≥ WARN] --> H3[AlertHandler ≥ ERROR] --> X[end]\n```\n\n## Iterator — sequential access without exposing structure\n\nYou use it every day (`for (Item i : cart)`). Interview depth needed: implement `Iterable<T>`/`Iterator<T>` on your own class so internals stay private.\n\n```java\npublic class Playlist implements Iterable<Song> {\n    private final List<Song> songs = new ArrayList<>();\n    @Override public Iterator<Song> iterator() {\n        return new Iterator<>() {                               // could be shuffled, lazy, filtered…\n            private int i = 0;\n            public boolean hasNext() { return i < songs.size(); }\n            public Song next() { if (!hasNext()) throw new NoSuchElementException(); return songs.get(i++); }\n        };\n    }\n}\n```\n\nPoints worth a sentence: multiple simultaneous traversals (each `iterator()` call = fresh cursor), `ConcurrentModificationException` is fail-fast detection of structural change mid-iteration, and custom iterators can encode policy (tree in-order, paginated fetch).\n\n## Mediator — a hub instead of a mesh\n\n**Trigger:** n objects all talking to each other (chat rooms, air-traffic control, complex form widgets, auction houses). n² pairwise links → each talks only to the mediator.\n\n```java\npublic class ChatRoom {                                        // the mediator\n    private final Map<String, User> users = new HashMap<>();\n    public void send(String from, String to, String msg) { users.get(to).receive(from, msg); }\n    public void broadcast(String from, String msg) {\n        users.values().stream().filter(u -> !u.name().equals(from))\n             .forEach(u -> u.receive(from, msg));\n    }\n}\n```\n\nColleagues stay dumb and reusable; interaction rules live in one place. Risk to admit: the mediator can grow into a god class — split by interaction type when it does. Observer vs Mediator: observer broadcasts \"something happened\" one-way; mediator *coordinates* multi-party conversations with logic.\n\n## Memento — snapshot state without breaking encapsulation\n\n**Trigger:** save/restore, checkpoints, transactional rollback of an object, game saves.\n\n```java\npublic class Editor {\n    private StringBuilder text = new StringBuilder();\n    public record Snapshot(String text) {}                     // opaque to outsiders\n    public Snapshot save() { return new Snapshot(text.toString()); }\n    public void restore(Snapshot s) { text = new StringBuilder(s.text()); }\n}\n// caretaker (History) stores Snapshots but cannot peek inside or edit them\n```\n\nKey phrase: the *originator* creates/consumes snapshots; the *caretaker* only stores them. Pairs with Command for undo (Ch 2.3 Q3).\n\n## Visitor — new operations over a fixed object family\n\n**Trigger:** stable class hierarchy (AST nodes, shapes, file nodes) + ever-growing operations (print, evaluate, export, lint). Double dispatch via `accept`:\n\n```java\npublic interface Shape { <R> R accept(ShapeVisitor<R> v); }\npublic record Circle(double r) implements Shape {\n    public <R> R accept(ShapeVisitor<R> v) { return v.visit(this); }\n}\npublic interface ShapeVisitor<R> { R visit(Circle c); R visit(Rect r); }\n// AreaVisitor, PerimeterVisitor, SvgExportVisitor — new ops, zero edits to shapes\n```\n\nTrade: adding operations is easy, adding *element types* touches every visitor — the exact inverse of polymorphic methods. **Modern Java note:** sealed interfaces + pattern-matching `switch` (Java 21) now cover most Visitor use cases with less ceremony — mentioning this is a strong currency signal.\n\n## Null Object — a do-nothing implementation instead of null\n\n```java\npublic final class NoDiscount implements DiscountPolicy {\n    public long discount(long amount) { return 0; }            // safe default, no null checks\n}\n```\n\nKills scattered `if (policy != null)`. Use for optional collaborators (logger, discount, listener). Don't overuse — a Null Object that hides a genuine error is worse than an NPE.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Template Method = frozen skeleton + abstract steps + optional hooks; prefer lambda-composition when no shared state.\n- CoR = pipeline of handlers; know first-match vs process-all flavors; it's the Logger/ATM answer.\n- Mediator centralizes n² chatter; Memento = opaque snapshots; Visitor = operations over a sealed family (or use sealed+switch).\n- Iterator/Null Object: small, but naming them at the right moment earns points.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- ATM cash dispensing = CoR over denominations (2000 → 500 → 100), first-match style with remainder passing — a 5-minute win if you've rehearsed it.\n- Approval-workflow questions (\"expense > ₹50k needs VP\") are CoR verbatim: handler = approver level.\n- If the interviewer's problem has both a fixed pipeline *and* varying steps, Template (vertical: skeleton) and CoR (horizontal: handlers) can coexist — say which axis each covers.\n- Visitor only when they say the hierarchy is stable; otherwise answer \"polymorphic method\" and save 10 minutes.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Template Method vs Strategy — both vary an algorithm. Choose.**\n> Template varies *steps inside* a fixed skeleton via inheritance: one workflow, blanks filled by subclasses; the parent controls order. Strategy varies the *whole algorithm* via composition: swappable at runtime, testable standalone, no inheritance chain. Default to Strategy for flexibility; choose Template when the invariant sequence itself is the asset to protect (and mark the template `final`). Middle path I often take: template class whose steps are injected functions.\n\n**Q2. Design a logging framework's level handling. Why CoR over a simple if-ladder?**\n> Handlers (console/file/alert) each carry a threshold and a `next`. Win over if-ladder: the *pipeline is data, not code* — reconfigure order, add a RemoteHandler, or give prod vs dev different chains without touching logic; each handler is single-responsibility and unit-testable. It's process-and-continue flavor: every handler sees every message and decides independently. Full design in Ch 5.10.\n\n**Q3. How does Visitor achieve double dispatch, and why is it needed?**\n> Java picks methods by the *static* type of arguments — `print(shape)` can't pick `print(Circle)` at runtime. Visitor routes through two virtual calls: `shape.accept(v)` dispatches on the shape's runtime type; inside, `v.visit(this)` dispatches on the visitor with `this` statically typed as `Circle`. Two single dispatches compose into behavior chosen by *both* types. Needed whenever an operation depends on the concrete element type but can't live inside the element.\n\n**Q4. Your Mediator is becoming a god class. Now what?**\n> Expected failure mode — centralizing interaction logic concentrates it. Fixes: split by conversation type (SeatSelectionMediator vs PaymentMediator); demote simple notifications to Observer events and keep only genuine multi-party coordination in the mediator; extract rule objects (Strategy) the mediator merely sequences. If most interactions are broadcast-shaped, the honest refactor is to an event bus and delete the mediator.\n\n**Q5. Iterator seems trivial — what's actually worth discussing?**\n> Three things. Encapsulation: iteration without exposing whether it's an array, tree, or remote pages — I can change structure without breaking clients. Policy iterators: shuffle-play, filtered views, BFS vs DFS over a composite — the traversal *algorithm* is a first-class object. Concurrency: fail-fast iterators + `ConcurrentModificationException`, vs snapshot iterators (`CopyOnWriteArrayList`) vs weakly-consistent ones (`ConcurrentHashMap`) — choosing the right collection *is* choosing iterator semantics (Part 3).\n"}, {"id": "02-design-patterns/05-choosing-patterns", "path": "02-design-patterns/05-choosing-patterns.md", "part": "Part 2 · Design Patterns", "title": "2.5 Choosing the Right Pattern — The Problem → Pattern Map", "md": "# 2.5 Choosing the Right Pattern — The Problem → Pattern Map\n\n> Patterns are answers; interviews give you problems. This chapter is the reverse index: hear the requirement, recognize the shape, name the pattern *with a reason*. Never say a pattern name without the reason.\n\n---\n\n## The master map\n\n| You hear… | Shape of the problem | Pattern | One-line justification to say |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| \"different pricing / matching / eviction rules\" | algorithm varies | **Strategy** | \"new rules shouldn't edit tested code\" |\n| \"notify users / update scoreboard when X happens\" | one event, many reactions | **Observer** | \"publisher shouldn't know its consumers\" |\n| \"the machine behaves differently once coin inserted\" | mode-dependent behavior | **State** | \"no if-matrix; illegal events impossible\" |\n| \"undo / redo / schedule / replay actions\" | actions need to be values | **Command** | \"an action object can be stored & inverted\" |\n| \"toppings / add-ons / optional features stack\" | combinatorial add-ons | **Decorator** | \"n classes instead of 2ⁿ\" |\n| \"folders contain files or folders\" | tree, uniform ops | **Composite** | \"clients treat one and many the same\" |\n| \"integrate Stripe/Razorpay/legacy API\" | alien interface | **Adapter** | \"domain stays vendor-free\" |\n| \"one call should orchestrate 5 subsystems\" | complexity behind a door | **Facade** | \"callers get a use-case API\" |\n| \"log levels / approval levels / cash denominations\" | pipeline of handlers | **Chain of Responsibility** | \"pipeline is config, not code\" |\n| \"exactly one lot / logger / config\" | single instance | **Singleton** | \"one instance, but I'd DI it\" |\n| \"create the right subclass from a type\" | creation varies | **Factory** | \"one place knows the concretes\" |\n| \"object with 6 optional fields, immutable\" | complex construction | **Builder** | \"named steps + validate once + immutable\" |\n| \"same workflow, steps differ per subtype\" | skeleton + blanks | **Template Method** | \"the sequence is the invariant\" |\n| \"control access / cache / lazy-load the real thing\" | stand-in object | **Proxy** | \"client can't tell; access is governed\" |\n| \"millions of similar objects\" | memory from duplication | **Flyweight** | \"share the immutable part\" |\n| \"chat members / widgets all inter-talking\" | n² links | **Mediator** | \"n links to a hub instead\" |\n| \"class names = Axis1×Axis2 combinations\" | two dimensions multiply | **Bridge** | \"n+m classes, not n×m\" |\n\n## Recognition flowchart\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Q{{\"What is varying?\"}}\n    Q -->|\"an algorithm/rule\"| S[Strategy]\n    Q -->|\"object creation\"| C{{\"single product or family?\"}}\n    C -->|single| F[Factory Method]\n    C -->|family| AF[Abstract Factory]\n    C -->|\"many optional params\"| B[Builder]\n    Q -->|\"behavior over time\"| ST[State]\n    Q -->|\"who reacts to events\"| O[Observer]\n    Q -->|\"features stacking\"| D[Decorator]\n    Q -->|\"who handles a request\"| CH[Chain of Responsibility]\n    Q -->|\"structure is a tree\"| CP[Composite]\n    Q -->|\"interfaces don't fit\"| A[Adapter]\n```\n\n## Pattern combos that win case studies\n\nReal designs are pattern *sentences*, not single words:\n\n- **Parking Lot** = Singleton lot + **Strategy** (spot assignment, fee) + **Factory** (vehicles) + Observer (display boards).\n- **Vending Machine / ATM** = **State** (machine modes) + CoR (cash denominations) + Singleton.\n- **Elevator** = **State** (moving/idle/door) + **Strategy** (scheduling: nearest-car, LOOK) + Observer (floor displays).\n- **BookMyShow** = **Factory** + **Strategy** (pricing) + Observer (seat release) + explicit locking (Part 3).\n- **Logger** = **Chain of Responsibility** + Singleton + **Strategy** (formatters/appenders).\n- **Splitwise** = **Strategy** (equal/exact/percent splits) + Factory + Observer (balance notifications).\n- **Chess** = **Factory** (pieces) + **Strategy** (per-piece movement) + Command (moves → undo) + Memento (board snapshots).\n- **Uber** = **Strategy** (matching, pricing) + **State** (trip lifecycle) + Observer (location updates).\n- **Text editor** = **Command** (+ Memento checkpoints) + Flyweight (glyphs) + Composite (document tree).\n\nNotice the frequency table: Strategy ~everywhere, State in anything machine-like, Observer wherever something must be *told*. That's your prior.\n\n## The anti-checklist — when NOT to pattern\n\n- **Two variants, no third in sight** → an `if` is honest. Patterns pay rent when variation is real.\n- **Pattern before problem** (\"let me add Observer in case…\") → YAGNI violation, reads as cargo cult.\n- **Singleton as global grab-bag** → hidden coupling; inject instead.\n- **Visitor on an evolving hierarchy** → every new type breaks every visitor.\n- **Decorator for one fixed add-on** → just a field.\n- The tell interviewers watch for: can you produce the **reason** on demand? \"Strategy, because the problem statement promises new fee rules\" ✓. \"Strategy, because it's best practice\" ✗.\n\n## How to *say* patterns in the interview\n\n1. **Recognize aloud:** \"Pricing varies by plan — that's a Strategy seam.\"\n2. **Justify with the change axis:** \"so future rules are add-a-class, not edit-the-service.\"\n3. **Show the seam in code:** interface + injection point (implementations can stay stubbed).\n4. **Concede the cost:** \"more classes; if rules never multiply this was overhead — I think the requirement justifies it.\"\n\nThat 4-beat rhythm — recognize, justify, seam, cost — is what \"uses patterns judiciously\" means on the rubric.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Map requirement *shapes* to patterns; the reason is mandatory, the name is optional.\n- Real designs are combos: Strategy + State + Factory covers most of Part 5.\n- Under-patterning a stated variation loses points; over-patterning an unstated one loses more.\n- Rhythm: recognize → justify → seam → cost.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Memorize the combo list above — it doubles as a table of contents for Part 5.\n- When stuck, ask \"what did the interviewer say will change?\" — the answer names your pattern.\n- If you can't decide between two patterns in 30 seconds, pick the simpler one and state the upgrade path.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Interviewer: \"Why did you use a pattern here at all?\"**\n> Because the requirement encodes a change axis: you said fee rules differ per vehicle and will grow. The Strategy seam makes that growth additive — new class, no edits to tested code — and it cost one interface. If that requirement disappeared, I'd delete the interface: patterns are responses to variation, not decoration.\n\n**Q2. \"Which pattern is most overused, in your opinion?\"**\n> Singleton — it's really a global variable with ceremony: hidden dependencies, test pollution via shared state, and a concurrency hazard when it holds mutable data. Second place: Factory layers wrapping a constructor that has exactly one implementation. My rule: a pattern must be *pulled in* by a requirement I can quote; otherwise it's résumé-driven design.\n\n**Q3. \"Design X\" has three patterns applicable. How do you present that?**\n> Lead with the one solving the stated pain, mention the others as seams. For a notification system: Observer is the spine (decoupled fan-out); Strategy sits inside each channel for formatting; Factory picks channels from user prefs. Presenting them as *layers with jobs* — spine, variation point, creation point — shows architecture, not pattern bingo.\n\n**Q4. \"Can you solve it without any patterns?\"**\n> Yes, and I'd start there: concrete classes, direct calls, an if per variation. The patterns emerge as refactors when variation shows up — that's actually their definition: named refactoring targets. What I won't do is *hide* structure to seem simple: if the if-ladder on type already exists in three methods, extracting Strategy is the simpler design, pattern name or not.\n\n**Q5. \"How do patterns relate to SOLID?\"**\n> Patterns are SOLID's standard solutions. OCP → Strategy/Decorator/Template (extend without editing). DIP → Adapter/Factory (depend on abstractions, translate at edges). SRP → Facade/Mediator (separate orchestration from work). LSP discipline shows up in State/Composite contracts; ISP shapes the small interfaces patterns depend on. If I forget every pattern name but honor SOLID, I'll reinvent them — patterns are just the shared vocabulary for the shapes SOLID produces.\n"}, {"id": "03-concurrency/01-java-concurrency-essentials", "path": "03-concurrency/01-java-concurrency-essentials.md", "part": "Part 3 · Concurrency", "title": "3.1 Java Concurrency Essentials — Threads, Locks, Visibility, Executors", "md": "# 3.1 Java Concurrency Essentials — Threads, Locks, Visibility, Executors\n\n> \"Is your design thread-safe?\" is the sharpest follow-up in LLD interviews. This chapter gives you the vocabulary and tools; Ch 3.3 turns them into interview moves.\n\n---\n\n## The two problems (name them precisely)\n\n1. **Atomicity / race conditions:** `count++` is read-modify-write; two threads interleave and one update is lost. Check-then-act (`if (spot.isFree()) spot.assign(v)`) races the same way.\n2. **Visibility / ordering:** without synchronization, one thread's write may *never* be seen by another (CPU caches, compiler reordering). A `boolean running` flag without `volatile` can loop forever.\n\nBoth are fixed by establishing **happens-before** edges: `synchronized`, `volatile`, `Lock`, atomics, thread start/join, and concurrent-collection operations all create them. Using the term \"happens-before\" correctly is a senior signal.\n\n## synchronized — the default tool\n\n```java\npublic class SpotInventory {\n    private final Map<SpotId, Spot> spots = new HashMap<>();\n    private final Object lock = new Object();               // private lock object > locking 'this'\n\n    public Optional<Spot> reserve(VehicleType t) {\n        synchronized (lock) {                               // atomicity + visibility together\n            return spots.values().stream()\n                .filter(s -> s.fits(t) && s.isFree())\n                .findFirst()\n                .map(s -> { s.assign(); return s; });       // check-then-act INSIDE the lock\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n- Reentrant (a thread can re-acquire its own monitor).\n- **Lock the check and the act together** — the classic interview bug is synchronizing them separately.\n- Prefer a private final lock object: locking `this` lets outside code interfere with your locking protocol.\n\n## volatile — visibility only, not atomicity\n\n```java\nprivate volatile boolean shutdownRequested;      // ✓ flag: one writer, many readers\nprivate volatile int count;                       // ✗ count++ still races (read-modify-write)\n```\n\nUse for: flags, publish-once references (the DCL singleton). If more than one thread *writes based on the old value* → you need atomics or locks.\n\n## Atomics — lock-free counters and CAS\n\n```java\nprivate final AtomicLong ticketSeq = new AtomicLong();\npublic String nextTicketId() { return \"T-\" + ticketSeq.incrementAndGet(); }\n\n// CAS loop — the idiom behind all atomics:\npublic boolean tryReserve(AtomicReference<SpotState> state) {\n    SpotState cur = state.get();\n    return cur == FREE && state.compareAndSet(FREE, HELD);   // succeeds only if unchanged\n}\n```\n\n`compareAndSet` = \"set only if still what I read\" — optimistic concurrency in one instruction. Say \"CAS\" when explaining `AtomicInteger`, `LongAdder` (better under heavy contention), and lock-free structures.\n\n## ReentrantLock — when synchronized isn't enough\n\n```java\nprivate final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();     // (true) = fair mode, FIFO\n\npublic boolean parkWithTimeout(Vehicle v) throws InterruptedException {\n    if (!lock.tryLock(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) return false;   // can't do this with synchronized\n    try {\n        return doPark(v);\n    } finally {\n        lock.unlock();                                       // ALWAYS in finally\n    }\n}\n```\n\nChoose over `synchronized` for: `tryLock` / timeouts (deadlock avoidance), fairness, multiple `Condition`s per lock, lock across non-block-structured code. Also know **`ReadWriteLock`**: many concurrent readers, exclusive writer — ideal for read-heavy caches/inventories. (**`StampedLock`** exists for optimistic reads — name-drop only.)\n\n## wait/notify & Condition — waiting for state, not spinning\n\n```java\n// bounded buffer, Condition version (Ch 3.3 has the full producer-consumer)\nlock.lock();\ntry {\n    while (queue.size() == capacity)      // WHILE, never if — spurious wakeups + recheck\n        notFull.await();\n    queue.add(item);\n    notEmpty.signal();\n} finally { lock.unlock(); }\n```\n\nThe three rules they probe: hold the lock while waiting/signaling; **wait in a while-loop**; prefer `signal`-with-distinct-conditions over `notifyAll`-storms. Then say: \"in real code I'd use `BlockingQueue` and delete all of this\" (Ch 3.2).\n\n## Executors — never raw `new Thread()` in a design\n\n```java\nExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(8);          // CPU-bound: ≈ cores\nScheduledExecutorService timer = Executors.newScheduledThreadPool(1);  // timeouts, sweepers\n\nFuture<Receipt> f = pool.submit(() -> exitService.process(ticket));\ntimer.scheduleAtFixedRate(this::releaseExpiredHolds, 1, 1, TimeUnit.MINUTES);\n```\n\nDesign-conversation points: thread pools bound resource use and reuse threads; `CompletableFuture` for composing async steps; **virtual threads (Java 21)** — \"with Loom I'd give each request its own virtual thread and keep the code blocking-style\" is a great modern aside. Always shut pools down (`shutdown()` + `awaitTermination`).\n\n## Deadlock — the four conditions and the one fix you must know\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    T1[Thread 1<br/>holds Lock A] -->|wants| LB[Lock B]\n    T2[Thread 2<br/>holds Lock B] -->|wants| LA[Lock A]\n    LB -.held by.-> T2\n    LA -.held by.-> T1\n```\n\nNeeds all four: mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait. **Standard fix: global lock ordering** — always acquire locks in a canonical order (e.g., by account ID):\n\n```java\npublic void transfer(Account a, Account b, long amt) {\n    Account first = a.id().compareTo(b.id()) < 0 ? a : b;    // order by ID\n    Account second = first == a ? b : a;\n    synchronized (first) { synchronized (second) { doTransfer(a, b, amt); } }\n}\n```\n\nAlternatives to mention: `tryLock` with timeout + retry (breaks hold-and-wait), coarser single lock, or redesign to avoid multi-lock operations.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Two failure modes: lost updates (atomicity) and stale reads (visibility); every fix creates happens-before edges.\n- `synchronized` on a private lock covers 90%; `ReentrantLock` buys tryLock/fairness/conditions; atomics buy lock-free counters via CAS.\n- `volatile` = visibility flag only. `while`-loop around every `await/wait`.\n- Executors over raw threads; deadlock's cure is lock ordering.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- The moment you write a shared mutable field in an interview, say where its lock is. Narrating lock ownership per field is exactly what \"thread-safe design\" means.\n- \"I'll take a coarse lock first for correctness, then show how to shrink it\" — locks in the right order of concerns.\n- Immutable objects (Part 1) and confinement (\"only this thread touches it\") are thread-safety *without* locks — claim them explicitly when they apply.\n- Know cold: why DCL needs volatile; why `wait` needs while; why `HashMap` under concurrency is corruption, not just staleness.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. What exactly does `synchronized` guarantee?**\n> Mutual exclusion on the monitor — one thread in any guarded section per lock object — plus a memory contract: entering re-reads (acquire), exiting flushes (release), so everything thread A did before unlocking happens-before what B sees after locking. Both halves matter: without the visibility half, mutual exclusion would still let B read stale values afterward.\n\n**Q2. `synchronized` vs `ReentrantLock` — how do you choose?**\n> Default `synchronized`: less code, impossible to forget unlock, JVM-optimized (biased/adaptive locking). Upgrade when I need a capability: `tryLock(timeout)` for deadlock-resistant multi-lock code, fair queuing, multiple Conditions on one lock (bounded buffer's notFull/notEmpty), or interruptible waits. In interviews I say the default aloud and upgrade with a stated reason.\n\n**Q3. Why is `count++` broken even with `volatile`, and what fixes it?**\n> `volatile` makes the read see the latest value and the write visible — but `++` is still three steps, and two threads can read the same value and both write back +1: one increment lost. Fixes: `AtomicLong.incrementAndGet()` (CAS retry loop, lock-free), `LongAdder` under high contention (striped cells), or `synchronized` if the increment is part of a larger invariant that must be atomic as a whole.\n\n**Q4. Two threads deadlock in your parking system. Diagnose and fix.**\n> Suspect any place acquiring two locks: e.g., transfer-between-floors locking floorA then floorB while another thread does the reverse. Confirm via thread dump (`jstack` shows the cycle). Fixes in preference order: restructure so one lock suffices (lock the lot, not floors, if contention allows); global ordering (lock lower floor-id first); `tryLock` both with timeout, release and retry on failure. And prevention: keep lock scopes tiny and never call foreign/unknown code while holding a lock.\n\n**Q5. When are virtual threads relevant to an LLD answer?**\n> When my design has blocking I/O per request/task — payment calls, DB reads — and the interviewer pushes on \"10K concurrent bookings.\" Platform threads cap at thousands (1MB-ish stacks, scheduler load); virtual threads are cheap fibers multiplexed onto carriers, so thread-per-request stays valid at 100K+ with simple blocking code — no reactive rewrite. Caveats worth saying: they don't speed up CPU-bound work, and `synchronized` blocks that pin carriers (pre-24) should become ReentrantLock.\n"}, {"id": "03-concurrency/02-concurrent-collections", "path": "03-concurrency/02-concurrent-collections.md", "part": "Part 3 · Concurrency", "title": "3.2 Concurrent Collections & Coordination — The java.util.concurrent Toolkit", "md": "# 3.2 Concurrent Collections & Coordination — The java.util.concurrent Toolkit\n\n> Senior answers don't hand-roll locks — they pick the right j.u.c. tool and explain *why it's enough*. This chapter is your toolbox with the exact sentences that justify each pick.\n\n---\n\n## ConcurrentHashMap — the workhorse\n\n```java\nprivate final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Ticket> activeTickets = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\n// atomic check-then-act — the methods that make CHM more than \"thread-safe HashMap\":\nactiveTickets.putIfAbsent(ticket.id(), ticket);                 // insert if new, atomically\nactiveTickets.computeIfAbsent(userId, id -> new UserSession(id)); // get-or-create, once\nactiveTickets.compute(key, (k, v) -> v == null ? one() : v.increment()); // atomic update\nactiveTickets.merge(word, 1L, Long::sum);                       // atomic counter map\n```\n\n**Why not `synchronized(map)` around a HashMap?** CHM gives per-bin locking (reads mostly lock-free, writers contend only per bucket) → scales with cores; and its atomic compound ops remove the check-then-act races that external locking gets wrong. **The trap they test:** `if (!map.containsKey(k)) map.put(k, v)` is a race *even on CHM* — two thread-safe calls don't compose into one atomic action. Use `putIfAbsent`/`compute*`.\n\nNotes: no null keys/values; iterators are *weakly consistent* (no `ConcurrentModificationException`, may or may not see concurrent updates); `size()` is an estimate under concurrency — don't build invariants on it. `compute*` lambdas should be short and never touch other keys (deadlock risk).\n\n## BlockingQueue — producer-consumer, solved\n\n```java\nBlockingQueue<LogEvent> buffer = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(10_000);   // BOUNDED — always say why\n\n// producer                              // consumer (worker thread)\nbuffer.put(event);      // blocks full   LogEvent e = buffer.take();   // blocks empty\nbuffer.offer(event);    // false if full — or offer(e, 50, MILLIS) to shed load\n```\n\n| Implementation | Traits | Pick when |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ArrayBlockingQueue` | bounded, array, single lock | default bounded buffer |\n| `LinkedBlockingQueue` | optionally bounded, two locks (head/tail) | higher producer/consumer parallelism |\n| `PriorityBlockingQueue` | unbounded, heap order | task scheduling by priority/time |\n| `DelayQueue` | elements available after delay | timeouts, seat-hold expiry, retry queues |\n| `SynchronousQueue` | zero capacity, direct handoff | thread-pool handoff (cached pool uses it) |\n| `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` | *non*-blocking, CAS-based | when you never want waiting, just fast FIFO |\n\n**The sentence that scores:** \"bounded queue = backpressure — when consumers fall behind, producers block/fail fast instead of the process dying with OOM.\" Unbounded queues hide overload; bounded queues surface it as a policy decision.\n\n## CopyOnWriteArrayList — read-mostly listener lists\n\nEvery mutation copies the array; reads are lock-free on an immutable snapshot; iteration never throws CME. Perfect for observer lists (reads = every event, writes = rare register/unregister). Wrong for write-heavy data — O(n) copy per write.\n\n## Coordination primitives\n\n```java\n// CountDownLatch — one-shot \"wait until N things done\"\nCountDownLatch ready = new CountDownLatch(3);\n// three init tasks each call ready.countDown(); main thread: ready.await();\n\n// Semaphore — N permits for a limited resource\nSemaphore spots = new Semaphore(50, true);              // 50 parking spots, fair\nif (spots.tryAcquire(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS)) {            // park with timeout\n    try { doPark(v); } finally { /* release on UNpark, not here */ }\n}\n\n// CyclicBarrier — N threads rendezvous repeatedly (phased simulations)\nCyclicBarrier round = new CyclicBarrier(players, this::evaluateRound);\n```\n\nSemaphore *is* a counting inventory — for \"limited spots/permits/connections\", `Semaphore` alone may replace a lock-and-count design; say that. Latch = one-shot gate; Barrier = reusable meeting point; **Phaser** generalizes both (name-drop level).\n\n## Pattern: get-or-create without races\n\n```java\n// One session per user, exactly once, no locks visible:\nsessions.computeIfAbsent(userId, UserSession::new);\n```\n\nThis single line replaces the DCL-per-key dance and is the canonical answer to \"how do you lazily create per-key objects safely?\"\n\n## Pattern: per-key locking (lock striping)\n\nGlobal lock serializes everything; per-key locks let independent keys proceed:\n\n```java\nprivate final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Object> keyLocks = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n\npublic void bookSeat(String seatId, User u) {\n    Object lock = keyLocks.computeIfAbsent(seatId, k -> new Object());\n    synchronized (lock) {                       // only contends with SAME seat\n        Seat seat = seats.get(seatId);\n        if (!seat.isAvailable()) throw new SeatTakenException(seatId);\n        seat.hold(u);                           // check-then-act atomic per seat\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThis is the heart of BookMyShow/inventory answers (Ch 5.8). Alternative: `Striped<Lock>`-style fixed array of locks indexed by `hash(key) % N` — bounds memory when the key space is huge.\n\n## Which tool for which interview moment\n\n| Moment in the design | Reach for | The justifying sentence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Shared registry/cache/inventory map | `ConcurrentHashMap` + `compute*` | \"atomic per-key updates, no global lock\" |\n| Work handoff between components | bounded `BlockingQueue` | \"decouples rates; bound = backpressure\" |\n| Listener/observer lists | `CopyOnWriteArrayList` | \"read-mostly; iteration is snapshot-safe\" |\n| Limited identical resources | `Semaphore` | \"permits *are* the inventory\" |\n| Await N startup/completion events | `CountDownLatch` | \"one-shot gate\" |\n| Expiring holds/retries | `DelayQueue` / scheduled executor | \"time-ordered availability\" |\n| Unique IDs / metrics | `AtomicLong` / `LongAdder` | \"CAS, no lock\" |\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- CHM's `putIfAbsent`/`computeIfAbsent`/`merge` are the answer to almost every shared-map race; two safe calls ≠ one atomic action.\n- BlockingQueue deletes hand-rolled wait/notify; **always bound it** and own the overflow policy.\n- Semaphore = counting inventory; Latch = one-shot gate; COW list = observer storage.\n- Per-key locking (via CHM of locks or striping) = concurrency without global serialization — the inventory-booking superpower.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Swap vocabulary: never \"I'll synchronize the map\" — instead \"CHM with computeIfAbsent, because the check-and-create must be atomic.\"\n- When asked \"what if two users click at once?\" — per-key lock or CAS on the entity's state is your first move, not a service-wide lock.\n- Mention the overflow policy the moment you draw a queue: block, drop-oldest, or reject — choosing is the design.\n- `Collections.synchronizedMap` exists; knowing *why you're not using it* (compound-op races, global lock, manual sync for iteration) is the actual test.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why can `map.containsKey(k)` + `map.put(k,v)` race even on a ConcurrentHashMap?**\n> Each call is atomic, but between them another thread can insert the same key — check-then-act across two operations has no combined atomicity. Result: overwritten value or duplicate initialization. Fix: the compound atomic ops — `putIfAbsent`, or `computeIfAbsent` when creation is expensive (it runs the factory at most once per key). General lesson: thread safety doesn't compose; atomicity must cover the whole decision.\n\n**Q2. Design choice: BlockingQueue vs wait/notify for a producer-consumer buffer?**\n> BlockingQueue, always, unless explicitly asked to implement the primitive. It encapsulates the lock, both conditions, the while-loop recheck, and interruption handling — the four places hand-rolled versions go wrong. I choose `ArrayBlockingQueue(capacity)` for the bound and say the backpressure sentence. If the interviewer wants internals, I then write the two-Condition ReentrantLock version (Ch 3.3) — knowing both layers is the win.\n\n**Q3. What does \"weakly consistent iterator\" mean and why is it fine?**\n> CHM/CLQ iterators traverse a view that reflects the state at or after creation: they never throw ConcurrentModificationException, never return an element twice, but may miss concurrent inserts. Fine because concurrent readers by definition tolerate a moving target — a monitoring sweep over active tickets doesn't need a frozen snapshot. When I *do* need one, I copy deliberately (or use COW structures) and pay for it knowingly.\n\n**Q4. 100 parking spots, park() and unpark() from many gates — no explicit locks. Design it.**\n> `Semaphore(100)` embodies the count: `tryAcquire` at entry (fail/timeout = lot full), `release` at exit. Pair it with a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue<Spot>` of free spots: acquire permit → poll a spot (guaranteed non-null because permits ≤ spots) → on exit, offer the spot back then release the permit. Permit ordering makes the invariant airtight without a single synchronized block — and fairness mode prevents gate starvation.\n\n**Q5. When is LongAdder better than AtomicLong?**\n> Under heavy multi-thread contention on a counter that's written often and read rarely (metrics, hit counts). AtomicLong = one CAS target: threads retry-storm on the same cache line. LongAdder stripes across cells (threads hit different cells, `sum()` aggregates) — much higher write throughput, at the cost of an approximate/racy read and more memory. For an ID generator I still want AtomicLong: I need each value exactly once, not a statistical sum.\n"}, {"id": "03-concurrency/03-thread-safe-lld-patterns", "path": "03-concurrency/03-thread-safe-lld-patterns.md", "part": "Part 3 · Concurrency", "title": "3.3 Thread-Safe LLD — The Concurrency Moves Interviews Actually Test", "md": "# 3.3 Thread-Safe LLD — The Concurrency Moves Interviews Actually Test\n\n> Every LLD interview has a concurrency moment: \"what if two users book the same seat?\" This chapter is the playbook — five moves that answer 95% of those follow-ups, plus the classic implement-from-scratch exercises.\n\n---\n\n## Move 0 — The triage script (say this first)\n\nWhen concurrency comes up, don't dive into locks. Triage aloud:\n\n1. **\"What's shared and mutable?\"** — immutable objects and thread-confined state need nothing.\n2. **\"What's the invariant?\"** — \"a seat has ≤1 booking\", \"balance ≥ 0\", \"spot count matches assignments\".\n3. **\"What's the smallest scope that protects it?\"** — entity-level lock beats service-level lock.\n\nThis 3-question script *is* the senior answer; the mechanism you pick after it is almost secondary.\n\n## Move 1 — Make it immutable (no locks at all)\n\n`Money`, `Position`, `RouteSegment`, config — model as records, share freely. Mutation = replace the reference:\n\n```java\nprivate final AtomicReference<PricingConfig> config = new AtomicReference<>(initial);\npublic void updateConfig(PricingConfig next) { config.set(next); }   // readers never see a torn config\n```\n\n## Move 2 — Per-entity locking (the seat-booking answer)\n\n```java\npublic class Seat {\n    private final String id;\n    private SeatStatus status = AVAILABLE;\n    private String heldBy;\n\n    public synchronized boolean tryHold(String userId, Instant until) {  // lock on THE SEAT\n        if (status != AVAILABLE) return false;\n        status = HELD; heldBy = userId; holdExpiry = until;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public synchronized void confirm(String userId) {\n        if (status != HELD || !heldBy.equals(userId)) throw new IllegalStateException(\"hold lost\");\n        status = BOOKED;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nContention only per seat — two users fighting for A1 don't slow row Z. **Multi-seat booking** (the follow-up): acquire seat locks **in sorted ID order** to prevent deadlock, or `tryLock` all-or-release-all:\n\n```java\npublic boolean holdAll(List<Seat> seats, String user) {\n    seats.sort(comparing(Seat::id));                       // global order = no deadlock\n    List<Seat> held = new ArrayList<>();\n    for (Seat s : seats) {\n        if (!s.tryHold(user, expiry())) { held.forEach(h -> h.release(user)); return false; }\n        held.add(s);\n    }\n    return true;                                           // all-or-nothing\n}\n```\n\n## Move 3 — CAS / atomic state transition (optimistic)\n\nFor single-field state machines, skip locks entirely:\n\n```java\nprivate final AtomicReference<SpotState> state = new AtomicReference<>(FREE);\npublic boolean tryOccupy() { return state.compareAndSet(FREE, OCCUPIED); }  // exactly one winner\n```\n\nOptimistic vs pessimistic — the trade to narrate: CAS wins under low contention (no blocking, no lock overhead); locks win when the critical section is multi-step or contention is fierce (CAS retry storms). Databases mirror this: version-column optimistic locking vs `SELECT FOR UPDATE` — bridging to the DB layer is a bonus point.\n\n## Move 4 — Confine and queue (the actor move)\n\nInstead of locking shared state, give it an owner thread; everyone else sends messages:\n\n```java\n// Elevator: one thread owns the state machine; requests arrive via queue\nBlockingQueue<FloorRequest> requests = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();\nvoid controlLoop() {                                  // single thread → zero locks on elevator state\n    while (running) {\n        FloorRequest r = requests.poll(100, MILLISECONDS);\n        if (r != null) planStops(r);\n        step();                                       // move one floor, open doors, etc.\n    }\n}\n```\n\nPerfect for elevators, game engines, matching engines, and any \"brain\" with complex invariants. Sentence: \"I'll confine the scheduler state to one thread and make gates/callers producers — correctness by architecture, not by locks.\"\n\n## Move 5 — Time-based cleanup (holds that expire)\n\nHolds/reservations must expire or abandoned carts freeze inventory forever:\n\n```java\nscheduler.scheduleAtFixedRate(() ->\n    seats.values().stream().filter(Seat::holdExpired).forEach(Seat::releaseExpired),\n    5, 5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);\n// or: DelayQueue<SeatHold> — a dedicated thread takes expired holds and releases them\n```\n\nAlso handle expiry *lazily* at read time (`isAvailable()` checks expiry) so correctness doesn't depend on sweeper timing.\n\n---\n\n## Classic exercise 1 — Producer-consumer from scratch (know cold)\n\n```java\npublic class BoundedBuffer<T> {\n    private final Queue<T> q = new ArrayDeque<>();\n    private final int capacity;\n    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();\n    private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();\n    private final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();\n\n    public BoundedBuffer(int capacity) { this.capacity = capacity; }\n\n    public void put(T item) throws InterruptedException {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            while (q.size() == capacity) notFull.await();   // while: recheck after wakeup\n            q.add(item);\n            notEmpty.signal();\n        } finally { lock.unlock(); }\n    }\n\n    public T take() throws InterruptedException {\n        lock.lock();\n        try {\n            while (q.isEmpty()) notEmpty.await();\n            T item = q.poll();\n            notFull.signal();\n            return item;\n        } finally { lock.unlock(); }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nWhy two conditions: `notifyAll` on one monitor wakes producers *and* consumers (thundering herd); distinct conditions wake exactly the right party.\n\n## Classic exercise 2 — Thread-safe counter/ledger with invariant\n\n```java\npublic class Wallet {\n    private long balance;                                   // guarded by 'this'\n    public synchronized void transferTo(Wallet other, long amt) { /* see Ch 3.1 lock ordering! */ }\n    public synchronized boolean tryDebit(long amt) {\n        if (amt > balance) return false;                    // invariant check + mutation, one lock\n        balance -= amt; return true;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Classic exercise 3 — Rate limiter, thread-safe (full design: Ch 5.11)\n\nToken bucket where refill+consume must be atomic → `synchronized` on the bucket, or an atomic-state CAS loop.\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Q{\"Two users hit the<br/>same aggregate?\"}\n    Q -->|\"single field flips\"| CAS[\"CAS on AtomicReference\"]\n    Q -->|\"multi-step invariant\"| PK[\"Per-entity lock<br/>(sorted order if multiple)\"]\n    Q -->|\"complex brain, many events\"| ACT[\"Confine to one thread<br/>+ BlockingQueue\"]\n    Q -->|\"fixed pool of N resources\"| SEM[\"Semaphore permits\"]\n    CAS --> EXP[\"+ scheduled/lazy expiry<br/>for time-bound holds\"]\n    PK --> EXP\n```\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Triage first: shared? invariant? smallest scope? — then pick: immutable → CAS → per-entity lock → confinement, coarsening only when forced.\n- Multi-entity operations: sorted lock order or tryLock-all-or-release — never nested locks in arbitrary order.\n- Holds expire: sweeper + lazy check. Producer-consumer with two Conditions is a must-memorize.\n- \"Correctness by architecture\" (confinement/queues) usually beats \"correctness by locking.\"\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- The phrase \"the lock lives on the entity, so contention is per-seat, not per-show\" instantly upgrades your answer.\n- Pre-empt the double-booking question — raise it yourself while designing: \"two threads may reach here; tryHold is synchronized so exactly one wins.\"\n- If asked to *prove* safety, walk two threads through the interleaving line by line — that demonstration is worth more than the code.\n- Know the escalation path across scales: JVM lock → DB optimistic/pessimistic lock → distributed lock (Redis) — one sentence each, and say the single-JVM assumption out loud.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Two users click \"book seat A1\" at the same instant. Walk me through exactly why your code books it once.**\n> Both threads call `seat.tryHold(user, expiry)`, which is synchronized on that Seat instance. The JVM admits one — say thread A — which sees AVAILABLE, sets HELD/heldBy=A, returns true. B enters only after A exits; the monitor's happens-before guarantees B sees status=HELD, so B returns false and gets \"seat unavailable.\" The invariant holds because the check and the write are inside one critical section on the same lock the state belongs to.\n\n**Q2. Your seat-level locks work on one JVM. What breaks with two app servers?**\n> JVM monitors don't span processes — each server would happily hold the same seat. The invariant must move to a shared arbiter: the database (optimistic: `UPDATE seats SET status='HELD', version=v+1 WHERE id=? AND version=v` — 0 rows updated = lost race; or pessimistic `SELECT FOR UPDATE`), or a distributed lock/atomic op in Redis (`SET seat:A1 user NX PX 300000`). In-JVM locks remain as a cheap first filter, but the source of truth decides. Flagging this boundary unprompted is exactly what senior candidates do.\n\n**Q3. Why is a `while` loop around `await()` mandatory?**\n> Three reasons. Spurious wakeups: the JVM may wake a waiter with no signal. Stolen wakeups: between signal and reacquiring the lock, another thread may consume the state (queue slot filled again). Shared conditions: `notifyAll`/broadcast wakes threads whose predicate is still false. The while re-checks the predicate under the lock before proceeding — `if` turns all three into corruption (add to a full buffer).\n\n**Q4. Lock-free vs locked — how do you decide in an interview?**\n> Structure first, contention second. Single-variable transition (FREE→OCCUPIED, counter) → CAS/atomics: non-blocking, no deadlock, fastest at low contention. Multi-field invariant that must change together (status + holder + expiry) → lock; a CAS can't cover three writes atomically (unless I bundle state in one immutable object behind an AtomicReference — worth mentioning). Under extreme contention CAS retry-storms, so hot multi-writer counters → LongAdder, hot complex state → lock or confinement. And I only optimize past a plain lock when the interviewer signals scale matters.\n\n**Q5. How do you *test* thread-safety in a machine-coding round?**\n> A stress test with a latch-released thundering herd: N threads await one CountDownLatch, all call `bookSeat(\"A1\")`, then assert exactly one success and the final state is booked-once. Plus an invariant sweep under mixed load (park/unpark hammering: free+occupied always equals capacity). It can't prove absence of races, but it catches the naive check-then-act instantly, and writing it shows I know *what* property to assert — atomicity of the decision, integrity of the invariant.\n"}, {"id": "04-machine-coding/01-machine-coding-playbook", "path": "04-machine-coding/01-machine-coding-playbook.md", "part": "Part 4 · Machine Coding", "title": "4.1 The Machine Coding Playbook — 90 Minutes to Working, Clean Code", "md": "# 4.1 The Machine Coding Playbook — 90 Minutes to Working, Clean Code\n\n> Machine coding (Flipkart, Swiggy, Uber, Razorpay style): 90–120 minutes, a problem statement, and one demand — **working code that's demo-able and extensible**. Most failures are process failures, not knowledge failures. This is the process.\n\n---\n\n## The time budget (90-minute round)\n\n```mermaid\ngantt\n    dateFormat mm\n    axisFormat %M\n    section Plan\n    Read twice + clarify + scope     :00, 8m\n    Entities & interfaces on paper   :08, 7m\n    section Build\n    Skeleton — models, enums, repos  :15, 15m\n    Core flow 1 (the must-work path) :30, 20m\n    Core flow 2 + edge cases         :50, 15m\n    section Prove\n    Demo runner / tests              :65, 15m\n    Refactor + walkthrough prep      :80, 10m\n```\n\n**The one rule above all: a working core flow beats a complete skeleton of everything.** Interviewers extend working code; they can't evaluate ambitions.\n\n## Phase 1 — Scope like a professional (first 8 minutes)\n\nRead the statement **twice**. Then say the four sentences:\n\n1. *\"Here's what I understand the core flows to be: X, Y, Z — confirm?\"*\n2. *\"For 90 minutes I'll prioritize X and Y fully working; Z I'll stub behind an interface.\"*\n3. *\"I'll build in-memory storage behind repository interfaces — no DB, no UI, driven by a main/test runner?\"*\n4. *\"Any requirement you specifically want to see handled — concurrency? persistence?\"*\n\nWriting requirements as a checklist in a comment block keeps you honest and shows the interviewer a live scoreboard:\n\n```java\n/* REQUIREMENTS\n * [x] add expense, equal split        [x] show balances\n * [ ] percent split (stub ready)      [ ] simplify debts (stretch)\n */\n```\n\n## Phase 2 — Design on paper (7 minutes, no IDE)\n\n- Nouns → entities; verbs → services/methods (Ch 1.4).\n- Mark the **one or two variation axes** the statement hints at → those get interfaces (Strategy).\n- Decide package layout now:\n\n```\ncom.example.splitwise\n├── model/        // Entities & value objects: User, Group, Expense, Money\n├── repository/   // InMemoryExpenseRepository implements ExpenseRepository\n├── service/      // ExpenseService, BalanceService — business logic\n├── strategy/     // SplitStrategy: Equal, Exact, Percent\n├── exception/    // Domain exceptions\n└── App.java      // demo runner wiring everything (the composition root)\n```\n\nThis layout *is* an evaluation criterion — \"well-structured code\" literally means they can predict where things live.\n\n## Phase 3 — Build in the right order\n\n1. **Enums + value objects first** (2–5 min): `Money`, statuses, types — zero dependencies, everything uses them.\n2. **Entities with invariants**: constructors validate; no setters unless needed.\n3. **Repository interfaces + in-memory impls** (5 min): `Map<Id, T>` inside; unlocks services and tests.\n\n```java\npublic interface TicketRepository {\n    void save(Ticket t);\n    Optional<Ticket> byId(String id);\n}\npublic class InMemoryTicketRepository implements TicketRepository {\n    private final Map<String, Ticket> store = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    public void save(Ticket t) { store.put(t.id(), t); }\n    public Optional<Ticket> byId(String id) { return Optional.ofNullable(store.get(id)); }\n}\n```\n\n4. **The core service flow end-to-end** — resist perfecting; get `park() → Ticket` running.\n5. **Demo runner immediately after flow 1** — from now on you re-run after every change (a poor man's test suite).\n6. Then flow 2, edge cases, and the strategy variants.\n\n## Phase 4 — Edge cases & errors (what separates good from great)\n\nDomain exceptions, not return-nulls, not generic RuntimeException:\n\n```java\npublic class SeatUnavailableException extends RuntimeException {\n    public SeatUnavailableException(String seatId) { super(\"Seat already booked: \" + seatId); }\n}\n```\n\nStandard edge checklist (run it against any problem): invalid input · not found · duplicate action (park same car twice, pay same bill twice — **idempotency**) · capacity full · thing expired · operation on wrong state (cancel a completed order). Handling *duplicate action* explicitly is rare among candidates and heavily noticed.\n\n## Phase 5 — Prove it works\n\nA `main` that tells the problem's story beats silent tests in an interview demo:\n\n```java\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    ParkingLot lot = ParkingLot.init(2 /*floors*/, 10 /*spots each*/);\n    Ticket t1 = lot.park(new Car(\"KA-01-1234\"));\n    System.out.println(\"Parked: \" + t1);\n    Receipt r = lot.unpark(t1.id());                 // happy path\n    System.out.println(\"Fee: \" + r.fee());\n    try { lot.unpark(t1.id()); }                     // edge: double exit\n    catch (TicketNotActiveException e) { System.out.println(\"OK: \" + e.getMessage()); }\n}\n```\n\nIf JUnit is available, 3–5 tests on the money paths (fee calculation, split math, double-booking) — pure-logic classes are the best test targets per minute. Say: *\"repositories are interfaces precisely so these tests need no infra.\"*\n\n## The walkthrough (last 5 minutes — rehearse the shape)\n\n1. Requirements coverage: \"flows X, Y done; Z stubbed here.\"\n2. One-breath architecture: \"models → repositories → services; variation behind these two strategies.\"\n3. A live demo run.\n4. **Self-review:** \"with more time: split BalanceService, add tests for percent-split rounding, per-group locking.\" Naming your own debts preempts the criticism and reads as senior.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Budget: ~15 plan / ~50 build / ~25 prove-and-polish. Working core flow first, always.\n- Standard package layout + repository interfaces + composition root = \"well-structured\" by definition.\n- Build order: enums/VOs → entities → repos → one full flow → demo runner → the rest.\n- Domain exceptions + idempotency handling + a story-telling main = the visible quality signals.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Type fast on boilerplate, slow on invariants — reviewers skim getters but read `tryHold()` letter by letter.\n- Stuck 5+ minutes on design? Choose the simpler option, add `// TODO: revisit if <condition>`, move on — momentum outscores elegance.\n- Never gold-plate flow 1 while flow 2 is missing; two working flows at 7/10 polish beat one at 10/10.\n- Keep a rehearsed personal template: Money class, repository pattern, exception base, demo-main shape. Muscle memory buys you 15 minutes.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. What do machine-coding evaluators actually score?**\n> Roughly in order: (1) does the core flow *run correctly*, including edge cases; (2) readability/structure — naming, packages, small methods; (3) extensibility — are the stated variation axes behind interfaces; (4) OO modeling — invariants in entities, not smeared in services; (5) demo/test quality. Cleverness and framework knowledge score near zero. That ranking dictates the time budget: correctness first, structure as you go, patterns only where variation is stated.\n\n**Q2. In-memory storage — how do you keep it credible?**\n> Behind an interface: services depend on `TicketRepository`, and `InMemoryTicketRepository` is one implementation — so \"add a real DB\" is a new class, not a rewrite. I keep repository methods query-shaped (`findActiveByVehicle`) rather than leaking `Map` semantics, use ConcurrentHashMap so the concurrency story holds, and say the sentence: \"persistence is a port; today's adapter is a map.\"\n\n**Q3. You're running out of time with a feature half-done. What do you do?**\n> Cut to a clean seam, not mid-function: make the completed part demo-able, stub the remainder behind its interface with a clear exception (`UnsupportedOperationException(\"percent split — interface ready, see PercentSplitStrategy\")`), and update the requirements checklist. Then in the walkthrough I *lead* with the cut and its restoration path. An honest, well-shaped 80% consistently outscores a broken 100%.\n\n**Q4. Should you write tests in a machine coding round?**\n> A demo-main is non-negotiable — it's how the interviewer sees it work. Formal tests: yes if the round expects them or logic is math-heavy (splits, fees, rounding) — 3–5 focused JUnit tests on pure logic classes are cheap because my repos are injectable fakes. What I avoid: mocking frameworks, exhaustive suites, and testing getters — the ROI question is always \"which test catches the bug the interviewer will probe?\" That's fees, splits, and double-actions.\n\n**Q5. How do you handle an interviewer changing requirements at minute 60?**\n> That's the test of the seams, and I say so cheerfully. Route the change: new variant of an existing axis → new strategy class, minutes (\"weekend pricing\" = new FeeStrategy). New axis → add the interface now, implement the one variant needed, note the old code path preserved. Genuinely breaking change → negotiate scope: \"I can restructure X in the remaining time, or stub it and explain the design — which is more useful to you?\" The worst response is silently absorbing it into if-statements.\n"}, {"id": "04-machine-coding/02-clean-code-for-lld", "path": "04-machine-coding/02-clean-code-for-lld.md", "part": "Part 4 · Machine Coding", "title": "4.2 Clean Code & API Design for LLD — What Reviewers Actually Read", "md": "# 4.2 Clean Code & API Design for LLD — What Reviewers Actually Read\n\n> Two candidates produce the same design; one gets \"strong hire\". The delta is almost always code-level: names, method size, error handling, and API shape. These are cheap points — collect all of them.\n\n---\n\n## Naming — the 80/20 of readability\n\n| Smell | Fix | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ParkingLotManager`, `DataHelper` | `SpotAllocator`, `FeeCalculator` | -er/-or names tied to one job |\n| `process()`, `handle()`, `doStuff()` | `assignSpot()`, `settleBalance()` | verb = contract |\n| `list1`, `temp`, `flag` | `availableSpots`, `isWithinGrace` | reader never decodes |\n| `getUser()` that creates a user | `findOrCreateUser()` | least astonishment |\n| boolean param `book(seat, true)` | `book(seat, BookingType.PREMIUM)` | call sites self-document |\n\nTest: read the call site aloud — `lot.park(car)` reads as English; `mgr.process(obj, true, 2)` doesn't.\n\n## Methods — small, one level of abstraction\n\n```java\n// ❌ 40-line method mixing policy and plumbing        // ✅ the method reads as its own outline\npublic Receipt unpark(String ticketId) {\n    Ticket t = activeTicketOrThrow(ticketId);\n    Duration stay = clock.durationSince(t.entryTime());\n    Money fee = feePolicy.feeFor(t.vehicleType(), stay);\n    releaseSpot(t.spotId());\n    return receipts.issue(t, fee);\n}\n```\n\nRules of thumb: ≤ 15 lines; one level of abstraction per method (don't mix `stream().filter()` plumbing with business decisions); early-return guard clauses instead of arrow-shaped nesting:\n\n```java\npublic void cancel(Booking b) {\n    if (b.status() == CANCELLED) return;                       // idempotent, flat\n    if (b.status() == COMPLETED) throw new IllegalStateException(\"completed bookings can't cancel\");\n    b.markCancelled();\n    refunds.issue(b);\n}\n```\n\n## Errors — a small taxonomy, used consistently\n\n- **Domain exceptions** extend `RuntimeException`, named by business meaning: `SeatUnavailableException`, `InsufficientBalanceException`, `TicketNotFoundException`. Carry context in the message (ids, amounts).\n- **`Optional<T>`** for *expected* absence (`findById`); exceptions for *violated expectations* (`getActiveTicket` when the flow guarantees one). Never `Optional` fields/params; never return null collections — return empty.\n- **Validate at the boundary** (constructor/service entry), then trust internally — no re-checking nulls five layers deep.\n\n```java\npublic Expense(String paidBy, Money amount, List<Split> splits) {\n    this.paidBy = Objects.requireNonNull(paidBy);\n    if (amount.isNegativeOrZero()) throw new InvalidExpenseException(\"amount must be positive\");\n    if (!Split.sumsTo(splits, amount)) throw new InvalidExpenseException(\"splits must equal amount\");\n    this.amount = amount; this.splits = List.copyOf(splits);   // defensive copy\n}\n```\n\nAn object that *cannot be constructed invalid* deletes whole categories of downstream checks — say \"invariants enforced at construction\" while writing it.\n\n## API shape — design the calls before the classes\n\nWrite the dream client code first, then make it compile:\n\n```java\nSplitwiseService s = SplitwiseService.create();\nGroup trip = s.createGroup(\"Goa\", alice, bob, carol);\ns.addExpense(trip, alice.paid(rupees(3000)), Split.equally());\ns.addExpense(trip, bob.paid(rupees(1200)), Split.percent(alice, 50, bob, 30, carol, 20));\nList<Settlement> due = s.settlementsFor(trip);\n```\n\nPrinciples: **make illegal states unrepresentable** (an `enum` + sealed types instead of string status; `Split.percent(...)` that validates 100% at creation); accept the most abstract type (`Collection` in, `List` out); return immutable views; keep services stateless where possible (state lives in entities/repos).\n\n## Comments & formatting\n\n- Comments say **why**, never what: `// LOOK algorithm: keep direction until no requests ahead` ✓; `// increment i` ✗.\n- Dead code and commented-out blocks: delete — they read as indecision.\n- Consistent formatting > any particular style. In an interview, let the IDE format; never hand-align.\n\n## The senior-signal checklist (drop these in naturally)\n\n- `final` fields everywhere possible; `List.copyOf` on the way in and out.\n- A `Clock` injected instead of `Instant.now()` scattered — testable time (\"I can unit-test the grace period\").\n- IDs as value types or at least dedicated `String` wrappers created in one `IdGenerator` — not `UUID.randomUUID()` sprinkled.\n- `Objects.requireNonNull` in constructors; `@Override` always; enums over int/string constants.\n- Streams where they clarify (mapping/filtering), loops where they don't (complex mutation, early exit).\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Names carry the design: -er/-or classes with one job, verb methods, no Manager/Helper/Util.\n- Small methods at one abstraction level, guard clauses, idempotent operations.\n- Domain exceptions + Optional-for-absence + construct-valid-or-throw = the whole error story.\n- Design the client call first; make illegal states unrepresentable; inject the Clock.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- When you catch yourself writing a bad name, rename aloud — visible taste beats silent perfection.\n- One defensive copy with the sentence \"so callers can't mutate my internals\" scores more than ten getters.\n- If an interviewer reads any method of yours without asking \"what does this do?\", you're winning the round.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Checked vs unchecked exceptions in your interview code — and why?**\n> Unchecked domain exceptions. Checked exceptions pollute every intermediate signature, and in a 90-minute round that friction buys nothing: callers of `bookSeat` can't recover from `SeatUnavailableException` by catching it mid-stack — it should fly to the boundary that reports it. I reserve checked-style thinking for genuinely recoverable, expected outcomes — and for those I prefer return types: `Optional`, or a small `Result`/sealed outcome when the caller must branch (hold-succeeded vs seat-taken vs payment-declined).\n\n**Q2. Where do you draw the line between an anemic model and a fat entity?**\n> Rule: behavior lives with the data it guards. Entity owns its invariants and state transitions — `seat.tryHold()`, `wallet.debit()`, `order.cancel()` — because that's the only way they're enforced everywhere. Services own orchestration across entities — booking = hold seats + charge payment + issue ticket. Anemic = entities as field-bags with services doing `seat.setStatus(HELD)` (invariants optional, scattered). Fat = entity calling repositories/gateways (untestable, coupled). The line: entities never do I/O.\n\n**Q3. Why inject a Clock — isn't that over-engineering for an interview?**\n> It's one constructor parameter, and it converts my most bug-prone logic — fees by duration, hold expiry, grace periods — from untestable to trivially testable: `clock.fixed(...)`, park, advance, assert fee. Without it, testing \"2h 5m costs ₹60\" means sleeping or reflection hacks. It also centralizes time zone/now decisions. This is the rare abstraction whose cost is one line and whose payoff appears within the same interview when I demo the fee test.\n\n**Q4. `Optional` return vs throwing — give your exact rule.**\n> If absence is a *normal outcome the caller decides about* → `Optional`: `findTicket(id)` — caller may create, report 404, or retry. If absence means *the system broke its own assumption* → throw: inside `unpark()`, the ticket must exist because the flow validated it — `orElseThrow(TicketNotFoundException::new)` documents that assumption and fails loud. Corollaries: never `Optional.get()` bare, never Optional parameters (overload instead), and empty collections instead of Optional<List>.\n\n**Q5. The interviewer says your code is \"too many small classes.\" Defend or concede?**\n> First check honestly: are any classes ceremony — an interface with one forever-implementation, a layer that only forwards? Concede those; inline them live (\"you're right, `TicketValidator` can be a private method until validation grows\"). Defend the ones carrying a real axis: \"FeeStrategy exists because you said pricing varies; folding it back couples pricing changes to tested allocation code.\" The meta-answer that wins: class count isn't the metric — reasons-to-change per class is; I can defend each class's reason or delete it.\n"}, {"id": "04-machine-coding/03-object-modeling", "path": "04-machine-coding/03-object-modeling.md", "part": "Part 4 · Machine Coding", "title": "4.3 Object Modeling — Entities, Value Objects, Repositories & IDs", "md": "# 4.3 Object Modeling — Entities, Value Objects, Repositories & IDs\n\n> Before patterns, before concurrency, an interviewer watches one thing: **can you turn a messy problem statement into the right objects?** This chapter is the modeling method — lightweight DDD, interview-sized.\n\n---\n\n## The two kinds of objects (decide for every noun)\n\n| | **Entity** | **Value Object** |\n|---|---|---|\n| Identity | Has an ID; identity persists through change | *Is* its attributes; no ID |\n| Equality | By ID | By value (`equals` on all fields) |\n| Mutability | Mutable state machine (status, balance) | **Immutable** — \"change\" = new instance |\n| Examples | `User`, `Order`, `Ticket`, `Seat`, `Trip` | `Money`, `Address`, `Position`, `TimeRange`, `SeatId` |\n| Java shape | class, private fields, behavior methods | `record` |\n\n**Why the split matters:** value objects are free of lifecycle/concurrency/persistence questions — every noun you push into the VO column simplifies the design. Say: *\"Money, Position, TimeRange — I'll model these as immutable records; only Order and Seat carry identity and state.\"*\n\n```java\npublic record TimeRange(Instant start, Instant end) {\n    public TimeRange {                                       // compact constructor = validation\n        if (!start.isBefore(end)) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"start must precede end\");\n    }\n    public boolean overlaps(TimeRange other) {\n        return start.isBefore(other.end) && other.start.isBefore(end);\n    }   // behavior on the VO — this one method IS the meeting-scheduler kata\n}\n```\n\n## Modeling method — nouns, verbs, invariants, states\n\n1. **Nouns** → candidate objects; sort each into entity / VO / *attribute of another* (many nouns don't deserve classes — \"license plate\" is a String field, maybe a tiny VO, not an entity).\n2. **Verbs** → behavior; attach each verb to the object *whose data it uses* (tell-don't-ask). `calculateFee` uses ticket times + rates → `FeePolicy.feeFor(ticket)`, not a method on `ParkingLot`.\n3. **Invariants** → constructor/method guards on whichever object owns them (\"splits sum to total\" lives in `Expense`).\n4. **States** → if an entity has a lifecycle, draw it *now* and encode legal transitions:\n\n```java\npublic enum OrderStatus { CREATED, PAID, SHIPPED, DELIVERED, CANCELLED;\n    private static final Map<OrderStatus, Set<OrderStatus>> LEGAL = Map.of(\n        CREATED, Set.of(PAID, CANCELLED),\n        PAID, Set.of(SHIPPED, CANCELLED),\n        SHIPPED, Set.of(DELIVERED));\n    public boolean canGoTo(OrderStatus next) { return LEGAL.getOrDefault(this, Set.of()).contains(next); }\n}\n// entity: public void transitionTo(OrderStatus next) {\n//     if (!status.canGoTo(next)) throw new IllegalTransitionException(status, next); ... }\n```\n\nAn explicit transition table (vs scattered ifs) is one of the highest-value 10-line blocks you can write in any interview.\n\n## Aggregates — who is allowed to touch whom\n\nAn **aggregate** = a cluster of objects treated as one consistency unit, entered only through its **root**. `ParkingLot → Floors → Spots`: outsiders call `lot.park(vehicle)`, never `floor3.spot12.assign()` directly.\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class ParkingLot {\n        <<aggregate root>>\n        +park(Vehicle) Ticket\n        +unpark(String ticketId) Receipt\n    }\n    ParkingLot \"1\" *-- \"n\" Floor\n    Floor \"1\" *-- \"m\" Spot\n    class Ticket { <<entity, separate aggregate>> }\n    ParkingLot ..> Ticket : issues\n```\n\nPayoffs to narrate: invariants (\"free-spot count matches assignments\") have **one enforcement point**; the root is the natural lock scope (Ch 3.3); the API surface shrinks. Keep aggregates small — `Ticket` is its *own* aggregate referenced by ID, not owned by the lot, so ticket history doesn't bloat the lot's consistency boundary.\n\n## Repositories — collections you pretend are infinite\n\nOne repository **per aggregate root** (a `SpotRepository` is a smell — spots come with the lot). Interface speaks the domain; implementation is a map today, a DB tomorrow:\n\n```java\npublic interface BookingRepository {\n    void save(Booking b);\n    Optional<Booking> byId(BookingId id);\n    List<Booking> activeForShow(ShowId show);      // query methods, domain-named\n}\n```\n\nKeep queries *needed by your flows* only — don't speculatively build `findAllByEverySortOrder`.\n\n## IDs — small decisions that show polish\n\n```java\npublic record BookingId(String value) {\n    public static BookingId newId() { return new BookingId(\"B-\" + UUID.randomUUID()); }\n}\n```\n\n- Typed IDs (`BookingId`, not `String`) make `cancel(userId, bookingId)` un-swappable — cheap compile-time safety; mention it even if you use raw Strings under time pressure.\n- Generation: `UUID` for uniqueness-without-coordination; `AtomicLong` sequence for readable demo output; never DB-auto-increment semantics in an in-memory round.\n- Never expose mutable internal collections as the \"ID space\" — hand out IDs, resolve via repository.\n\n## Worked mini-example — modeling \"Library Management\" in 4 minutes\n\nNouns: library, book, **copy** (the crucial catch: `Book` = title/ISBN metadata — a VO-ish catalog entry; `BookCopy` = the physical entity with a barcode and LOAN state), member, loan, fine.\n- Entities: `BookCopy(status: AVAILABLE/LOANED/LOST)`, `Member`, `Loan(dueDate, returnedAt)`.\n- VOs: `Isbn`, `Money`, `LoanPeriod`.\n- Aggregate roots: `Member` (owns active loans count invariant ≤ 5), `BookCopy`; `Loan` links them by ID.\n- Verbs: `library.checkout(member, copy) → Loan` (facade/service orchestrating both roots), `loan.returnBook(clock) → Optional<Fine>`.\n\nThe Book-vs-BookCopy split is the archetype of a modeling insight: **when one noun hides two lifecycles, split it.** (Same move: `Show` vs `Seat` vs `Booking` in BookMyShow; `Movie` vs `Screening`.)\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Every noun: entity (ID + lifecycle), value object (immutable record), or mere field. Push hard toward VO.\n- Attach verbs to the data they use; encode invariants in constructors and a transition table.\n- Aggregate root = single entry point + consistency + lock scope; one repository per root.\n- Watch for one-noun-two-lifecycles (Book/BookCopy) — spotting it is a hire signal by itself.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Narrate the sorting: \"ticket — entity; money — value object; license plate — just a field.\" Interviewers hear method, not guessing.\n- If two entities keep needing each other's internals, you've drawn the aggregate boundary wrong — merge or introduce a service.\n- The transition-table enum takes 3 minutes and preempts every \"can a cancelled order ship?\" question.\n- Reference other aggregates **by ID** (`Loan` holds `MemberId`), not object references — it prevents the everything-touches-everything object graph.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Entity vs value object — why does the distinction change your code?**\n> Identity changes equality (ID vs all-fields), mutability (state machine vs immutable), and infrastructure (entities need repositories, locks, lifecycle; VOs need nothing). Concretely: making `Money` a record deletes defensive-copy bugs, makes it a safe map key, and thread-safe for free; making `Seat` an entity gives its status transitions one home I can lock. Misclassifying — a mutable Money, or an ID-less Seat compared by position — creates exactly the double-booking and lost-update bugs interviews probe for.\n\n**Q2. How do you decide aggregate boundaries?**\n> By invariants that must be transactionally true: whatever must be consistent *together* lives under one root. \"Free spots = capacity − assignments\" spans floors and spots → they share the ParkingLot root. \"Ticket paid-ness\" doesn't affect that count → Ticket is its own aggregate, referenced by ID. Then I sanity-check size: a giant root serializes everything behind one lock (Ch 3.3), so anything not needed for the invariant gets pushed out. Boundaries = invariants first, contention second.\n\n**Q3. Where does logic go when it spans two aggregates?**\n> A domain service: `CheckoutService.checkout(member, copy)` orchestrates `member.canBorrow()` and `copy.loan()` and creates the `Loan`. Neither entity should reach into the other (coupling, and no natural lock order); the service sequences the calls, owns cross-aggregate rules (\"max 5 loans\"), and handles partial failure (copy loaned but member at limit → release). Entities keep their own invariants; services keep the choreography — that's the whole layering.\n\n**Q4. Why one repository per aggregate root rather than per class?**\n> Because the root is the consistency unit: loading/saving spots independently of their lot invites invariant-breaking writes from the side. Per-root repositories keep every mutation flowing through root methods that enforce the rules. It also shrinks the persistence surface (fewer interfaces, coherent transactions later) and matches how you'd actually store it — the lot document/row owns its spots. A repository per class is how anemic designs leak: it hands out scalpel access to internals.\n\n**Q5. Your Book/BookCopy split — how do you find such splits under time pressure?**\n> Two probes. Lifecycle probe: ask of each noun \"can two instances of this differ in state while being 'the same thing'?\" — two copies of the same ISBN, one loaned one lost → split. Counting probe: if a requirement counts something (\"3 copies available\") that another requirement treats as one (\"the book's author\"), there are two concepts. The same probes generate Show/Seat/Booking, Flight/FlightInstance, Product/SKU/InventoryItem. When a design feels tangled, it's very often one noun doing two jobs.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/01-parking-lot", "path": "05-case-studies/01-parking-lot.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.1 Design a Parking Lot", "md": "# 5.1 Design a Parking Lot\n\n> The \"hello world\" of LLD — asked everywhere because in 45 minutes it exercises modeling, enums, Strategy, Singleton, concurrency, and extensibility. Master this walkthrough as your **template for every other problem**.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (4 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Let me confirm scope: multiple floors; spot sizes for bike/car/truck; entry issues a ticket, exit computes a fee; multiple entry/exit gates operating concurrently. Do you want display boards and payment integration, or park/unpark with fees as the core?\"*\n\n**Functional:** park → ticket; unpark → fee + receipt; spot sizing rules (bike fits any spot? truck needs large); query availability per floor.\n**Non-functional (yes, LLD has them):** thread-safe against concurrent gates; fee rules will change (hint: Strategy); extensible to EV spots, reservations. **Out of scope (say it):** payments processing, license-plate recognition, persistence.\n\n## Step 2 — Entities & relationships (3 min)\n\nNouns → `ParkingLot` (aggregate root), `Floor`, `Spot` (entity, state), `Vehicle` (VO-ish input), `Ticket` (entity, own aggregate), `Gate`. Verbs → `park`, `unpark`, `assignSpot`, `calculateFee`.\nSorting: `VehicleType`, `SpotType`, `Money` — enums/VOs. `Ticket` referenced by ID, not owned by floors.\n\n## Step 3 — Class diagram (draw before code)\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class ParkingLot {\n        -List~Floor~ floors\n        -SpotAssignmentStrategy assignment\n        -FeePolicy feePolicy\n        -TicketRepository tickets\n        +park(Vehicle) Ticket\n        +unpark(String ticketId) Receipt\n    }\n    class Floor {\n        -int number\n        -Map~SpotType, List~Spot~~ spots\n        +findFree(SpotType) Optional~Spot~\n    }\n    class Spot {\n        -SpotId id\n        -SpotType type\n        -SpotStatus status\n        +tryAssign(Vehicle) boolean\n        +release() void\n    }\n    class SpotAssignmentStrategy { <<interface>> +findSpot(List~Floor~, Vehicle) Optional~Spot~ }\n    class FeePolicy { <<interface>> +feeFor(VehicleType, Duration) Money }\n    class Ticket {\n        -String id\n        -SpotId spotId\n        -String plate\n        -Instant entryAt\n        -TicketStatus status\n    }\n    ParkingLot \"1\" *-- \"n\" Floor\n    Floor \"1\" *-- \"m\" Spot\n    ParkingLot o-- SpotAssignmentStrategy\n    ParkingLot o-- FeePolicy\n    ParkingLot ..> Ticket : issues\n    SpotAssignmentStrategy <|.. NearestFirstStrategy\n    FeePolicy <|.. HourlyFeePolicy\n```\n\n**Say while drawing:** *\"Two variation axes — how spots are chosen and how fees are computed — go behind interfaces. Everything enters through `ParkingLot`, which makes it my consistency and locking boundary.\"*\n\n## Step 4 — Key decisions & patterns\n\n| Decision | Choice | Why (say this) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Spot fitting | `SpotType` enum + `fits(VehicleType)` | bike→any, car→medium/large, truck→large: data, not if-chains |\n| Assignment | **Strategy** (`NearestFirst`, `RandomEvenWear`) | stated axis of change |\n| Fees | **Strategy/Policy** (`HourlyFeePolicy` + grace period) | pricing always changes |\n| Vehicle creation | **Factory** from `VehicleType` | one place knows concretes |\n| Lot instance | Singleton-ish via composition root | \"one lot\" — but injected, not `getInstance()` everywhere |\n| Ticket state | `ACTIVE → PAID` enum transition | double-exit becomes impossible |\n\n## Step 5 — Core Java (the code that matters)\n\n```java\npublic enum SpotType {\n    SMALL, MEDIUM, LARGE;\n    public boolean fits(VehicleType v) {\n        return switch (v) {\n            case BIKE  -> true;\n            case CAR   -> this != SMALL;\n            case TRUCK -> this == LARGE;\n        };\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Spot {\n    private final SpotId id; private final SpotType type;\n    private SpotStatus status = SpotStatus.FREE;\n\n    public synchronized boolean tryAssign(Vehicle v) {          // per-spot lock: check+act atomic\n        if (status != SpotStatus.FREE || !type.fits(v.type())) return false;\n        status = SpotStatus.OCCUPIED;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public synchronized void release() { status = SpotStatus.FREE; }\n}\n\npublic class ParkingLot {\n    private final List<Floor> floors;\n    private final SpotAssignmentStrategy assignment;\n    private final FeePolicy feePolicy;\n    private final TicketRepository tickets;\n    private final Clock clock;\n\n    public Ticket park(Vehicle v) {\n        // strategy proposes; spot's own lock disposes — loop handles races between gates\n        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {\n            Optional<Spot> candidate = assignment.findSpot(floors, v);\n            if (candidate.isEmpty()) throw new LotFullException(v.type());\n            Spot spot = candidate.get();\n            if (spot.tryAssign(v)) {                            // atomic claim\n                Ticket t = Ticket.issue(spot.id(), v.plate(), clock.instant());\n                tickets.save(t);\n                return t;\n            }                                                    // lost the race → re-ask strategy\n        }\n        throw new LotFullException(v.type());\n    }\n\n    public Receipt unpark(String ticketId) {\n        Ticket t = tickets.byId(ticketId).orElseThrow(() -> new TicketNotFoundException(ticketId));\n        t.markPaid();                                           // throws if not ACTIVE (double exit)\n        Money fee = feePolicy.feeFor(t.vehicleType(), Duration.between(t.entryAt(), clock.instant()));\n        spotById(t.spotId()).release();\n        return new Receipt(t, fee);\n    }\n}\n\npublic class HourlyFeePolicy implements FeePolicy {\n    private final Map<VehicleType, Money> ratePerHour;\n    private final Duration grace = Duration.ofMinutes(15);\n    public Money feeFor(VehicleType type, Duration stay) {\n        if (stay.compareTo(grace) <= 0) return Money.zero();\n        long hours = Math.max(1, (long) Math.ceil(stay.toMinutes() / 60.0));\n        return ratePerHour.get(type).times(hours);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Step 6 — Concurrency (they *will* ask)\n\n**Q: two gates, one last free spot?** Both gates' strategies may propose the same spot; `tryAssign` is synchronized on the spot, so exactly one wins; the loser loops and re-asks the strategy (which now skips the occupied spot) — optimistic retry, no global lock. **Say:** *\"contention is per-spot; gates parking on different floors never wait on each other.\"*\nFree-spot counts per floor: `AtomicInteger` decrement on assign / increment on release — display boards read it without locks (Observer pattern for live boards).\n\n## Step 7 — Extensibility follow-ups (rehearse one-liners)\n\n- **EV charging spots:** new `SpotType.EV_*` + `fits` logic; `ChargingSession` attached to ticket — no structural change.\n- **Reservations:** `Spot` gains `RESERVED` state with `TimeRange`; assignment strategy filters by availability window.\n- **Dynamic pricing:** new `FeePolicy` (surge multiplier by occupancy) — the seam pays off.\n- **Multiple lots / valet / handicapped-near-elevator:** strategy per lot config; `NearElevatorStrategy` for accessible spots.\n- **Persistence:** repositories already interfaces → JPA impls; the domain doesn't change.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nScope confirmation → entity sort (root/entities/VOs) → class diagram with two strategy seams → `SpotType.fits` data-driven sizing → park flow with per-spot atomic claim + retry → fee policy with grace period → ticket state machine blocking double-exit → concurrency narration (per-spot locks, atomic counters) → two extensibility one-liners.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- The propose/claim/retry loop is the single most reusable trick in LLD — it reappears in seat booking, driver matching, and inventory.\n- Volunteer the concurrency story *before* being asked; parking lot without thread-safety is a junior answer.\n- Keep `Vehicle` dumb (type + plate). Candidates who give vehicles behavior (\"vehicle.parkItself()\") tangle the model.\n- If time is short, skip Floor and flat-list the spots — say \"floors are a grouping detail; the design is unchanged.\"\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why is the assignment strategy separate from the spot's own locking?**\n> Separation of *policy* from *safety*. The strategy is pure selection logic — testable, swappable (nearest, even-wear, accessible-first) — and holds no locks, so it can scan freely. Safety lives in `tryAssign`, the one place where check-and-act must be atomic. The retry loop stitches them: stale proposals are corrected by re-proposing, not by locking the whole scan. A strategy that locked floors while scanning would serialize all gates and still race between scan and assign.\n\n**Q2. A truck needs two adjacent medium spots. How does the design absorb that?**\n> Assignment returns a `SpotAllocation` (1..n spots) instead of one spot; a new `AdjacentPairStrategy` finds neighbors (spots keep row/index for adjacency). Claiming multiple spots = multi-lock: sort by SpotId, `tryAssign` each, release-all on any failure (all-or-nothing, no deadlock by ordering — Ch 3.3). Ticket stores the allocation. The seam absorbs the feature; only the strategy and the claim helper are new.\n\n**Q3. Where would Observer fit here?**\n> Display boards and analytics: `ParkingLot` publishes `SpotOccupied`/`SpotReleased` events; `FloorDisplayBoard` updates counts, `OccupancyAnalytics` aggregates, a future `SurgePricingPolicy` could subscribe too. Publisher knows only the listener interface; adding a consumer touches nothing. I'd fire events after state change commits, and isolate listener exceptions so a broken board can't fail parking.\n\n**Q4. How do you test this in the round?**\n> Three tests carry the grade: (1) fee math with a fixed Clock — park, advance 2h05m, assert rate×3 with grace applied; (2) double-exit — unpark twice, second throws `TicketNotActiveException`; (3) the race — N threads, one CountDownLatch, one free spot, assert exactly one ticket issued. All run without infra because repositories and Clock are injected.\n\n**Q5. Interviewer: \"Make it a paid-subscription lot with monthly passes.\"**\n> Model a `Pass` entity (member, vehicle plates, validity `TimeRange`, allowed spot types) and resolve at entry: `park` consults a `PassRegistry`; pass-holders get a `PassTicket` (zero fee at exit) and possibly a reserved-section strategy. Fee seam handles it naturally — `FeePolicy` returns zero for pass tickets, or better, a `PassAwareFeePolicy` decorator wraps the hourly policy. Billing of the subscription itself is a separate bounded context — out of the lot's aggregate, and I'd say so.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/02-elevator-system", "path": "05-case-studies/02-elevator-system.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.2 Design an Elevator System", "md": "# 5.2 Design an Elevator System\n\n> The scheduling-heavy classic. Tests: State pattern, Strategy (scheduling algorithms), the confinement concurrency model, and whether you can keep a simulation loop clean. Uber, Amazon, and Microsoft favorites.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (4 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"N elevators, M floors. Hall calls (floor + direction) and cabin calls (target floor). I'll design the controller that assigns calls and each elevator's state machine. Simulation-step time model OK? Capacity limits and door events in scope; weight sensors and maintenance mode as extensions?\"*\n\n**Functional:** request pickup (floor, direction); request destination (inside cabin); elevator serves requests in a sensible order; doors open/close; idle behavior.\n**Non-functional:** no starvation (a floor can't wait forever); efficient travel (don't zigzag); thread-safe request submission from many floors at once.\n\n## Step 2 — Entities\n\n`ElevatorSystem` (root/controller) · `Elevator` (entity: floor, direction, door, stop-set) · `HallCall(floor, direction)` / `CabinCall(floor)` (VOs) · `SchedulingStrategy` (which elevator takes a call) · `ElevatorState` (moving/idle/door-open).\n\n## Step 3 — Class & state diagrams\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class ElevatorSystem {\n        -List~Elevator~ elevators\n        -SchedulingStrategy scheduler\n        +requestPickup(int floor, Direction d)\n        +step()\n    }\n    class Elevator {\n        -int id\n        -int currentFloor\n        -Direction direction\n        -TreeSet~Integer~ upStops\n        -TreeSet~Integer~ downStops\n        -ElevatorState state\n        +addStop(int floor)\n        +step()\n    }\n    class SchedulingStrategy { <<interface>> +pick(List~Elevator~, HallCall) Elevator }\n    ElevatorSystem \"1\" *-- \"n\" Elevator\n    ElevatorSystem o-- SchedulingStrategy\n    SchedulingStrategy <|.. NearestCarStrategy\n    SchedulingStrategy <|.. LookAwareStrategy\n```\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Idle\n    Idle --> MovingUp : stop added above\n    Idle --> MovingDown : stop added below\n    MovingUp --> DoorOpen : reached a stop\n    MovingDown --> DoorOpen : reached a stop\n    DoorOpen --> MovingUp : more stops above (same direction first)\n    DoorOpen --> MovingDown : more stops below\n    DoorOpen --> Idle : no stops left\n```\n\n## Step 4 — The core insight: LOOK algorithm (say the name)\n\nReal elevators run **LOOK** (the disk-scheduling algorithm): *keep moving in the current direction, stopping at requested floors, until no requests remain ahead; then reverse.* Two sorted stop-sets make it trivial:\n\n```java\npublic class Elevator {\n    private int currentFloor = 0;\n    private Direction direction = Direction.NONE;\n    private final NavigableSet<Integer> upStops = new TreeSet<>();     // floors above, ascending\n    private final NavigableSet<Integer> downStops = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.reverseOrder());\n\n    public void addStop(int floor) {\n        if (floor == currentFloor) return;                             // already here → door event\n        if (floor > currentFloor) upStops.add(floor); else downStops.add(floor);\n        if (direction == Direction.NONE)\n            direction = floor > currentFloor ? Direction.UP : Direction.DOWN;\n    }\n\n    public void step() {                                               // one tick of simulation\n        NavigableSet<Integer> ahead = direction == Direction.UP ? upStops : downStops;\n        if (ahead.isEmpty()) {                                         // nothing ahead → reverse or idle\n            direction = !upStops.isEmpty() ? Direction.UP\n                      : !downStops.isEmpty() ? Direction.DOWN : Direction.NONE;\n            if (direction == Direction.NONE) return;\n            ahead = direction == Direction.UP ? upStops : downStops;\n        }\n        currentFloor += direction == Direction.UP ? 1 : -1;            // move one floor\n        if (ahead.contains(currentFloor)) {\n            ahead.remove(currentFloor);\n            openAndCloseDoors();                                       // emits events to observers\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Why not FCFS?** Serving requests in arrival order zigzags (10 → 2 → 9 → 1…) — O(N) extra travel per request; LOOK batches by direction. Saying \"this is LOOK, same family as disk scheduling; SCAN goes to the ends, LOOK reverses early\" is a guaranteed point.\n\n## Step 5 — Assigning hall calls: Strategy\n\n```java\npublic class NearestCarStrategy implements SchedulingStrategy {\n    public Elevator pick(List<Elevator> cars, HallCall call) {\n        return cars.stream()\n            .min(Comparator.comparingInt(e -> cost(e, call)))\n            .orElseThrow();\n    }\n    // cost: distance, heavily discounted if the car is already heading toward the call\n    // in the call's direction; penalized if it must finish opposite-direction work first.\n    private int cost(Elevator e, HallCall c) {\n        int dist = Math.abs(e.currentFloor() - c.floor());\n        boolean onTheWay = e.direction() == c.direction()\n            && (c.direction() == Direction.UP ? e.currentFloor() <= c.floor()\n                                              : e.currentFloor() >= c.floor());\n        if (e.direction() == Direction.NONE) return dist;              // idle: pure distance\n        return onTheWay ? dist : dist + 2 * SPAN;                      // detour penalty\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAlternatives to name: round-robin (simple, poor), least-loaded (by pending stops), zone-based (skyscrapers: cars own floor ranges), destination dispatch (passengers enter target *at the hall panel* — modern buildings; groups riders by destination).\n\n## Step 6 — Concurrency: confinement, not locks\n\n**Say:** *\"Elevator state is complex and event-driven — instead of locking floor/direction/stop-sets, I confine each elevator to the controller thread and make button presses producers to a queue.\"* (Move 4, Ch 3.3.)\n\n```java\npublic class ElevatorSystem {\n    private final BlockingQueue<HallCall> calls = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();\n    public void requestPickup(int floor, Direction d) { calls.offer(new HallCall(floor, d)); }  // any thread\n\n    void controlLoop() {                                    // single thread owns ALL elevator state\n        while (running) {\n            HallCall c;\n            while ((c = calls.poll()) != null)              // drain new calls\n                scheduler.pick(elevators, c).addStop(c.floor());\n            elevators.forEach(Elevator::step);              // advance simulation one tick\n            sleepTick();\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nZero locks on elevator internals; the queue is the only synchronized structure. Starvation guard: LOOK inherently serves every floor in ≤ one full sweep; add aging to the cost function if a strategy could starve a call.\n\n## Step 7 — Extensibility follow-ups\n\n- **Capacity/weight:** `Elevator.isFull()` → strategy skips full cars; full car bypasses hall calls but honors cabin calls.\n- **Express/zoned elevators:** strategy filters by serviceable floors (`Set<Integer> servedFloors` per car).\n- **Maintenance mode:** a state that drains stops, parks, and removes the car from scheduling — State pattern earns its keep.\n- **Destination dispatch:** replace HallCall with `(from, to)`; scheduler groups by destination bands.\n- **Emergency (fire):** override state: all cars → ground, doors open — a mode on the *controller*, not per-car hacks.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nRequirements incl. starvation & efficiency → controller + per-car state machine (diagrammed) → LOOK with two sorted stop-sets (named!) → hall-call assignment as Strategy with a cost function → confinement concurrency (queue in, single control thread) → capacity/zoning/maintenance one-liners.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Two `TreeSet`s (up/down) is the implementation detail that makes your code visibly clean — memorize it.\n- \"Same-direction stops first, then reverse\" answers 80% of \"what order are floors served?\" probes.\n- The simulation `step()` model sidesteps timers/threads-per-elevator — offer real-time scheduling only if asked.\n- Don't model buttons/panels as classes unless asked — they're just call sources; over-modeling here burns 15 minutes.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why TreeSet for stops instead of a queue?**\n> Stops must be served in *positional* order along the travel direction, not arrival order — a queue replays request history; a sorted set gives \"next floor ahead\" as `ceiling/floor(currentFloor)` in O(log n), automatically merges duplicate requests for the same floor, and makes reversal trivial (switch sets). Arrival order is irrelevant once you've committed to LOOK — position is the schedule.\n\n**Q2. A passenger going down presses \"up\" to grab the car sooner. Does your design break?**\n> No — it degrades gracefully, like real elevators. The car stops at their floor going up; they board and press a lower floor; that becomes a downStop served after the up-sweep. Cost: one wasted stop for others. Mitigations if probed: destination dispatch eliminates the lie (passengers declare targets upfront), and load sensors + repeated-no-boarding heuristics can deprioritize suspicious hall calls — but I'd flag those as real-world tuning, not core design.\n\n**Q3. How does your single-threaded controller scale to 32 elevators × 100 floors?**\n> Comfortably — the loop's work per tick is tiny (drain queue: O(calls × N) cost evaluations; step each car: O(log stops)). 32 cars is trivial CPU. If it ever weren't: shard controllers by elevator bank/zone (each bank has an independent controller thread + queue), which mirrors how buildings physically group cars. What I *don't* do is thread-per-elevator with shared state — that buys contention and races for zero gain at this scale.\n\n**Q4. Where exactly do State and Strategy live in your design, and could you drop them?**\n> Strategy = hall-call assignment (`SchedulingStrategy`) — droppable to a hardcoded nearest-car if variation weren't obvious, but the interviewer's \"how would you improve pickup times?\" question is *answered by the seam*. State = per-car lifecycle (idle/moving/door-open/maintenance); with only 3 happy-path states an enum+switch inside `step()` is defensible and I'd start there, upgrading to state classes when door timing, maintenance, and emergency modes make each state's behavior rich. I say the upgrade trigger aloud — that's the calibrated answer.\n\n**Q5. How would you test the scheduler's quality, not just correctness?**\n> Simulation-based metrics: generate call workloads (morning up-peak, lunch two-way, random), run K ticks, measure average/max wait, travel time, and starvation counts per strategy. Correctness asserts invariants each tick (car within bounds, doors only open at stops, every call eventually served). This turns \"is nearest-car better than round-robin?\" from opinion into a table — and mentioning workload-dependent results (up-peak favors zoning) shows systems maturity.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/03-vending-machine", "path": "05-case-studies/03-vending-machine.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.3 Design a Vending Machine", "md": "# 5.3 Design a Vending Machine\n\n> *The* State pattern question. Also probes: money handling, inventory, and change-making (a hidden greedy/DP mini-problem). If you hear \"vending machine\", \"ATM\", or \"ticket kiosk\" — same skeleton.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (3 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Machine sells items at coded slots (A1..). Flow: insert money → select item → dispense + change; cancel refunds. Accepts coins/notes of fixed denominations. Should I handle: insufficient change scenarios, restocking/admin, and concurrent access — or is this single-user-at-a-time (physical machine implies it)?\"*\n\n**Functional:** insert money (multiple insertions), select, dispense, return change, cancel/refund, restock, collect cash.\n**Edge cases to list upfront:** item out of stock, insufficient payment, **machine can't make exact change**, cancel mid-flow.\n\n## Step 2 — Entities\n\n`VendingMachine` (root, holds current state) · `MachineState` (interface: Idle / HasMoney / Dispensing / OutOfService) · `Inventory` (slot → item + count) · `CashRegister` (denomination counts + change-making) · `Item`, `Money`, `Denomination` (VOs/enums).\n\n## Step 3 — Diagrams\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> Idle\n    Idle --> HasMoney : insertMoney\n    HasMoney --> HasMoney : insertMoney / selectItem (underpaid)\n    HasMoney --> Dispensing : selectItem (paid ≥ price, change makeable)\n    HasMoney --> Idle : cancel → refund\n    Dispensing --> Idle : dispense + change\n    Idle --> OutOfService : admin lock / fault\n    OutOfService --> Idle : admin unlock\n```\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class VendingMachine {\n        -MachineState state\n        -Inventory inventory\n        -CashRegister register\n        -long insertedAmount\n        +insertMoney(Denomination d)\n        +selectItem(String code)\n        +cancel()\n    }\n    class MachineState {\n        <<interface>>\n        +insertMoney(VendingMachine, Denomination) MachineState\n        +selectItem(VendingMachine, String) MachineState\n        +cancel(VendingMachine) MachineState\n    }\n    MachineState <|.. IdleState\n    MachineState <|.. HasMoneyState\n    MachineState <|.. DispensingState\n    VendingMachine o-- MachineState\n    VendingMachine *-- Inventory\n    VendingMachine *-- CashRegister\n```\n\n## Step 4 — State pattern (the expected core)\n\n```java\npublic class HasMoneyState implements MachineState {\n    @Override public MachineState insertMoney(VendingMachine m, Denomination d) {\n        m.addInserted(d);\n        return this;\n    }\n    @Override public MachineState selectItem(VendingMachine m, String code) {\n        Item item = m.inventory().itemAt(code)\n            .orElseThrow(() -> new InvalidSlotException(code));\n        if (m.inventory().countAt(code) == 0) { m.display(\"Out of stock\"); return this; }\n        if (m.insertedAmount() < item.price()) {\n            m.display(\"Insert \" + (item.price() - m.insertedAmount()) + \" more\"); return this;\n        }\n        long changeDue = m.insertedAmount() - item.price();\n        if (!m.register().canMakeChange(changeDue)) {           // ← the case juniors miss\n            m.display(\"Exact change only\"); return this;         // don't take money you can't return\n        }\n        return new DispensingState(code, changeDue);\n    }\n    @Override public MachineState cancel(VendingMachine m) {\n        m.refundInserted();\n        return new IdleState();\n    }\n}\n\npublic class VendingMachine {\n    private MachineState state = new IdleState();\n    public void insertMoney(Denomination d) { state = state.insertMoney(this, d); }  // pure delegation\n    public void selectItem(String code)    { state = state.selectItem(this, code); }\n    public void cancel()                   { state = state.cancel(this); }\n}\n```\n\n**Narrate the win:** every state must answer every event → the event×state matrix is complete by construction; \"select before paying\" is handled in `IdleState`, not by a forgotten `if`.\n\n## Step 5 — Change-making (the hidden algorithm)\n\n```java\npublic class CashRegister {\n    private final EnumMap<Denomination, Integer> stock = new EnumMap<>(Denomination.class);\n\n    /** Greedy largest-first — correct for canonical currency systems (say this caveat!). */\n    public Optional<Map<Denomination, Integer>> makeChange(long amount) {\n        Map<Denomination, Integer> result = new EnumMap<>(Denomination.class);\n        for (Denomination d : Denomination.largestFirst()) {\n            int use = (int) Math.min(amount / d.value(), stock.getOrDefault(d, 0));\n            if (use > 0) { result.put(d, use); amount -= (long) d.value() * use; }\n        }\n        return amount == 0 ? Optional.of(result) : Optional.empty();\n    }\n    public boolean canMakeChange(long amount) { return makeChange(amount).isPresent(); }\n    // commit: deduct result from stock + add inserted denominations — atomically with dispense\n}\n```\n\n**The caveat that scores:** greedy is optimal for canonical denominations (1/2/5/10…) but fails contrived sets *and* limited-stock cases (change 60 with {50×1, 20×3}: greedy takes 50 then dies; 20×3 works) — \"for limited stock I'd fall back to DP/backtracking over denomination counts.\" Knowing this beats knowing the pattern.\n\n## Step 6 — Money & atomicity of dispense\n\n- Money as `long` paise + `Denomination` enum — never double.\n- Dispense sequence: reserve item → compute change → **commit register** (add inserted, remove change) → release item → reset to Idle. On hardware failure mid-dispense → refund path + OutOfService; mention \"the physical world needs a mini-saga: reserve, act, compensate.\"\n- Concurrency: a physical machine is single-user (the UI serializes events); say it, then add \"if this were a shared kiosk service, the machine object's public methods get a single lock — coarse is correct here because operations are milliseconds.\"\n\n## Step 7 — Extensibility follow-ups\n\n- **Card/UPI payments:** `PaymentMethod` strategy; HasMoney generalizes to \"payment authorized\" — change-making skipped for digital.\n- **Dynamic pricing/combos:** `PricingPolicy` seam on item lookup.\n- **Restock/admin:** `OutOfService` state + `AdminPanel` facade (`restock`, `collectCash`, `setPrice`).\n- **Telemetry:** Observer on events (sold-out, cash-full) → restocking alerts.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nState diagram first → State pattern with pure-delegation machine → out-of-stock, underpayment, cancel handled per state → **change-feasibility check before dispensing** → greedy change-making with the canonical-coins caveat and limited-stock fallback → atomic commit order for dispense → digital-payments extension.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Draw the state diagram and get agreement *before* any code — this problem is won at the whiteboard.\n- \"Exact change only\" (checking `canMakeChange` **before** dispensing) is the differentiator edge case — lead with it.\n- ATM variant: same states + CoR for denomination dispensing (2000→500→100) + PIN/auth state — 90% shared skeleton.\n- Don't build a payment gateway; `insertMoney(Denomination)` is the whole payment API for the physical machine.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why the State pattern here instead of a status enum + switch?**\n> The behavior *per event per state* is rich: `selectItem` in Idle scolds, in HasMoney it runs a 4-branch decision, in Dispensing it's ignored. An enum+switch spreads that matrix across methods where any missed cell is a silent bug; state classes force every cell to be written, transitions are return values (impossible to forget), and adding OutOfService is a new class rather than +1 branch × N switches. For 2 trivial states I'd concede the enum — here the matrix is the complexity, so State earns its classes.\n\n**Q2. Where does the money go if dispensing fails after payment?**\n> Commit order protects the user: I take nothing irreversibly until the item is released. Reserve item (decrement with ability to restore), verify change feasibility, then physically dispense; only on dispense-success does the register commit (inserted cash absorbed, change counted out). Motor jam after commit → compensations: restore item count if not dropped, refund full inserted amount, transition to OutOfService, log for reconciliation. It's a tiny saga — each step has a compensation, and I can name them.\n\n**Q3. Greedy change fails with limited stock — show the fix.**\n> Feasibility becomes a bounded coin-change: denominations with per-denomination counts. DP over amount with counts (or DFS largest-first with pruning — fine at vending scale since amounts are small): `canMake(amount, denomIndex)` tries `use = min(count, amount/value) … 0` and recurses. Exponential worst case doesn't matter for amount ≤ a few thousand paise and ≤6 denominations; correctness does: greedy would refuse sales the machine can actually serve, or worse, promise change it can't produce if unchecked.\n\n**Q4. The interviewer swaps the problem to an ATM mid-interview. What transfers?**\n> The skeleton: State machine (Idle → CardInserted → PinVerified → TransactionSelected → Dispensing), CashRegister becomes the dispenser with the same feasibility logic, and money-safety commit ordering becomes debit-account-then-dispense with compensation (reverse debit on hardware failure — and note real banks reconcile asynchronously). New pieces: auth (PIN attempts state with lockout), account service behind an interface, and CoR for denomination dispensing. I'd literally say \"same skeleton, three new organs\" — showing transfer is the point of the swap.\n\n**Q5. How do you make this testable and what do you test?**\n> The machine takes `Inventory` and `CashRegister` via constructor; hardware actions (motor, cash slot) behind a `Dispenser` interface faked in tests. Key tests: full happy path asserting item + exact change + state back to Idle; underpay → prompt, still HasMoney; cancel → full refund; out-of-stock; **the change trap** — stock the register so change is infeasible, assert sale refused and money refundable; and a state-matrix sweep asserting every event in every state does something defined (no exceptions leaking). The matrix sweep is cheap because states are classes — the pattern pays for its own testing.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/04-lru-cache", "path": "05-case-studies/04-lru-cache.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.4 Design an LRU Cache", "md": "# 5.4 Design an LRU Cache\n\n> The bridge between DSA and LLD rounds — asked in both. DSA wants the O(1) structure; LLD wants generics, eviction as Strategy, TTL, and thread-safety. This chapter covers the full ladder, because interviewers climb it.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (2 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Fixed capacity, `get`/`put`, evict least-recently-used on overflow, both ops O(1). Then: should I make eviction pluggable (LRU/LFU/FIFO), add TTL, and make it thread-safe? That's usually the ladder — happy to climb it in that order.\"*\n\n## Step 2 — The core insight\n\nO(1) get + O(1) recency update needs two structures joined: **HashMap for lookup + doubly-linked list for order** (head = most recent, tail = victim). Map values point at list nodes so unlinking is O(1).\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n    subgraph HashMap\n        K1[key A] --> N1\n        K2[key B] --> N2\n        K3[key C] --> N3\n    end\n    subgraph \"Doubly-linked list (MRU → LRU)\"\n        H[head] <--> N1[A] <--> N2[B] <--> N3[C] <--> T[tail]\n    end\n```\n\n## Step 3 — Clean-room implementation (know this cold)\n\n```java\npublic class LruCache<K, V> {\n    private final int capacity;\n    private final Map<K, Node<K, V>> map = new HashMap<>();\n    private final Node<K, V> head = new Node<>(null, null);   // sentinels: no null checks ever\n    private final Node<K, V> tail = new Node<>(null, null);\n\n    public LruCache(int capacity) {\n        if (capacity <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"capacity must be positive\");\n        this.capacity = capacity;\n        head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;\n    }\n\n    public Optional<V> get(K key) {\n        Node<K, V> n = map.get(key);\n        if (n == null) return Optional.empty();\n        moveToFront(n);                                        // touch = recency update\n        return Optional.of(n.value);\n    }\n\n    public void put(K key, V value) {\n        Node<K, V> n = map.get(key);\n        if (n != null) { n.value = value; moveToFront(n); return; }\n        if (map.size() == capacity) {\n            Node<K, V> victim = tail.prev;                     // true LRU\n            unlink(victim);\n            map.remove(victim.key);\n        }\n        Node<K, V> fresh = new Node<>(key, value);\n        map.put(key, fresh);\n        addAfterHead(fresh);\n    }\n\n    private void moveToFront(Node<K, V> n) { unlink(n); addAfterHead(n); }\n    private void unlink(Node<K, V> n) { n.prev.next = n.next; n.next.prev = n.prev; }\n    private void addAfterHead(Node<K, V> n) {\n        n.next = head.next; n.prev = head;\n        head.next.prev = n; head.next = n;\n    }\n\n    private static final class Node<K, V> {\n        final K key; V value; Node<K, V> prev, next;\n        Node(K key, V value) { this.key = key; this.value = value; }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Details graders check:** sentinel head/tail (no edge-case ifs) · victim removed from *both* structures · update-existing moves to front · generic types · `Optional` over null (or say why null-return matches Map convention).\n\n**The shortcut to name, then not use:** `LinkedHashMap(capacity, 0.75f, true)` with `removeEldestEntry` override *is* an LRU cache — mention it (\"in production I'd start here or use Caffeine\"), then build clean-room since that's the ask.\n\n## Step 4 — LLD upgrade: eviction as Strategy\n\n```java\npublic interface EvictionPolicy<K> {\n    void onAccess(K key);           // get/put touch\n    void onInsert(K key);\n    K selectVictim();\n    void onRemove(K key);\n}\n// LruPolicy: the linked list above.  FifoPolicy: insertion queue, onAccess = no-op.\n// LfuPolicy: freq map + freq→keys buckets (O(1) LFU — name it, implement only if asked).\n\npublic class Cache<K, V> {\n    private final Map<K, V> store = new HashMap<>();\n    private final EvictionPolicy<K> policy;                    // injected\n    private final int capacity;\n\n    public void put(K key, V value) {\n        if (!store.containsKey(key) && store.size() == capacity) {\n            K victim = policy.selectVictim();\n            store.remove(victim); policy.onRemove(victim);\n        }\n        boolean fresh = store.put(key, value) == null;\n        if (fresh) policy.onInsert(key); else policy.onAccess(key);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Narrate:** *\"Eviction is the variation axis → Strategy. The cache owns storage + capacity; the policy owns ordering. LFU plugs in without touching tested code.\"* This refactor — from the fused DSA solution to the seamed LLD design — is often the *entire point* of the interview.\n\n## Step 5 — TTL (the common second rung)\n\n```java\nprivate record Entry<V>(V value, long expiresAtMillis) {\n    boolean expired(long now) { return now >= expiresAtMillis; }\n}\npublic Optional<V> get(K key) {\n    Entry<V> e = store.get(key);\n    if (e == null) return Optional.empty();\n    if (e.expired(clock.millis())) {                    // lazy expiry on read\n        store.remove(key); policy.onRemove(key);\n        return Optional.empty();\n    }\n    policy.onAccess(key);\n    return Optional.of(e.value());\n}\n// + scheduled sweeper for memory hygiene: lazy expiry alone never frees unread keys\n```\n\nSay both halves: **lazy check for correctness, background sweep for memory** — one without the other is a known gap. Inject the `Clock`.\n\n## Step 6 — Thread-safety (the third rung)\n\nLadder to climb aloud:\n1. **Coarse lock** — `synchronized` on both ops: correct, simple, contended. *Start here; it's honest.*\n2. **Why fine-grained is hard here:** even `get` mutates the list (recency), so a ReadWriteLock buys little — writes are everywhere. Naively CHM-for-map + lock-for-list re-introduces check-then-act races across the two structures.\n3. **Real designs (name, don't build):** segment the cache (N independent shards by `hash(key) % N` — contention ÷ N, LRU becomes per-shard approximate); or Caffeine's approach — lock-free reads with recency recorded in ring buffers, replayed in batches under one striped maintenance lock (approximate LRU on purpose).\n\n**The sentence that wins:** *\"exact LRU under concurrency is inherently serialized — every op reorders one list — so production caches relax exactness (sharding or buffered recency) to buy parallelism.\"*\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nO(1) map+DLL with sentinels → clean generic code with victim removed from both structures → `LinkedHashMap`/Caffeine name-drop → eviction extracted to Strategy (LRU/FIFO/LFU) → TTL with lazy+sweeper and injected Clock → thread-safety ladder ending at sharding/approximate-LRU insight.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Practice until the DLL code flows in <10 minutes — this one is typed, not discussed.\n- The three rungs (strategy, TTL, threads) are predictable — pre-plan the seams so upgrades look effortless.\n- Off-by-one check they love: capacity check happens only for *new* keys — updating an existing key at capacity must not evict.\n- If asked \"why not just timestamps in the map + scan for oldest?\" — that's O(n) eviction; the DLL *is* the sorted-by-recency structure maintained for free.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why a doubly-linked list — why not singly?**\n> `moveToFront` must unlink a node given only the node itself (we arrive via the map). Unlinking needs the predecessor: singly-linked = O(n) scan to find it; doubly = `n.prev` in O(1). The prev pointer is the price of O(1) recency updates. (Trick alternative: singly-linked with \"copy next node's data into me\" deletion breaks because the map's node references go stale.)\n\n**Q2. get() looks read-only. Why is it a write for concurrency purposes?**\n> Recency *is* state: get unlinks and relinks the node (and even in LinkedHashMap access-order mode, mutates link fields). So two concurrent gets race on list pointers — corruption, not staleness. That's why ReadWriteLock underdelivers here and why production caches decouple the read path from ordering: record the touch in a buffer, apply ordering later. Recognizing \"logical read, physical write\" is the core concurrency insight of this problem.\n\n**Q3. Sketch O(1) LFU since you named it.**\n> Two maps + bucket list: `key → (value, freq)` and `freq → LinkedHashSet<keys>`, plus a `minFreq` tracker. Access: move key from bucket f to f+1 (O(1) with hashed sets), bump minFreq if its bucket emptied. Evict: any (oldest-inserted for LRU tie-break) key from the `minFreq` bucket. All O(1). The subtle parts: minFreq resets to 1 on insert, and tie-breaking within a frequency uses insertion order — LinkedHashSet gives that free.\n\n**Q4. Cache stampede: 100 threads miss the same key simultaneously. Handle it.**\n> Single-flight per key: `ConcurrentHashMap<K, CompletableFuture<V>>` — first thread `computeIfAbsent`s a future and runs the loader; the other 99 get the *same future* and wait; on completion the value moves to the cache (remove the future on failure so retries can happen). This bounds backend load to one call per key per expiry. Bonus points: mention jittered TTLs and refresh-ahead so hot keys renew before expiring — stampede prevention, not just stampede handling.\n\n**Q5. How does this in-process design map to Redis in a real system?**\n> The interfaces survive; the guarantees shift. Storage/eviction move server-side (Redis maxmemory + allkeys-lru — which is *sampled*, approximate LRU: same exactness-vs-throughput trade we discussed). My cache class becomes a client wrapper: TTL via `SET EX`, single-flight still needed app-side (or via `SET NX` lock), and new failure modes appear — network timeouts (serve-stale policy), serialization, and cache-aside consistency (invalidate-after-write, the HLD conversation). Showing the same design pressures at both scales is exactly the LLD↔HLD bridge interviewers reward.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/05-board-games", "path": "05-case-studies/05-board-games.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.5 Design Tic-Tac-Toe & Snake-and-Ladder", "md": "# 5.5 Design Tic-Tac-Toe & Snake-and-Ladder\n\n> The \"show me clean fundamentals\" pair — common in machine coding screens. Scope is small, so graders zoom into modeling quality: turn management, win detection, board representation, and whether your design extends (N×N, new rules) without surgery.\n\n---\n\n## Part A — Tic-Tac-Toe\n\n### Step 1 — Clarify (2 min)\n**Say:** *\"Two players, N×N board (I'll build 3×3 but keep N generic), win = full row/column/diagonal, draw when full. CLI-driven moves. Extensions to keep in mind: N×N with M-in-a-row, >2 players, undo, or an AI player?\"*\n\n### Step 2 — Model\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Game {\n        -Board board\n        -Deque~Player~ turnOrder\n        -GameStatus status\n        -List~Move~ history\n        +play(Position p) MoveResult\n    }\n    class Board {\n        -Cell[][] grid\n        -int size\n        +place(Position, Symbol) void\n        +isFull() boolean\n        +winningLineThrough(Position) boolean\n    }\n    class Player { -String name -Symbol symbol }\n    class Position { <<record>> +int row +int col }\n    Game *-- Board\n    Game o-- \"2..*\" Player\n    Game --> GameStatus\n```\n\nDecisions to narrate: `Position` is a record VO; `Player` holds symbol (not the board); **turn order as a `Deque`** (poll → play → offer back) generalizes to N players free; `Game` is the aggregate root — the only public API.\n\n### Step 3 — The code that gets read\n\n```java\npublic MoveResult play(Position p) {\n    if (status != GameStatus.IN_PROGRESS) throw new GameOverException(status);\n    board.place(p, current().symbol());              // throws on occupied / out of bounds\n    history.add(new Move(current(), p));\n    if (board.winningLineThrough(p)) { status = GameStatus.WON; return MoveResult.win(current()); }\n    if (board.isFull())             { status = GameStatus.DRAW; return MoveResult.draw(); }\n    rotateTurn();\n    return MoveResult.next(current());\n}\n```\n\n**Win detection — the discussion point.** Checking all lines every move is O(N²); checking only lines *through the last move* is O(N):\n\n```java\npublic boolean winningLineThrough(Position p) {\n    Symbol s = cellAt(p).symbol();\n    return line(r -> cellAt(new Position(p.row(), r)), s)         // row\n        || line(r -> cellAt(new Position(r, p.col())), s)         // column\n        || (p.row() == p.col()      && line(r -> cellAt(new Position(r, r)), s))\n        || (p.row() + p.col() == size - 1 && line(r -> cellAt(new Position(r, size - 1 - r)), s));\n}\n```\n\nO(1) alternative to *name*: per-player counters per row/col/diagonal — increment on move, win when any counter hits N. Trade: O(N) extra memory per player, and undo gets harder. Saying both, with when-each-wins, is the senior move.\n\n### Step 4 — Extensibility answers (rehearsed)\n- **N×N, M-in-a-row:** board already generic; win check scans M-windows through the move — still O(N).\n- **Undo:** `history` + Command pattern — `Move.undo()` clears the cell, rotates turn back (this is why history exists from minute one).\n- **AI player:** `Player` becomes interface — `HumanPlayer` reads input, `BotPlayer` computes (`MoveStrategy`: random → minimax). *Game loop doesn't change* — that's the point to make.\n\n---\n\n## Part B — Snake and Ladder\n\n### Step 1 — Clarify (2 min)\n**Say:** *\"Board 1..100, dice 1..6, snakes and ladders as jumps, N players, exact-roll-to-finish or overshoot-wins? Multiple snakes can't chain from the same cell — do jumps chain (ladder → snake)? I'll assume: overshoot bounces back is out, exact finish not required, jumps don't chain — confirm.\"* (Asking about **chaining and exact-finish** shows you've actually played the game.)\n\n### Step 2 — The modeling insight: snakes and ladders are one thing\n\n```java\npublic record Jump(int from, int to) {\n    public boolean isSnake() { return to < from; }               // only presentation cares\n}\npublic class Board {\n    private final int size;                                       // 100\n    private final Map<Integer, Integer> jumps;                    // from → to, snakes AND ladders\n\n    public int destination(int cell) { return jumps.getOrDefault(cell, cell); }\n}\n```\n\nOne `Map<Integer,Integer>` handles both — modeling them as separate `Snake`/`Ladder` class hierarchies is the classic over-engineering trap here. Validate at construction: no jump from cell 1 or 100, no two jumps from one cell, no self-loops.\n\n### Step 3 — Game loop\n\n```java\npublic class Game {\n    private final Board board;\n    private final Deque<Player> turns;\n    private final Dice dice;                                      // interface! → test with loaded dice\n    private final Map<Player, Integer> positions = new HashMap<>();\n\n    public TurnResult takeTurn() {\n        Player p = turns.poll();\n        int roll = dice.roll();\n        int target = positions.get(p) + roll;\n        if (target > board.size()) target = positions.get(p);     // overshoot: stay (state the rule!)\n        int landed = board.destination(target);                   // apply snake/ladder\n        positions.put(p, landed);\n        if (landed == board.size()) return TurnResult.won(p, roll);\n        turns.offer(p);                                           // rotate only if game continues\n        return TurnResult.moved(p, roll, landed);\n    }\n}\npublic interface Dice { int roll(); }                             // RandomDice / LoadedDice(fixed seq)\n```\n\n**`Dice` as an interface is the star decision** — say: *\"injected dice makes every scenario deterministic in tests: I can force a snake, a ladder, an exact win.\"* Also handles \"two dice\" or \"roll again on 6\" via `DiceStrategy`.\n\n### Step 4 — Extensibility answers\n- **Roll-again-on-six / triple-six-penalty:** turn logic strategy (`TurnRule`), not ifs in the loop.\n- **Exact-finish rule:** the one-line branch flagged during clarification — flip per config.\n- **New board sizes / custom jumps:** constructor data.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\n**TTT:** Game/Board/Player split with Position VO, Deque turn rotation, last-move-local O(N) win check (O(1) counters named), occupied/out-of-bounds/game-over guarded, undo & bot extensions mapped to history & Player-interface.\n**S&L:** jumps unified in one map with construction-time validation, injected Dice, overshoot rule stated explicitly, rules-as-strategies for variants.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- These problems are graded on *polish per minute*: guard clauses, domain exceptions (`CellOccupiedException`), records, and a story-telling demo main.\n- Announce the traps as you dodge them: \"snakes and ladders are the same structure\"; \"win check only through the last move.\"\n- The Deque-rotation trick for turns is 3 lines and handles N players, eliminations (just don't re-offer), and skip-turns.\n- Never put game rules in `main` — the loop calling `game.takeTurn()` should be dumb.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Where do the game rules live, and why does that placement matter?**\n> Board owns *spatial* rules (valid cell, jumps, winning line); Game owns *procedural* rules (turn order, when the game ends, overshoot policy); Players own none. This split means changing dice rules never touches Board, and changing board size never touches Game. The failure mode it prevents: a `main`/UI that knows rules, which makes every variant a copy-paste of the loop.\n\n**Q2. Make Tic-Tac-Toe support undo cheaply.**\n> The `history` list is already the Command log. Undo: pop the last Move, clear its cell, set status back to IN_PROGRESS if it had ended, and rotate the turn deque backward (addFirst of the previous player). Board's counters-based win optimization would need decrements — which is exactly why I default to the last-move line-check version when undo is on the table: it's stateless, so undo is trivially correct.\n\n**Q3. Two players submit moves simultaneously in an online version. What changes?**\n> The Game aggregate becomes the serialization point: moves arrive as commands into a per-game queue processed by one worker (confinement — Ch 3.3), or `play()` is synchronized per game instance — either way, *turn validation* rejects the out-of-turn move that loses the race (\"not your turn\" is domain logic, not a lock artifact). Cross-server, the game state needs a single owner (sticky routing or a version-checked store with optimistic concurrency). Board-level locking is pointless — the turn rule already forbids concurrent legal moves.\n\n**Q4. Interviewer: \"Your Snake/Ladder classes… why is there just a Map?\"**\n> Because both are the same behavior — a forced relocation — differing only in direction, which nothing in the engine branches on. Classes earn existence through distinct behavior or invariants; here both would have one field-pair and identical `apply()`. I keep a `Jump` record for validation/display (isSnake for rendering) but the engine consumes the map. If a variant added behavior — \"ladders require a fee\", \"snakes trigger a card\" — *that's* when the hierarchy (or a `JumpEffect` strategy) appears. Modeling restraint is a feature.\n\n**Q5. How would you drop in an unbeatable Tic-Tac-Toe bot?**\n> `Player` is an interface with `Position nextMove(BoardView view)`; BotPlayer delegates to a `MoveStrategy` — minimax with alpha-beta over the board (3×3: trivial, ~5k states; memoize by board hash for N×N). Key design points: the bot receives a read-only `BoardView` (can't cheat), the Game loop is unchanged (it just asks the current player for a move), and strategies are swappable (RandomStrategy for easy mode). The absence of engine changes is the demonstration that the original seams were right.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/06-chess", "path": "05-case-studies/06-chess.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.6 Design Chess", "md": "# 5.6 Design Chess\n\n> The heavyweight modeling question. Nobody expects a full engine in 45 minutes — they expect **clean piece polymorphism, move validation layering, and honest scoping** of the hard rules (check, castling, en passant). Managing scope *is* the test.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify & scope (4 min — more than usual, on purpose)\n\n**Say:** *\"Full chess rules are deep — let me propose scope tiers: Tier 1: board, pieces, piece-legal moves, captures, turns. Tier 2: check/checkmate/stalemate. Tier 3: castling, en passant, promotion, draws. I'll design so all tiers fit, implement Tier 1 solidly, and talk through 2 and 3. Good?\"*\n\nThis scoping speech is the strongest possible opening — it shows you know the domain's depth *and* how to budget.\n\n## Step 2 — Model\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Game {\n        -Board board\n        -Color turn\n        -List~Move~ history\n        -GameStatus status\n        +makeMove(Move) MoveResult\n    }\n    class Board {\n        -Piece[][] squares\n        +pieceAt(Square) Optional~Piece~\n        +apply(Move) void\n        +undo(Move) void\n    }\n    class Piece {\n        <<abstract>>\n        -Color color\n        +candidateMoves(Board, Square) List~Square~\n    }\n    class Move {\n        <<record>>\n        +Square from\n        +Square to\n        +Piece captured\n        +MoveType type\n    }\n    Game *-- Board\n    Board o-- \"0..32\" Piece\n    Piece <|-- King\n    Piece <|-- Queen\n    Piece <|-- Rook\n    Piece <|-- Bishop\n    Piece <|-- Knight\n    Piece <|-- Pawn\n    Game --> Move : history\n```\n\nKey calls to narrate: `Square` is a record VO (file, rank); `Move` records **captured piece** — that single field makes undo possible, which makes check-detection cheap (see Step 4); pieces are *stateless about position* — the board knows where pieces are, pieces know how they move. (Position-in-piece duplicates state and desyncs — a classic trap.)\n\n## Step 3 — Piece movement: polymorphism + sliding reuse\n\n```java\npublic abstract class Piece {\n    protected final Color color;\n    public abstract List<Square> candidateMoves(Board b, Square from);   // ignores check!\n\n    /** Shared by rook/bishop/queen: walk rays until blocked. */\n    protected List<Square> slide(Board b, Square from, List<Direction> dirs) {\n        List<Square> out = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (Direction d : dirs) {\n            Square cur = from.plus(d);\n            while (cur.onBoard()) {\n                Optional<Piece> occupant = b.pieceAt(cur);\n                if (occupant.isEmpty()) { out.add(cur); cur = cur.plus(d); continue; }\n                if (occupant.get().color() != color) out.add(cur);        // capture, then stop\n                break;\n            }\n        }\n        return out;\n    }\n}\npublic class Rook extends Piece {\n    public List<Square> candidateMoves(Board b, Square from) { return slide(b, from, Direction.ORTHOGONALS); }\n}\npublic class Queen extends Piece {\n    public List<Square> candidateMoves(Board b, Square from) { return slide(b, from, Direction.ALL_EIGHT); }\n}\npublic class Knight extends Piece {\n    public List<Square> candidateMoves(Board b, Square from) {\n        return Direction.KNIGHT_JUMPS.stream().map(from::plus)\n            .filter(Square::onBoard)\n            .filter(sq -> b.pieceAt(sq).map(p -> p.color() != color).orElse(true))\n            .toList();\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe `slide()` helper is the elegance moment: rook/bishop/queen become one-liners (Template-ish reuse). Pawn is the honest mess — forward-only, double-step from start, diagonal-capture-only, promotion — keep it a dedicated class and *say* it's irreducibly special.\n\n## Step 4 — Move legality: the layered filter (the design's centerpiece)\n\n**Say:** *\"Legality has layers, and I'll keep them separate: (1) piece-shape moves from `candidateMoves`; (2) game-state filters — it's your piece, your turn; (3) the king-safety filter: a move is illegal if it leaves your king attacked. Layer 3 is expensive, so it's simulate-and-check.\"*\n\n```java\npublic MoveResult makeMove(Move m) {\n    Piece p = board.pieceAt(m.from()).orElseThrow(() -> new NoPieceException(m.from()));\n    if (p.color() != turn) throw new NotYourTurnException();\n    if (!p.candidateMoves(board, m.from()).contains(m.to())) throw new IllegalMoveException(m);\n\n    board.apply(m);                                   // simulate (captured piece saved in Move)\n    if (isInCheck(turn)) {                            // layer 3: king safety\n        board.undo(m);\n        throw new MoveLeavesKingInCheckException(m);\n    }\n    history.add(m);\n    turn = turn.opposite();\n    updateStatus();                                   // CHECK / CHECKMATE / STALEMATE / ongoing\n    return MoveResult.ok(status);\n}\n\nprivate boolean isInCheck(Color c) {\n    Square king = board.kingSquare(c);\n    return board.piecesOf(c.opposite()).stream()\n        .anyMatch(e -> e.piece().candidateMoves(board, e.square()).contains(king));\n}\n```\n\n**Checkmate = in check ∧ no legal move exists** (try every candidate of every piece with simulate-undo; if all fail king-safety → mate). **Stalemate = not in check ∧ no legal move.** Expensive but correct — and correctness-first with named optimizations (attack maps, incremental check tracking) is the right interview posture.\n\n## Step 5 — The special rules (talk, don't code)\n\n- **Castling:** conditions = king+rook unmoved (track via `hasMoved` flag or scan history), squares between empty, king's path not attacked. Modeled as `MoveType.CASTLE` — `Board.apply` moves two pieces.\n- **En passant:** legal only immediately after an enemy pawn's double-step — *this is why `history` exists*: last move is inspectable. `MoveType.EN_PASSANT` captures a piece not on the target square.\n- **Promotion:** `MoveType.PROMOTION` carries the chosen piece; apply swaps the pawn.\n- **Draws:** threefold repetition (board-state hash counts — name Zobrist hashing), fifty-move rule (counter reset on capture/pawn move).\n\nEach special rule confirms two design choices: **rich `Move` record + move history** — say that connection explicitly.\n\n## Step 6 — Extensions they might pull\n\n- **Undo/replay:** `Move` already reversible → Command pattern for free; replay = fold history over a fresh board (also gives save/load: serialize the move list, not the board — smaller and audit-friendly).\n- **Timers:** `ChessClock` per player, checked in `makeMove` — a Game concern, pieces untouched.\n- **AI:** `Player` interface (as in 5.5); minimax needs exactly the simulate/undo machinery legality already built — point out the reuse.\n- **Online play:** Game is the serialization point; moves are commands; state syncs by shipping moves (event sourcing in miniature).\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nTiered scoping speech → Board-owns-position / pieces-own-movement split → `slide()` reuse making Q/R/B one-liners → three-layer legality with simulate-undo king-safety → check/mate/stalemate definitions → special rules mapped to Move record + history → undo/AI/online as consequences of the same machinery.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Never start coding all six pieces — do Rook (slide), Knight (jumps), Pawn (the mess), and say \"Bishop/Queen are direction lists on slide.\"\n- \"Pieces don't know their position\" prevents the most common chess-design tangle — state it early.\n- The phrase \"candidate moves vs legal moves\" (pseudo-legal vs legal) is the domain vocabulary that signals depth.\n- If time collapses, sacrifice checkmate detection, never move validation — an illegal-move-permitting chess design is a failed one.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why abstract class for Piece and not an interface + strategies?**\n> Piece has shared state (color) and shared behavior (`slide`) → abstract class earns its slot. A `MoveStrategy` interface per piece type is a valid alternative — pieces become data (type+color) and movement becomes pluggable, which shines for variants (fairy chess). For standard chess the six types are closed and stable, so the hierarchy is simpler and the polymorphism direct. I'd name both and justify by change-likelihood: rules stable → hierarchy; rules moddable → strategy.\n\n**Q2. Why simulate-and-undo for check instead of computing attacked squares?**\n> Correctness-per-line-of-code. Simulate-undo reuses `candidateMoves` — one source of truth for attack logic — and handles the nasty indirect cases (pinned pieces, discovered checks) automatically, because \"does any enemy piece attack the king *after* the move\" is literally the definition. An attack-map is faster (engines maintain incremental bitboards) but duplicates movement knowledge and is easy to get subtly wrong in an interview. I optimize only if asked, and I can name the direction: precomputed attack tables, incremental update, Zobrist-keyed caches.\n\n**Q3. Where does en passant's legality actually live in your design?**\n> In Game-level validation consulting `history` — not in Pawn. Pawn's `candidateMoves` can't see the *time* dimension (was the double-step the immediately-previous move?); it only sees the board. So Pawn offers the diagonal as a candidate when an adjacent enemy pawn sits beside it, and the Game layer confirms the timing condition from the last move. This is a clean example of the layering: spatial rules in pieces, temporal rules in Game — same split that puts turn-checking in Game.\n\n**Q4. How do you test chess meaningfully in limited time?**\n> Scenario tests from set positions — a `Board.fromString(\"....\")` fixture parser is the highest-ROI helper I'd write. Then: each piece's moves from a known position (knight on rim, blocked rook); captures; the king-safety filter (moving a pinned piece must throw); one checkmate (back-rank) and one stalemate position; and an undo round-trip property (apply+undo returns the exact prior state — catches the captured-piece bookkeeping). Fixture-based tests double as documentation of the rules I claim to support.\n\n**Q5. Interviewer: \"Support Fischer-random (Chess960) and a 4-player variant — what breaks?\"**\n> Chess960: almost nothing — starting position becomes a `BoardSetup` strategy, and castling generalizes (king/rook start squares vary) → my `MoveType.CASTLE` handling parameterizes by origin squares rather than hardcoding e1/h1. That cheapness validates the design. Four-player: bigger surgery — `Color` becomes 4-valued, `turn` a rotation, \"checkmate\" per-player with elimination, and the board isn't 8×8 — which is why Board size/geometry should already be data, not constants. Honest answer: Tier-1 architecture survives, the Game-status rules are rewritten — and that's the right place for variant-specific code to live anyway.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/07-splitwise", "path": "05-case-studies/07-splitwise.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.7 Design Splitwise (Expense Sharing)", "md": "# 5.7 Design Splitwise (Expense Sharing)\n\n> The money-math question. Tests: Strategy (split types), BigDecimal/paise discipline, the balance-sheet data structure, and the famous **debt simplification** follow-up (a greedy algorithm hiding inside an LLD round).\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (3 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Users and groups; add an expense paid by one (or more?) users, split equal/exact/percent; view balances per user and pairwise; settle up. The classic follow-up is simplify-debts — want me to design for it? Assuming single currency and single payer per expense unless you say otherwise.\"*\n\n**Edge cases to name now:** splits must sum to total (validation), **rounding** (₹100 / 3 people), self-owed amounts, settling more than owed.\n\n## Step 2 — Entities\n\n`User` · `Group` (aggregate: members + expenses + balance sheet) · `Expense` (immutable once added: payer, amount, splits) · `Split` (VO: user + share) · `SplitStrategy` (equal/exact/percent) · `BalanceSheet` (the core structure) · `Settlement`.\n\n## Step 3 — Class diagram\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Group {\n        -List~User~ members\n        -List~Expense~ expenses\n        -BalanceSheet balances\n        +addExpense(ExpenseRequest) Expense\n        +settle(User from, User to, Money amt)\n        +balancesFor(User) Map~User, Money~\n    }\n    class Expense {\n        <<immutable>>\n        -User paidBy\n        -Money amount\n        -List~Split~ splits\n    }\n    class SplitStrategy {\n        <<interface>>\n        +computeSplits(Money total, List~User~, SplitSpec) List~Split~\n    }\n    SplitStrategy <|.. EqualSplitStrategy\n    SplitStrategy <|.. ExactSplitStrategy\n    SplitStrategy <|.. PercentSplitStrategy\n    class BalanceSheet {\n        -Map~UserPair, Money~ owed\n        +apply(Expense)\n        +netFor(User) Money\n    }\n    Group *-- BalanceSheet\n    Group o-- Expense\n    Expense *-- Split\n    Group ..> SplitStrategy\n```\n\n## Step 4 — Money & the rounding rule (the credibility test)\n\n```java\npublic record Money(long paise) {                       // integer minor units — never double\n    public Money times(int n) { return new Money(paise * n); }\n    public Money minus(Money o) { return new Money(paise - o.paise); }\n}\n\npublic class EqualSplitStrategy implements SplitStrategy {\n    public List<Split> computeSplits(Money total, List<User> users, SplitSpec ignored) {\n        long base = total.paise() / users.size();\n        long remainder = total.paise() % users.size();          // ₹100/3 → 3334,3333,3333\n        List<Split> splits = new ArrayList<>();\n        for (int i = 0; i < users.size(); i++) {\n            long share = base + (i < remainder ? 1 : 0);        // distribute extra paise deterministically\n            splits.add(new Split(users.get(i), new Money(share)));\n        }\n        return splits;                                          // invariant: Σ shares == total, exactly\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Say:** *\"Money is integer paise; the remainder paise are assigned deterministically (first k users) so splits always sum exactly to the total — the invariant `Expense` re-validates at construction.\"* This paragraph alone separates you from most candidates.\n\nPercent strategy: compute each share, round down, then distribute leftover paise the same way; Exact strategy: pure validation.\n\n## Step 5 — The balance sheet (design decision to discuss)\n\nTwo options — present both, pick one:\n\n- **(a) Pairwise map** `Map<(debtor,creditor), Money>`: preserves *who owes whom* exactly as expenses happened; settle-up is a direct decrement; auditable. O(n²) worst-case entries.\n- **(b) Net-only** `Map<User, Money>` (net positive = is owed): tiny and enough for \"how much am I owed?\", but *loses pairwise info* — you can't say \"you owe Bob ₹200\", only \"you owe ₹200\".\n\n**Pick (a), derive (b):** real Splitwise shows pairwise; net is a fold over pairs.\n\n```java\npublic class BalanceSheet {\n    private final Map<UserPair, Money> owed = new HashMap<>();   // canonical pair order + sign\n\n    public void apply(Expense e) {\n        for (Split s : e.splits()) {\n            if (s.user().equals(e.paidBy())) continue;           // payer's own share: no debt\n            addDebt(s.user(), e.paidBy(), s.amount());           // debtor → creditor\n        }\n    }\n    private void addDebt(User debtor, User creditor, Money amt) {\n        UserPair key = UserPair.of(debtor, creditor);            // canonical (minId, maxId)\n        Money cur = owed.getOrDefault(key, Money.zero());\n        owed.put(key, key.isForward(debtor) ? cur.plus(amt) : cur.minus(amt));  // opposite debts auto-net\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThe canonical-pair trick (store one entry per pair with a signed amount) auto-nets mutual debts — A owes B 300, B owes A 100 → one entry, 200. Worth narrating.\n\n## Step 6 — Simplify debts (the star follow-up — greedy on net balances)\n\n*\"Minimize the number of settlement transactions.\"* Key insight: **only net balances matter** — the pairwise graph can be discarded for settlement purposes.\n\n```java\npublic List<Settlement> simplify(Map<User, Money> net) {         // Σ net == 0 always\n    PriorityQueue<Entry> creditors = maxHeap();                  // most-owed first\n    PriorityQueue<Entry> debtors  = maxHeap();                   // most-owing first\n    net.forEach((u, m) -> { if (m.isPositive()) creditors.add(e(u,m));\n                            else if (m.isNegative()) debtors.add(e(u, m.negate())); });\n    List<Settlement> out = new ArrayList<>();\n    while (!creditors.isEmpty()) {\n        Entry c = creditors.poll(); Entry d = debtors.poll();\n        Money pay = Money.min(c.amt(), d.amt());\n        out.add(new Settlement(d.user(), c.user(), pay));        // debtor pays creditor\n        if (c.amt().minus(pay).isPositive()) creditors.add(e(c.user(), c.amt().minus(pay)));\n        if (d.amt().minus(pay).isPositive()) debtors.add(e(d.user(), d.amt().minus(pay)));\n    }\n    return out;                                                  // ≤ n-1 transactions\n}\n```\n\nGreedy max-creditor-vs-max-debtor gives ≤ n−1 transactions. **Honesty point:** the true minimum is NP-hard (partitioning into zero-sum subsets can beat n−1); greedy is the accepted practical answer — knowing that boundary is a strong signal.\n\n## Step 7 — Concurrency & extensions\n\n- Two members add expenses to one group concurrently → the Group is the consistency unit: `synchronized addExpense` per group (contention is per-group, fine), or queue-confine each group. Cross-server → optimistic versioning on the group.\n- **Extensions:** multi-currency (Money carries Currency; sheets per currency + FX at display), recurring expenses (scheduler + template), expense edit/delete (append a compensating expense — ledger-style, auditable — rather than mutating history; say \"immutable ledger, corrections as new entries\").\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nSplit strategies behind an interface → integer-paise Money with deterministic remainder distribution and sum-invariant validation → pairwise balance sheet with canonical-pair netting, net derived → settle-up flow → greedy debt simplification with the NP-hard honesty note → per-group consistency + immutable-ledger corrections.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Rounding is where they're waiting for you. Volunteer the ₹100/3 case before they ask.\n- \"Expenses are immutable; edits are compensating entries\" — the ledger mindset — echoes payment-system maturity and pre-answers audit questions.\n- The simplify algorithm needs only net balances — stating that reduction crisply is half its marks.\n- Don't build Users/auth/friends — group + expenses + balances is the whole game.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why is Expense immutable, and how do edits work then?**\n> Balances are *derived* from expenses; if expenses mutate, every derived structure (pairwise sheet, nets, past settlements) silently invalidates, and concurrent edit vs settle is a nightmare. Immutable ledger: an edit appends a reversal entry plus the corrected expense — balance sheet just applies both; history remains auditable (\"why do I owe this?\" is answerable); settlements that happened in between stay consistent. It's the same reason banks never UPDATE a transaction row.\n\n**Q2. Prove your equal split can't lose a paisa.**\n> Shares are `base = total/n` (integer division) with the remainder `r = total mod n` distributed as +1 paisa to the first r users. Sum = n·base + r = total by the division identity — exact, not approximate. Determinism (first-r rule) matters too: recomputing the split yields identical shares, so validation `Σ splits == total` in the Expense constructor is a hard invariant, not a tolerance check. With doubles, none of these statements are even well-defined — 0.1 isn't representable.\n\n**Q3. Walk through simplify-debts on: A +600, B −100, C −500 (net).**\n> Creditors heap: {A:600}; debtors: {C:500, B:100}. Round 1: A vs C → C pays A min(600,500)=500; A reduces to 100, C done. Round 2: A vs B → B pays A 100; both done. Result: 2 transactions for 3 people (≤ n−1 ✓). Contrast the pairwise graph, which might have contained A→B, B→C, C→A chains — netting collapsed them. And Σnet=0 guarantees the heaps exhaust together, so the loop terminates cleanly.\n\n**Q4. Where do the strategies get chosen, and how does a new split type ship?**\n> `AddExpense` request carries a `SplitSpec` (type + per-type data); a `SplitStrategyFactory` maps type → strategy; Group stays strategy-blind. New type — \"split by shares/weights\": one `WeightedSplitStrategy` class implementing computeSplits with the same remainder discipline + one factory registration + a SplitSpec variant. Zero edits to Group, Expense, BalanceSheet — which is the OCP payoff I set up the seam for; I'd demo it by writing the weighted strategy in ~10 lines if asked.\n\n**Q5. How does this design survive going multi-currency?**\n> Money gains a Currency and refuses cross-currency arithmetic (fail-fast at the type level). Each expense is in one currency; the balance sheet keys become (pair, currency) — debts in different currencies don't net against each other (300 INR vs 5 USD has no exact sum). Display-level conversion uses an injected `ExchangeRateProvider`, clearly marked approximate; *settlement* in a different currency is an explicit user action recording the agreed rate as part of the settlement entry. The trap to avoid — and I'd say this — is netting across currencies inside the ledger: rates fluctuate, ledgers must not.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/08-movie-ticket-booking", "path": "05-case-studies/08-movie-ticket-booking.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.8 Design a Movie Ticket Booking System (BookMyShow)", "md": "# 5.8 Design a Movie Ticket Booking System (BookMyShow)\n\n> The concurrency showpiece. Everything funnels to one moment: **two users, one seat, same instant.** Your entire design is judged by how cleanly you handle that — plus the seat-hold lifecycle that real ticketing runs on.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (3 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Search movies → pick a show → see the seat map → select seats → pay → confirmed ticket. Core tension is concurrent seat booking, so I'll design the hold-then-confirm flow carefully. Assume one city/theatre chain in-process; payments behind an interface. Cancellations and dynamic pricing as extensions?\"*\n\n## Step 2 — The entity insight: Movie ≠ Show ≠ Seat ≠ Booking\n\nThe modeling test is separating four lifecycles (the Book/BookCopy move, Ch 4.3):\n- `Movie` — catalog metadata (VO-ish).\n- `Screen` — physical room with a fixed `SeatLayout`.\n- `Show` — **movie × screen × time**; the unit that owns bookable seats.\n- `ShowSeat` — seat *within a show* (the thing with AVAILABLE/HELD/BOOKED state). A physical `Seat` is layout data; `ShowSeat` is inventory.\n- `Booking` — user + show + seats + payment state.\n\n## Step 3 — Class diagram\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Show {\n        -Movie movie\n        -Screen screen\n        -Instant startsAt\n        -Map~SeatId, ShowSeat~ seats\n        +availableSeats() List~SeatId~\n        +holdSeats(List~SeatId~, User) SeatHold\n    }\n    class ShowSeat {\n        -SeatId id\n        -SeatCategory category\n        -SeatState state\n        -String heldBy\n        -Instant holdExpiry\n        +tryHold(User, Instant) boolean\n        +confirm(User) void\n        +release() void\n    }\n    class BookingService {\n        -PaymentProcessor payments\n        -PricingPolicy pricing\n        +book(User, ShowId, List~SeatId~) Booking\n    }\n    class SeatHold {\n        -String holdId\n        -List~SeatId~ seats\n        -Instant expiresAt\n    }\n    class PaymentProcessor { <<interface>> +charge(Money, PaymentMethod) PaymentResult }\n    class PricingPolicy { <<interface>> +priceFor(Show, List~ShowSeat~) Money }\n    Show \"1\" *-- \"n\" ShowSeat\n    BookingService ..> Show\n    BookingService o-- PaymentProcessor\n    BookingService o-- PricingPolicy\n    BookingService ..> SeatHold : issues\n    Booking --> SeatHold\n```\n\n## Step 4 — The core flow: HOLD → PAY → CONFIRM (never book-then-pay)\n\n**Say the why first:** *\"Payment takes seconds-to-minutes; seats can't be both sellable and promised during that window. So: short exclusive hold (5 min) → pay → confirm; expiry auto-releases.\"*\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    actor U as User\n    participant B as BookingService\n    participant S as Show\n    participant P as PaymentProcessor\n    U->>B: book(show, [A1, A2])\n    B->>S: holdSeats([A1, A2], user)\n    alt all seats held\n        S-->>B: SeatHold (expires in 5 min)\n        B->>P: charge(price, method)\n        alt payment success\n            B->>S: confirmSeats(hold)\n            B-->>U: Booking CONFIRMED\n        else payment failed/timeout\n            B->>S: releaseHold(hold)\n            B-->>U: PaymentFailed (seats freed)\n        end\n    else any seat taken\n        S-->>B: SeatUnavailable(A2)\n        B-->>U: pick different seats\n    end\n```\n\n## Step 5 — The concurrency core (this code wins or loses the round)\n\n```java\npublic class ShowSeat {\n    private SeatState state = SeatState.AVAILABLE;\n    private String heldBy; private Instant holdExpiry;\n\n    public synchronized boolean tryHold(String userId, Instant expiry, Clock clock) {\n        releaseIfExpired(clock);                              // lazy expiry: correctness w/o sweeper\n        if (state != SeatState.AVAILABLE) return false;\n        state = SeatState.HELD; heldBy = userId; holdExpiry = expiry;\n        return true;\n    }\n    public synchronized void confirm(String userId, Clock clock) {\n        if (state != SeatState.HELD || !userId.equals(heldBy) || clock.instant().isAfter(holdExpiry))\n            throw new HoldLostException();                    // pay-after-expiry loses cleanly\n        state = SeatState.BOOKED;\n    }\n    public synchronized void releaseIfExpired(Clock c) {\n        if (state == SeatState.HELD && c.instant().isAfter(holdExpiry)) { state = SeatState.AVAILABLE; heldBy = null; }\n    }\n}\n\npublic SeatHold holdSeats(List<SeatId> ids, String userId) {\n    List<SeatId> sorted = ids.stream().sorted().toList();     // canonical order → no deadlock\n    List<ShowSeat> acquired = new ArrayList<>();\n    Instant expiry = clock.instant().plus(HOLD_TTL);\n    for (SeatId id : sorted) {\n        ShowSeat seat = seats.get(id);\n        if (!seat.tryHold(userId, expiry, clock)) {\n            acquired.forEach(ShowSeat::release);              // all-or-nothing\n            throw new SeatUnavailableException(id);\n        }\n        acquired.add(seat);\n    }\n    return SeatHold.of(userId, sorted, expiry);\n}\n```\n\nPoints to narrate (all from Ch 3.3): lock lives on the seat (per-seat contention) · multi-seat = sorted order + release-on-failure · **lazy expiry inside tryHold** plus a background sweeper for the seat map display · confirm re-verifies holder *and* expiry — payment success after hold-expiry must fail safely (refund path).\n\n## Step 6 — Payments: idempotency & the double-charge question\n\n- `charge(idempotencyKey = holdId, ...)` — retries after timeout can't double-charge (the gateway dedupes by key).\n- Payment timeout = *unknown outcome*: query gateway status; if charged but hold expired → **refund**, don't resurrect seats (someone else may hold them). State machine on Booking: `PENDING_PAYMENT → CONFIRMED | PAYMENT_FAILED | REFUND_DUE`.\n- Multiple gateways → `PaymentProcessor` adapters (Ch 2.2).\n\n## Step 7 — Extensions (one-liners ready)\n\n- **Dynamic pricing:** `PricingPolicy` strategy (category, time-to-show, occupancy) — the seam exists.\n- **Cancellation:** Booking state machine + refund policy strategy (100%/50%/0 by cutoff); seats → AVAILABLE, notify waitlist (Observer).\n- **Waitlist:** per-show queue; seat-release event offers a short exclusive hold to the head.\n- **Seat-map UI staleness:** display is eventually consistent; truth is enforced only at `tryHold` — say \"the seat map is a hint, the hold is the contract.\"\n- **Scale-out (multi-JVM):** the seat invariant moves to the store — DB optimistic version per ShowSeat or Redis `SET NX PX` per seat; JVM locks remain a fast first filter (Ch 3.3 Q2).\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nMovie/Show/ShowSeat/Booking lifecycle split → hold-pay-confirm with TTL (sequence diagram) → per-seat synchronized tryHold + sorted multi-seat all-or-nothing → lazy expiry + sweeper → confirm re-validating holder/expiry → idempotent payments with unknown-outcome handling → pricing/cancellation/waitlist seams → single-JVM assumption stated with the DB/Redis escalation.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Raise the double-booking scenario yourself, immediately — it's the interviewer's only real question and pre-empting it flips the dynamic.\n- \"The seat map is a hint; the hold is the contract\" defuses all UI-staleness follow-ups in one sentence.\n- Holds must expire *both* lazily (correctness) and via sweeper (hygiene) — naming both is a checklist item.\n- Don't model theatres/cities/search — the interviewer wants the booking core; spend your minutes there.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Two users click seat A1 within a millisecond. Trace exactly what happens.**\n> Both threads reach `A1.tryHold`. The seat's monitor admits one — say user X: sees AVAILABLE, writes HELD/heldBy=X, returns true. Y enters after X exits, sees HELD (guaranteed visible by the monitor's happens-before), returns false; BookingService releases any seats Y already acquired and returns SeatUnavailable. X proceeds to payment with a 5-minute exclusive window. One winner, no partial holds, and the loser fails in milliseconds — before payment, which is the UX win of hold-first.\n\n**Q2. Payment succeeded at t=5:01 but the hold expired at t=5:00. Who gets the seat?**\n> Nobody gets it *by that payment*. `confirm` re-checks expiry and throws HoldLost — because the seat may already be held by someone else; resurrecting it would double-book. The charge is reversed via the refund path (Booking → REFUND_DUE, async refund by idempotency key). This is the unavoidable trade of TTL holds: rare pay-but-lose events handled by refund, versus frequent seats-stuck-forever without TTL. Real systems bias TTL ≥ p99 payment latency to make it rare, and I'd say that number aloud (5 min).\n\n**Q3. Why hold at seat granularity rather than locking the whole show?**\n> Contention scoping. A show-level lock serializes every booking for that show — during a blockbuster opening, thousands of users queue behind one monitor for seats that don't even conflict. Per-seat locks make disjoint selections fully parallel; conflicts (same seat) are exactly the cases that *must* serialize. Cost: multi-seat operations need deadlock discipline (sorted acquisition) — a price I pay in five lines. Show-level locking is my fallback only if seat operations were compound in ways per-seat locks can't cover.\n\n**Q4. Where does this break with two app servers, and what's the minimal fix?**\n> JVM monitors don't cross processes: both servers could hold A1. The invariant must move to shared state: optimistic — `UPDATE show_seat SET state='HELD', held_by=?, expiry=? WHERE id=? AND state='AVAILABLE'`, 0 rows = lost race (plus expiry check in the WHERE for expired holds); or Redis `SET seat:{show}:{id} user NX PX 300000`, DEL to release, and a Lua script for confirm's compare-holder-and-set. The in-memory design maps 1:1 — tryHold becomes that conditional write — which is exactly why designing the single-JVM version with clean atomic points matters: they become the atomic operations of the distributed version.\n\n**Q5. Design the cancellation + waitlist interaction.**\n> Cancel: Booking must be CONFIRMED and before the cutoff → refund per `RefundPolicy` strategy, seats released atomically per seat, `SeatReleased` events published. Waitlist: per-show FIFO (or priority) queue of `WaitRequest`s; a listener on SeatReleased tries to match head-of-queue seat count against the freed seats and issues a standard SeatHold with a *short* TTL and a notification — reusing the exact hold-pay-confirm machinery, no new booking path. Key invariants: waitlist offers can't leapfrog an active hold (they go through tryHold like everyone), and an unclaimed offer expires back to the pool. One mechanism, three flows — that reuse is the design's proof of quality.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/09-ride-hailing", "path": "05-case-studies/09-ride-hailing.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.9 Design a Ride-Hailing Service (Uber/Ola)", "md": "# 5.9 Design a Ride-Hailing Service (Uber/Ola)\n\n> The strategy-and-state-machine buffet. In LLD form (vs the HLD version), the graded parts are: trip lifecycle as a state machine, driver matching as Strategy, pricing composition, and the driver-assignment race.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (3 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Rider requests a ride (pickup, drop, cab type) → match a nearby driver → driver accepts → trip runs → fare → rating. LLD scope: the domain objects and flows in one service — I'll treat maps/ETA/geo-index as injected interfaces, not build them. Surge pricing and scheduled rides as extensions?\"*\n\n## Step 2 — Entities\n\n`Rider`, `Driver` (entity: status, location, vehicle) · `RideRequest` (VO) · `Trip` (aggregate root: the state machine) · `DriverMatchingStrategy` · `PricingStrategy` · `LocationService` / `NotificationService` (interfaces — out of scope internals) · `TripRepository`.\n\n## Step 3 — Class diagram & trip state machine\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class RideService {\n        -DriverMatchingStrategy matching\n        -PricingStrategy pricing\n        -DriverRegistry drivers\n        +requestRide(Rider, RideRequest) Trip\n        +driverAccepts(DriverId, TripId)\n        +startTrip(TripId)  +endTrip(TripId) Invoice\n    }\n    class Trip {\n        -TripId id\n        -RiderId rider\n        -DriverId driver\n        -TripStatus status\n        -RideRequest request\n        +assignDriver(DriverId)\n        +transitionTo(TripStatus)\n    }\n    class Driver {\n        -DriverId id\n        -DriverStatus status\n        -Location location\n        +tryAssign(TripId) boolean\n    }\n    class DriverMatchingStrategy { <<interface>> +candidates(RideRequest, DriverRegistry) List~Driver~ }\n    class PricingStrategy { <<interface>> +fare(Trip, RouteInfo) Money }\n    RideService o-- DriverMatchingStrategy\n    RideService o-- PricingStrategy\n    RideService ..> Trip\n    DriverMatchingStrategy <|.. NearestDriverStrategy\n    DriverMatchingStrategy <|.. RatingWeightedStrategy\n    PricingStrategy <|.. BaseFareStrategy\n    PricingStrategy <|.. SurgePricingDecorator\n```\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> REQUESTED\n    REQUESTED --> MATCHING : search drivers\n    MATCHING --> ASSIGNED : driver accepts\n    MATCHING --> CANCELLED : no drivers / rider cancels\n    ASSIGNED --> ARRIVING : driver en route\n    ARRIVING --> IN_PROGRESS : rider picked up (OTP)\n    ASSIGNED --> CANCELLED : either party cancels (fee rules)\n    IN_PROGRESS --> COMPLETED : drop-off\n    COMPLETED --> [*] : invoice + ratings\n```\n\nEncode legal transitions in the enum table (Ch 4.3) — \"cancel after pickup\" and \"start before assignment\" become impossible, not just unhandled.\n\n## Step 4 — The matching flow & the assignment race\n\nMultiple trips matching concurrently can pick the same driver — same shape as the parking spot race (Ch 5.1): **strategy proposes, entity disposes, loop retries.**\n\n```java\npublic Trip requestRide(Rider rider, RideRequest req) {\n    Trip trip = Trip.create(rider.id(), req);\n    trips.save(trip);\n    trip.transitionTo(TripStatus.MATCHING);\n\n    for (Driver d : matching.candidates(req, drivers)) {       // ranked nearby drivers\n        if (d.tryAssign(trip.id())) {                          // atomic: AVAILABLE → OFFERED\n            notifier.offerRide(d, trip);                       // driver has T seconds to accept\n            return trip;                                       // async continues via accept/decline\n        }                                                      // taken by another trip → next candidate\n    }\n    trip.transitionTo(TripStatus.CANCELLED);\n    throw new NoDriversAvailableException(req);\n}\n\npublic class Driver {\n    private final AtomicReference<DriverStatus> status = new AtomicReference<>(DriverStatus.AVAILABLE);\n    public boolean tryAssign(TripId t) {\n        return status.compareAndSet(DriverStatus.AVAILABLE, DriverStatus.OFFERED);  // CAS: one winner\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Offer timeout:** driver doesn't respond in T seconds → CAS back OFFERED→AVAILABLE (guarded by tripId to avoid releasing a newer offer), move to next candidate — a `DelayQueue`/scheduler job, same expiry discipline as seat holds.\n\n**Say:** *\"Driver status is a single-field state machine → CAS beats a lock; the matching strategy stays pure and lock-free; races resolve by retrying down the ranked list.\"*\n\n## Step 5 — Pricing: Strategy + Decorator composing\n\n```java\npublic class BaseFareStrategy implements PricingStrategy {\n    public Money fare(Trip t, RouteInfo r) {\n        return base(t.cabType())\n            .plus(perKm(t.cabType()).times(r.distanceKm()))\n            .plus(perMin(t.cabType()).times(r.durationMin()));\n    }\n}\npublic class SurgePricingDecorator implements PricingStrategy {\n    private final PricingStrategy inner;\n    private final SurgeIndex surge;                            // zone → multiplier\n    public Money fare(Trip t, RouteInfo r) {\n        return inner.fare(t, r).times(surge.multiplierFor(t.pickupZone(), t.requestedAt()));\n    }\n}\n// pricing = new PromoDecorator(new SurgePricingDecorator(new BaseFareStrategy(), surge), promos);\n```\n\nDecorators stack surge, promos, tolls, cancellation fees — each independently testable. **Fare lock-in rule to state:** quote at request time, persist the quote on the Trip, honor it at invoice (re-pricing at drop-off = support tickets).\n\n## Step 6 — Ratings, cancellation, notifications\n\n- **Rating:** `RatingService` keeps (sum, count) per user — running average, O(1) update; both sides rate; affects matching via `RatingWeightedStrategy`.\n- **Cancellation:** policy strategy keyed by trip state + elapsed time (free in MATCHING; fee in ARRIVING after grace). The state machine supplies the context.\n- **Notifications:** Observer on trip transitions (rider app, driver app, SMS) — decoupled, failure-isolated.\n\n## Step 7 — Extensions\n\n- **Scheduled rides:** `ScheduledRide` entity + scheduler that injects a RideRequest at T−lead-time — reuses the whole pipeline.\n- **Pooling:** Trip → `PooledTrip` with multiple riders/stops; matching gains route-compatibility scoring — flag it as a big feature living behind the same strategy seam.\n- **Geo at scale:** `DriverRegistry.nearby()` today = filter all drivers; the interface hides a geohash/quadtree index — \"the seam where LLD meets the HLD geo-index conversation.\"\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nTrip state machine with a legal-transition table → matching as ranked-candidates Strategy + CAS `tryAssign` + offer-timeout recycling → pricing as Strategy composed with Decorators + quote lock-in → cancellation policy driven by state × time → Observer notifications → scheduled/pooling/geo behind existing seams.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- The driver-assignment race is this problem's seat-booking moment — bring it up yourself with the CAS solution.\n- \"Strategy proposes, entity disposes\" — reusing the phrase from parking lot shows you see the shared shape (interviewers *love* cross-problem structure).\n- Persist the fare quote on the trip — a one-sentence decision that preempts the \"surge changed mid-ride\" trap.\n- Do NOT design maps/ETA/geo-sharding — name the interface and move on; drifting into HLD burns the round.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why CAS on driver status instead of synchronized like your seat example?**\n> The guarded state is a single enum field with simple transitions — exactly CAS's sweet spot: lock-free, no monitor overhead on the hot matching path, and failure (someone else took the driver) is a normal outcome I handle by trying the next candidate, not an error to block on. Seats needed synchronized because the hold writes three fields (state+holder+expiry) atomically. If Driver's assignment grew multi-field, I'd bundle it into an immutable record behind one AtomicReference — same CAS, wider payload — or fall back to a lock.\n\n**Q2. A driver goes offline mid-MATCHING after tryAssign succeeded. What prevents a stuck trip?**\n> The offer is leased, never granted forever: OFFERED carries a deadline; a scheduled expiry job (DelayQueue keyed by offer) CASes OFFERED→AVAILABLE if unanswered — and the trip's matcher then continues down the candidate list. Every intermediate state needs an exit timer — I'd generalize: MATCHING has a total budget (fail to CANCELLED with \"no drivers\"), ARRIVING has a no-show timeout. State machines without timeouts leak entities; that sentence is the design principle.\n\n**Q3. How does surge pricing stay fair when it changes between request and drop-off?**\n> Freeze at quote time: when the rider confirms, the composed pricing pipeline runs once and the resulting quote (with its surge multiplier and promo breakdown) is persisted on the Trip. The invoice at COMPLETED recomputes only *route-actuals* (distance/time) against the frozen rates — never re-reads the surge index. Disputes become auditable (\"your quote captured 1.4× at 18:02\"). The decorator design makes freezing natural: the pipeline output is a `FareQuote` value object, not a live calculation.\n\n**Q4. Matching should prefer nearby drivers but avoid starving far ones / low-rated ones. Design the strategy.**\n> A scoring strategy, not a filter: score = w₁·proximity + w₂·rating + w₃·acceptRate + w₄·idleTime (idleTime is the anti-starvation term — drivers waiting longer rank up). Candidates ranked by score; weights are config (A/B-testable). The structure matters more than the weights: because it's a `DriverMatchingStrategy` implementation, fairness policy changes are new strategies or new weights — no service edits. If asked about strict fairness, I'd mention a two-queue hybrid: mostly score-ranked, every k-th match from the longest-idle queue.\n\n**Q5. Sketch the pooling variant's impact honestly.**\n> Trip stops being rider-singular: `PooledTrip` holds ordered `Stop`s (pickups/drops per rider), and IN_PROGRESS becomes a per-rider sub-state (rider B is aboard, C not yet). Matching becomes route-compatibility: does adding rider C keep detour under X minutes for aboard riders — an insertion-cost computation against the current route, behind the same strategy interface but with real algorithmic meat (this is where I'd time-box in an interview). Pricing splits by rider with a pool discount decorator. The honest summary: state machine and seams survive, matching's internals are rewritten — and that's the correct place for the complexity to concentrate.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/10-logging-framework", "path": "05-case-studies/10-logging-framework.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.10 Design a Logging Framework", "md": "# 5.10 Design a Logging Framework\n\n> The machine-coding favorite (Flipkart/Razorpay staple). Tests Chain of Responsibility *or* the appender model, Strategy for formatting, Singleton discipline, and one sharp concurrency question: **how do you log without slowing the app down?**\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (3 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"A library like Log4j-lite: leveled logging (DEBUG<INFO<WARN<ERROR), multiple destinations — console, file, remote — with per-destination level thresholds, pluggable formats, and a global config. Should I optimize for the async/non-blocking discussion? That's usually where this goes. Rotation and per-package loggers as extensions?\"*\n\n## Step 2 — Model (the appender architecture — how real frameworks do it)\n\n`Logger` (client API) · `LogEvent` (VO: level, message, timestamp, thread, loggerName, throwable) · `Appender` (interface: console/file/remote sinks) · `Formatter` (Strategy: text/JSON) · `LevelThreshold` (filter per appender + global) · `LoggerRegistry` (the singleton bit) · `AsyncAppender` (Decorator — the concurrency answer).\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Logger {\n        -String name\n        -LogLevel threshold\n        -List~Appender~ appenders\n        +debug(String) +info(String) +warn(String) +error(String, Throwable)\n    }\n    class LogEvent {\n        <<record>>\n        +LogLevel level\n        +String message\n        +Instant at\n        +String threadName\n        +String loggerName\n    }\n    class Appender { <<interface>> +append(LogEvent) }\n    class Formatter { <<interface>> +format(LogEvent) String }\n    Logger o-- \"n\" Appender\n    Appender <|.. ConsoleAppender\n    Appender <|.. FileAppender\n    Appender <|.. AsyncAppender\n    AsyncAppender o-- Appender : wraps\n    ConsoleAppender o-- Formatter\n    FileAppender o-- Formatter\n    Formatter <|.. PatternFormatter\n    Formatter <|.. JsonFormatter\n```\n\n**On Chain of Responsibility:** many interview write-ups frame log levels as a CoR chain (DebugLogger → InfoLogger → …). Present it as an option, then say why the appender model wins: *\"CoR couples level-filtering to a linked structure; real frameworks treat level as a simple comparison filter and destinations as an independent list — orthogonal concerns, orthogonal objects. I'll take the appender model and use CoR where it genuinely fits: a filter pipeline per appender (level filter, rate-limit filter, redaction filter).\"* Knowing both and choosing with a reason beats reciting either.\n\n## Step 3 — Core code\n\n```java\npublic enum LogLevel { DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR;               // ordinal = severity order\n    public boolean allows(LogLevel msg) { return msg.ordinal() >= this.ordinal(); }\n}\n\npublic class Logger {\n    private final String name;\n    private volatile LogLevel threshold;                        // hot-reloadable config\n    private final List<Appender> appenders;                     // fixed after build (immutable list)\n\n    public void log(LogLevel level, String msg, Throwable t) {\n        if (!threshold.allows(level)) return;                   // cheap early exit — the hot path\n        LogEvent e = new LogEvent(level, msg, Instant.now(),\n                                  Thread.currentThread().getName(), name, t);\n        for (Appender a : appenders) a.append(e);               // appenders filter further if needed\n    }\n    public void info(String msg) { log(LogLevel.INFO, msg, null); }\n    public void error(String msg, Throwable t) { log(LogLevel.ERROR, msg, t); }\n}\n\npublic class FileAppender implements Appender {\n    private final Formatter formatter;\n    private final BufferedWriter out;                           // opened once, flushed by policy\n    private final LogLevel threshold;\n\n    @Override public synchronized void append(LogEvent e) {     // file writes must serialize\n        if (!threshold.allows(e.level())) return;\n        try { out.write(formatter.format(e)); out.newLine(); }\n        catch (IOException ex) { /* NEVER throw from a logger — fallback or drop, count it */ }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nTwo rules stated aloud earn points: **the disabled-level path must be near-free** (threshold check before any formatting/allocation — mention `Supplier<String>` lazy messages: `log.debug(() -> expensive())`), and **a logger must never throw into the app** — swallow, count, and optionally fall back to console.\n\n## Step 4 — The async answer (the differentiator)\n\nSynchronous file/remote appends put I/O on the caller's thread. Wrap any appender:\n\n```java\npublic class AsyncAppender implements Appender {\n    private final Appender delegate;\n    private final BlockingQueue<LogEvent> queue;                // BOUNDED — overflow policy is design\n    private final Thread worker;\n\n    public AsyncAppender(Appender delegate, int capacity, OverflowPolicy policy) {\n        this.delegate = delegate;\n        this.queue = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(capacity);\n        this.policy = policy;\n        this.worker = new Thread(this::drain, \"log-writer\");\n        worker.setDaemon(true);\n        worker.start();\n    }\n\n    @Override public void append(LogEvent e) {\n        if (!queue.offer(e)) policy.onOverflow(queue, e);       // DROP_OLDEST / DROP_NEW / BLOCK\n    }\n    private void drain() {\n        try {\n            while (true) delegate.append(queue.take());          // single writer: no file contention\n        } catch (InterruptedException ie) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }\n    }\n    public void shutdown() { /* drain remaining, flush, join with timeout */ }\n}\n```\n\n**The trade to narrate:** producers never block on I/O; single consumer serializes file writes without locks on the hot path. Cost: crash can lose queued events (acceptable for logs — say it; unacceptable for an audit trail → sync fsync appender for that one). **Overflow policy is a real decision:** blocking preserves logs but can stall the app under I/O degradation — most frameworks drop and increment a dropped-counter.\n\n## Step 5 — Config & registry\n\n```java\npublic final class LogManager {                                 // the one Singleton\n    private static final LogManager INSTANCE = new LogManager();\n    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, Logger> loggers = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    public static Logger getLogger(String name) {\n        return INSTANCE.loggers.computeIfAbsent(name, INSTANCE::buildFromConfig);\n    }\n}\n```\n\nHierarchical names (`com.app.payment` inherits `com.app` config) — resolve by walking name segments; `volatile` thresholds make runtime level changes safe. Config from a builder/file at bootstrap.\n\n## Step 6 — Extensions\n- **Rotation:** `RollingFileAppender` — size/time policy object decides rollover in `append`; rotation is a policy strategy, not a new appender per scheme.\n- **MDC (request context):** `ThreadLocal<Map<String,String>>` merged into LogEvent — name it, note virtual-thread caveat (use scoped values on Loom).\n- **Redaction/sampling:** filters in the per-appender CoR pipeline.\n- **Remote appender:** batching + retry inside the appender behind the same interface — always wrapped async.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nAppender-list architecture (with CoR discussed and correctly placed as filter pipelines) → LogEvent as record → cheap disabled-path + lazy messages → never-throw appenders → AsyncAppender decorator with bounded queue + explicit overflow policy + daemon writer + shutdown drain → CHM registry with hierarchical config → rotation/MDC/redaction one-liners.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Lead with the hot-path principle: \"logging is called millions of times; the design optimizes the *disabled* and *enqueue* paths.\"\n- The bounded-queue overflow policy question is where this interview is decided — bring the three options and pick with a reason.\n- `AsyncAppender` as a Decorator (wraps any appender) rather than a parallel class hierarchy — structural elegance they notice.\n- Mention shutdown flushing unprompted: lost final logs are the classic real-world logging bug.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why must the logger never throw, and how do you still learn about its failures?**\n> A logger is invoked from every code path including error handlers — an exception from logging turns a minor incident into an app-wide failure loop (log the error → logging throws → log *that* error…). So appenders catch everything at the boundary. Failures stay observable via: an internal status channel (stderr fallback), a dropped/error counter exposed as metrics, and a last-resort periodic retry to reopen sinks. Fail-silent for the app, loud for operators.\n\n**Q2. Compare your appender model with the CoR level-chain design.**\n> The chain design (Debug→Info→Warn handlers) entangles two orthogonal axes: severity filtering and output destination — adding a destination means new links; per-destination thresholds contort the chain. The appender model keeps them orthogonal: level = a comparison filter (config), destination = a list entry; any combination composes. CoR still earns a place as the *filter pipeline* inside an appender (level → sampling → redaction) — ordered, independent, pluggable checks — which is where a pipeline pattern actually matches the problem shape.\n\n**Q3. Two hundred threads log to one file. Walk through the synchronization story.**\n> Without async: `FileAppender.append` is synchronized — correctness via one writer at a time, but the monitor + I/O serializes callers under load. With AsyncAppender: producers do `queue.offer` on an ArrayBlockingQueue (single brief internal lock, no I/O), and exactly one daemon worker performs writes — so the file needs no lock at all, batching becomes possible (drain N, write once, flush), and throughput scales with the queue, not the disk latency. Interleaving guarantee: events from one thread stay ordered (queue FIFO); cross-thread order is by enqueue time, which is what timestamps are for.\n\n**Q4. Where does Singleton hurt here and how do you keep it testable?**\n> `LogManager.getLogger` is a static global — convenient at call sites but hostile to tests (config bleeds between tests) and to multi-tenant embedding. Mitigations: the static facade delegates to an instance-based `LoggerContext` that owns registry+config — tests construct private contexts and never touch the static path; a reset/shutdown hook for the default context; and appenders/formatters always constructor-injected. That's the SLF4J/Log4j2 shape: static API for ergonomics, instance world behind it — I keep the singleton at the door, not in the architecture.\n\n**Q5. How would you add structured logging without breaking the design?**\n> LogEvent already carries structure; the mistake to avoid is pre-formatting into a String at the call site. Add key-value support at the API (`log.info(\"payment done\", kv(\"orderId\", id), kv(\"ms\", t))`) storing pairs on the event; Formatter decides rendering — PatternFormatter interpolates, JsonFormatter emits fields (plus MDC context merged in). Because appenders take LogEvent, not String, the change is additive: one API overload, one formatter. The principle: **format at the edge, never at the source** — same reason the async queue carries events, not strings.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/11-rate-limiter", "path": "05-case-studies/11-rate-limiter.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.11 Design a Rate Limiter", "md": "# 5.11 Design a Rate Limiter\n\n> The algorithm-flavored LLD question — also an HLD staple, so it doubles your prep. Deliverables: the four algorithms with trade-offs, clean Strategy structure, per-client state management, and airtight thread-safety on the check-and-consume.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (2 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Library-style limiter: `allow(clientId)` → boolean, N requests per window per client, pluggable algorithm. In-process first, then I'll cover the distributed story. Should rejected calls just return false, or do you want retry-after info?\"*\n\n## Step 2 — Structure: Strategy + per-client registry\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class RateLimiter {\n        -RateLimitStrategyFactory factory\n        -ConcurrentHashMap~String, RateLimitStrategy~ perClient\n        +allow(String clientId) boolean\n    }\n    class RateLimitStrategy { <<interface>> +tryAcquire() boolean }\n    RateLimitStrategy <|.. TokenBucket\n    RateLimitStrategy <|.. LeakyBucket\n    RateLimitStrategy <|.. FixedWindowCounter\n    RateLimitStrategy <|.. SlidingWindowLog\n    RateLimitStrategy <|.. SlidingWindowCounter\n    RateLimiter o-- RateLimitStrategy : one per client\n```\n\n```java\npublic class RateLimiter {\n    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, RateLimitStrategy> buckets = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();\n    private final Supplier<RateLimitStrategy> factory;          // configured algorithm\n\n    public boolean allow(String clientId) {\n        return buckets.computeIfAbsent(clientId, id -> factory.get()).tryAcquire();\n    }   // computeIfAbsent: per-client state created exactly once, race-free (Ch 3.2)\n}\n```\n\n## Step 3 — Token Bucket (the default answer — implement fully)\n\nCapacity = burst allowance; refill rate = sustained rate. **Lazy refill** (compute on access) beats a timer thread:\n\n```java\npublic class TokenBucket implements RateLimitStrategy {\n    private final long capacity;\n    private final double refillPerNano;\n    private double tokens;\n    private long lastRefillNanos;\n    private final Clock clock;                                  // injected → testable\n\n    public TokenBucket(long capacity, long refillPerSecond, Clock clock) {\n        this.capacity = capacity;\n        this.refillPerNano = refillPerSecond / 1e9;\n        this.tokens = capacity;                                 // start full: allow initial burst\n        this.lastRefillNanos = clock.nanos();\n        this.clock = clock;\n    }\n\n    @Override public synchronized boolean tryAcquire() {\n        refill();\n        if (tokens < 1) return false;\n        tokens -= 1;                                            // check+consume atomic under the lock\n        return true;\n    }\n    private void refill() {\n        long now = clock.nanos();\n        tokens = Math.min(capacity, tokens + (now - lastRefillNanos) * refillPerNano);\n        lastRefillNanos = now;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Why it's the default:** allows bursts up to capacity while capping the sustained rate; O(1) memory per client; lazy refill = no background threads. (Guava `RateLimiter` is token-bucket-ish — name it.)\n\n## Step 4 — The other three (compare, implement the one they pick)\n\n**Fixed Window Counter** — `Map<windowStart, AtomicInteger>`; simplest, but the **boundary burst** flaw: 100/min allows 100 at 0:59 + 100 at 1:01 → 200 in 2 seconds. Always volunteer this flaw.\n\n**Sliding Window Log** — `Deque<timestamp>` per client; evict entries older than window, allow if size < N. Exactly correct; O(N) memory per client — expensive at high limits.\n\n**Sliding Window Counter** — the practical fix: weighted sum of current+previous fixed windows: `prevCount × overlap% + currCount < N`. O(1) memory, approximately correct (assumes uniform distribution in prev window) — the industry compromise (Cloudflare's published approach).\n\n**Leaky Bucket** — queue draining at constant rate: perfectly smooth *output* (good for shaping calls to a fragile downstream), but no bursts and adds queueing latency; token bucket = burst-friendly *admission*, leaky = smoothed *processing*.\n\n| Algorithm | Memory/client | Accuracy | Bursts | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Token bucket | O(1) | rate-exact | ✓ up to capacity | default |\n| Fixed window | O(1) | boundary flaw ×2 | window-edge spikes | only with the caveat |\n| Sliding log | O(N) | exact | within budget | when exactness is a requirement |\n| Sliding counter | O(1) | ~approximate | smoothed | high-scale services |\n| Leaky bucket | O(queue) | output-exact | ✗ | traffic shaping |\n\n## Step 5 — Concurrency notes (they'll probe)\n\n- `tryAcquire` is synchronized *per client bucket* — contention only within one client; different clients touch different objects (per-key locking for free via the registry).\n- Lock-free alternative to mention: pack `(tokens, lastRefill)` into one `AtomicReference<State>` immutable record + CAS loop — faster under contention, more code; say when it's worth it (a hot shared bucket, e.g., global limit).\n- **Cleanup:** idle clients leak registry entries → expire buckets untouched for k windows (scheduled sweep, or LRU-bounded registry — reuse Ch 5.4!).\n\n## Step 6 — Where it sits & distributed reality\n\n- Placement: a servlet filter / gateway middleware — `allow()` before the handler; on reject return HTTP 429 + `Retry-After` (expose `nextAvailableAt()` from the strategy).\n- Multi-server: per-node limiters multiply the budget (N nodes × limit). Fixes: sticky routing by client; or centralize state in Redis — token bucket as a **Lua script** (atomic read-refill-consume server-side; the script is your `synchronized`), tolerating Redis latency/failure with a local fallback limiter and a fail-open-vs-fail-closed decision (rate limiting usually fails open; auth throttling fails closed — *say the distinction*).\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nStrategy interface + per-client `computeIfAbsent` registry → token bucket with lazy refill and atomic check-consume → four-algorithm comparison with the fixed-window boundary flaw and sliding-counter compromise → per-client lock scoping + CAS upgrade path → idle-bucket cleanup → 429/Retry-After integration → distributed story (Redis Lua, fail-open choice).\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Injected Clock isn't optional here — the *entire* test suite is time manipulation (burst, wait, refill, boundary).\n- \"Start the bucket full\" and \"cap at capacity during refill\" are the two small bugs graders look for.\n- The boundary-burst example (100 at 0:59 + 100 at 1:01) is your comparison anchor — one concrete number beats abstract accuracy talk.\n- If the interviewer says \"rate limit outgoing calls to a payment API\" — that's leaky bucket / smoothing territory; direction of protection changes the algorithm.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why is lazy refill better than a scheduled refill thread?**\n> A timer per bucket (or one timer scanning all buckets) costs threads, wakeups, and a scan proportional to client count — for state that nobody may be reading. Lazy refill computes tokens from `(now − lastRefill) × rate` at access time: mathematically identical, zero background work, and naturally consistent under the same lock as consumption. Timers reappear only for *cleanup* of idle buckets — a rare sweep, not a hot path.\n\n**Q2. Two threads call tryAcquire with one token left. Why exactly one succeeds — and what breaks if you used AtomicInteger naively?**\n> Under synchronized: thread A refills, sees 1 ≥ 1, decrements to 0, true; B enters after, refill adds ~nothing, sees 0 < 1, false. Check and consume share one critical section. Naive atomic version — `if (tokens.get() >= 1) tokens.decrementAndGet()` — is check-then-act across two atomics: both threads pass the check, tokens go to −1, both admitted. Correct lock-free needs the check and decrement in *one* CAS: loop on `compareAndSet(state, state.consume())` over an immutable (tokens, timestamp) snapshot.\n\n**Q3. A client with limit 100/min sends 100 requests at 12:00:59 and 100 at 12:01:01. Which algorithms admit what?**\n> Fixed window: all 200 (each window under its own count) — the flaw in one sentence. Sliding log: 100 total in any rolling 60s — second burst rejected until entries age out. Sliding counter at 12:01:01: ~97% overlap × 100 + current → over budget, rejected (approximately right). Token bucket (capacity 100, ~1.67/s refill): first 100 drain the bucket; two seconds later ~3 tokens exist → 3 admitted. Each algorithm's number *is* its trade-off — this example is the whole comparison compressed.\n\n**Q4. How do you rate-limit by multiple dimensions — per-user AND per-IP AND global?**\n> Compose limiters: each dimension keeps its own registry (keyed by its extractor), and a request must pass all — evaluate cheapest/most-selective first, and **only consume when all admit** (two-phase: tryAcquire with rollback, or check-all-then-consume-all under ordered per-bucket locks) — otherwise a request rejected by the global bucket still burned the user's tokens. In practice most systems accept the token-burn (simpler, slightly conservative) — I'd name both and pick the simple one unless fairness is a stated requirement.\n\n**Q5. Your Redis is down. What does the limiter do?**\n> Decided policy, not accident. Fail-open with a local fallback: each node falls back to an in-process token bucket at `limit/N` (approximate but bounded), logs the degradation, and a circuit breaker retries Redis — protects availability, tolerates temporary over-admission. Fail-closed (reject when the source of truth is gone) is right when the limiter *is* the security control — login attempts, OTP requests. The senior answer is naming the two modes, tying the choice to what's being protected, and making the fallback limiter the same Strategy interface so it's config, not code.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/12-pub-sub-queue", "path": "05-case-studies/12-pub-sub-queue.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.12 Design an In-Memory Pub-Sub / Message Queue (Kafka-lite)", "md": "# 5.12 Design an In-Memory Pub-Sub / Message Queue (Kafka-lite)\n\n> The concurrency capstone (Flipkart machine-coding classic: \"build an in-memory Kafka\"). Tests: topic/partition/offset modeling, producer-consumer threading, consumer groups, and delivery-semantics vocabulary. Everything from Part 3 shows up here.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (3 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Topics; producers publish; consumers subscribe and process at their own pace. Two big models — fan-out pub-sub (every subscriber gets every message) vs consumer groups (messages divided among group members). I'll build log-based storage with offsets, which gives both. In scope: ordering, retries, at-least-once semantics. Out: persistence to disk, network.\"*\n\n## Step 2 — The central design choice: log + offsets (say why)\n\nNaive pub-sub pushes each message into every subscriber's queue (copies × subscribers, no replay, slow consumer = memory bomb *in the broker*). **Log model (Kafka's insight):** topic = append-only list; each consumer just moves a cursor (offset). One copy, replay for free, per-consumer pace isolation.\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Broker {\n        -Map~String, Topic~ topics\n        +publish(String topic, Message) long\n        +subscribe(String topic, String groupId, MessageHandler)\n    }\n    class Topic {\n        -List~Partition~ partitions\n        +append(Message) Offset\n    }\n    class Partition {\n        -List~Message~ log\n        +append(Message) long\n        +read(long offset) Message\n    }\n    class ConsumerGroup {\n        -String id\n        -Map~Partition, Consumer~ assignment\n        -Map~Partition, Long~ committedOffsets\n    }\n    class Consumer {\n        -long offset\n        -MessageHandler handler\n        +pollLoop()\n    }\n    class Message { <<record>> +String key +byte[] value +Instant at }\n    Broker \"1\" *-- \"n\" Topic\n    Topic \"1\" *-- \"p\" Partition\n    Broker o-- ConsumerGroup\n    ConsumerGroup o-- Consumer\n```\n\n## Step 3 — Core code: partition log + thread-safe append/read\n\n```java\npublic class Partition {\n    private final List<Message> log = new ArrayList<>();\n    private final Object appendLock = new Object();\n\n    public long append(Message m) {\n        synchronized (appendLock) {                     // writers serialize per partition\n            log.add(m);\n            return log.size() - 1;                      // offset = index\n        }\n    }\n    public Optional<Message> read(long offset) {\n        synchronized (appendLock) {                     // simple + correct; see Q&A for lock-free read\n            return offset < log.size() ? Optional.of(log.get((int) offset)) : Optional.empty();\n        }\n    }\n}\n\npublic class Topic {\n    private final List<Partition> partitions;\n    public long append(Message m) {\n        int p = m.key() == null\n              ? ThreadLocalRandom.current().nextInt(partitions.size())     // no key: spray\n              : Math.floorMod(m.key().hashCode(), partitions.size());      // key: sticky partition\n        return partitions.get(p).append(m);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**The ordering sentence (mandatory):** *\"Ordering is guaranteed per partition, and same-key messages hash to the same partition — so per-key order (all events of order #123) holds, while global order across partitions is deliberately not promised. That's the scalability trade Kafka made.\"*\n\n## Step 4 — Consumers: pull loop, groups, commits\n\n```java\npublic class Consumer implements Runnable {\n    private final Partition partition;\n    private final MessageHandler handler;\n    private final OffsetStore offsets;                    // committed progress per (group, partition)\n    private volatile boolean running = true;\n\n    @Override public void run() {\n        long offset = offsets.committed();                 // resume from last commit\n        while (running) {\n            Optional<Message> msg = partition.read(offset);\n            if (msg.isEmpty()) { parkBriefly(); continue; }        // or wait/notify on append\n            try {\n                handler.handle(msg.get());\n                offsets.commit(++offset);                  // commit AFTER processing → at-least-once\n            } catch (Exception e) {\n                if (!retryPolicy.retry(msg.get())) {       // bounded retries w/ backoff\n                    deadLetters.publish(partition.topic(), msg.get());   // DLQ, then move on\n                    offsets.commit(++offset);\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Consumer groups:** group = one logical subscriber scaled out; each partition is owned by exactly one consumer in the group (assignment map). Two different groups each get all messages (fan-out); within a group, messages divide (work-sharing). Max useful parallelism per group = partition count — say it. Rebalancing on join/leave = reassign partitions (implement round-robin assignment; name the real-world hard parts: pauses, duplicate window during handoff).\n\n## Step 5 — Delivery semantics (the vocabulary section — rehearse verbatim)\n\n- **At-most-once:** commit *before* processing → crash between commit and process loses the message. Fast, lossy.\n- **At-least-once:** commit *after* processing (code above) → crash after process, before commit ⇒ redelivery. **Duplicates are therefore a consumer problem → handlers must be idempotent** (dedupe by message id / idempotency key).\n- **Exactly-once:** not achievable by delivery alone across process boundaries — achieved as *effectively-once* via idempotent processing or atomic process+commit (transactional outbox / Kafka transactions). Saying \"exactly-once is idempotency wearing a suit\" lands well.\n\n## Step 6 — Backpressure & slow consumers\n\nLog model already isolates pace (slow consumer only grows its *lag*, not broker queues). Remaining issues to name: unbounded log growth → **retention** (drop entries older than T / beyond size; consumers falling behind retention lose data — surface lag as a metric); blocking `read` when caught-up → wait/notify on append instead of spin (Condition per partition); publisher flood → bounded log + publish-side policy if memory-capped.\n\n## Step 7 — Extensions\n- **Delayed messages:** `DelayQueue` staging area → append on due-time.\n- **Priorities:** priority = separate topics consumed preferentially (per-message priority breaks log ordering — say why).\n- **Persistence:** Partition behind a `Log` interface → memory-mapped/file impl; offsets already externalized.\n- **Filters (message selectors):** predicate per subscription applied at poll — cheap because reads don't mutate.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nLog+offset model with the why (vs per-subscriber queues) → key→partition hashing with the per-key ordering guarantee → per-partition append lock, pull-based consumers → consumer groups with partition ownership and parallelism ceiling → commit-after-processing ⇒ at-least-once ⇒ idempotent handlers + DLQ with bounded retries → retention/lag for backpressure → delayed/priority/persistence seams.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Draw the log-with-cursors picture first — every later question (replay, lag, groups) is answered by pointing at it.\n- \"Commit after processing\" + \"handlers must be idempotent\" is a paired statement — never say one without the other.\n- The DLQ with bounded retries is the difference between a toy and a system — include it even in a 60-minute build.\n- If asked to demo: two groups on one topic (fan-out proof) + two consumers in one group (sharing proof) + kill-and-resume (offset proof). Three tiny scenarios, whole design proven.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why pull-based consumers instead of the broker pushing?**\n> Pull puts flow control where capacity is known — the consumer: it takes the next message exactly when ready, so a slow handler self-throttles with zero broker bookkeeping; batch size is consumer-chosen; replay is trivial (set your own cursor). Push needs per-subscriber buffers, overflow policy, and backpressure signaling in the broker — all the problems the log model deleted. Cost of pull: caught-up consumers must wait efficiently (Condition-on-append, or long-poll) instead of busy-spinning — a small, local problem versus push's global one.\n\n**Q2. A consumer crashes after processing offset 41 but before committing. What happens and what must be true for correctness?**\n> On restart (or rebalance to a peer), reading resumes from committed offset 41 → message 41 is processed *again* — at-least-once redelivery, by design. Correctness therefore requires idempotent handling: dedupe on a stable message id (processed-ids set / unique DB constraint), or naturally idempotent operations (set-status vs increment). The alternative — commit-then-process — silently drops 41 on the same crash. I choose duplicates over loss and make the handler contract explicit: \"must tolerate redelivery.\"\n\n**Q3. How do two subscribers both receive every message while three workers share one subscription?**\n> Same mechanism, different group ids. Groups A and B each maintain independent committed offsets per partition — both cursors sweep the whole log: fan-out. Within group B, its three workers each *own* a disjoint subset of partitions (assignment), so each message (living in exactly one partition) is processed by exactly one worker: work-sharing. Corollary I'd volunteer: with 2 partitions and 3 workers, one worker idles — partition count bounds group parallelism, so partitioning is a capacity decision, not an implementation detail.\n\n**Q4. Reads take the same lock as appends — a hot topic with 50 consumers contends. Improve it.**\n> Exploit immutability: appended messages never change, so reads only need a safe view of \"how long is the log.\" Replace the ArrayList with a structure allowing lock-free reads: an atomic `size` published *after* the element write (happens-before via the volatile write), consumers read indices < size without locking — single-writer-per-partition makes this sound; or segment the log into immutable chunks (only the tail segment is active). This mirrors real Kafka: one writer per partition, many lock-free readers of immutable segments. Appends keep their lock — it was never the bottleneck; reader-vs-writer contention was.\n\n**Q5. Where would ordering break if you added per-message priority, and what's the accepted alternative?**\n> Priority means the consumer takes \"most important next,\" not \"next in log\" — the offset cursor becomes a scatter of consumed/unconsumed holes: commit semantics collapse (committed offset no longer means \"everything before is done\"), replay and lag lose meaning, and per-key ordering is violated when a high-priority message overtakes its predecessor key-mate. Accepted alternative: priority *lanes* — separate topics (orders-high, orders-low) with consumers polling high first (or weighted) — each lane keeps clean log semantics, and starvation is handled by weighting. Priority is routing, not reordering — that's the design principle.\n"}, {"id": "05-case-studies/13-in-memory-file-system", "path": "05-case-studies/13-in-memory-file-system.md", "part": "Part 5 · Case Studies", "title": "5.13 Design an In-Memory File System", "md": "# 5.13 Design an In-Memory File System\n\n> The Composite pattern's home turf (Google/Uber/Amazon ask it; LeetCode 588 is its DSA cousin). Tests: tree modeling, path resolution, uniform file/directory handling, and permissions/links as extension pressure.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Clarify requirements (2 min)\n\n**Say:** *\"Unix-flavored API: `mkdir -p`, `createFile`, `write/append`, `read`, `ls`, `rm -r`, `find`. Absolute paths with `/`. Should I include size accounting, permissions, or symlinks? I'll design so they slot in. Concurrency at the end as usual?\"*\n\n## Step 2 — Model: Composite, with the add/remove trade-off stated\n\n```mermaid\nclassDiagram\n    class Node {\n        <<abstract>>\n        -String name\n        -Directory parent\n        -Instant createdAt\n        +size() long\n        +accept(NodeVisitor) void\n    }\n    class FileNode {\n        -byte[] content\n        +size() long\n        +write(byte[]) +append(byte[]) +read() byte[]\n    }\n    class Directory {\n        -Map~String, Node~ children\n        +child(String) Optional~Node~\n        +addChild(Node)\n        +removeChild(String)\n        +size() long\n    }\n    Node <|-- FileNode\n    Node <|-- Directory\n    Directory \"1\" *-- \"n\" Node : children\n    class FileSystem {\n        -Directory root\n        -PathResolver resolver\n        +mkdir(String path)\n        +createFile(String path)\n        +ls(String path) List~String~\n        +rm(String path)\n    }\n    FileSystem *-- Directory : root\n```\n\nDecisions to narrate: children as `Map<String, Node>` (O(1) lookup, name-uniqueness enforced by the map — a `List` is the rookie choice); `addChild/removeChild` on **Directory only**, not Node — type safety over uniform mutation (the Composite trade-off from Ch 2.2, cited); parent pointer for path reconstruction & `mv`.\n\n## Step 3 — Path resolution (the algorithmic core — one place, used by everything)\n\n```java\npublic class PathResolver {\n    private final Directory root;\n\n    /** /a/b/c → walk children maps; O(depth). */\n    public Optional<Node> resolve(String path) {\n        if (path.equals(\"/\")) return Optional.of(root);\n        Node cur = root;\n        for (String part : split(path)) {                       // split, ignoring blanks\n            if (!(cur instanceof Directory d)) return Optional.empty();   // /file/x is invalid\n            Optional<Node> next = d.child(part);\n            if (next.isEmpty()) return Optional.empty();\n            cur = next.get();\n        }\n        return Optional.of(cur);\n    }\n\n    public Directory resolveParentOf(String path) {             // for create/delete: parent must exist\n        return resolve(parentPath(path))\n            .filter(Directory.class::isInstance).map(Directory.class::cast)\n            .orElseThrow(() -> new PathNotFoundException(parentPath(path)));\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Every operation = resolve + one local action.** That framing keeps the whole design small:\n\n```java\npublic void mkdir(String path) {                                // -p semantics: create as you walk\n    Node cur = root;\n    for (String part : split(path)) {\n        Directory d = asDirectoryOrThrow(cur, path);\n        cur = d.child(part).orElseGet(() -> {\n            Directory fresh = new Directory(part, d);\n            d.addChild(fresh);\n            return fresh;\n        });\n    }\n}\n\npublic List<String> ls(String path) {\n    Node n = resolver.resolve(path).orElseThrow(() -> new PathNotFoundException(path));\n    return switch (n) {\n        case FileNode f -> List.of(f.name());                   // ls on a file → the file (Unix-like)\n        case Directory d -> d.childNames().stream().sorted().toList();\n    };\n}\n```\n\n## Step 4 — Composite pays off: recursive operations\n\n```java\n// size(): the Composite classic — one polymorphic call, recursion hidden\npublic long size() { return children.values().stream().mapToLong(Node::size).sum(); }  // Directory\npublic long size() { return content.length; }                                          // FileNode\n\n// rm -r: detach the subtree — O(1) structural delete (GC reclaims); guard the root!\npublic void rm(String path) {\n    if (path.equals(\"/\")) throw new CannotDeleteRootException();\n    Node n = resolver.resolve(path).orElseThrow(() -> new PathNotFoundException(path));\n    n.parent().removeChild(n.name());\n}\n\n// find / du / tree-print: Visitor over the composite (or a DFS with a Consumer<Node>)\npublic void walk(Node start, Consumer<Node> visit) {\n    visit.accept(start);\n    if (start instanceof Directory d) d.children().forEach(c -> walk(c, visit));\n}\n```\n\nMention the O(n) `size()` upgrade: cache sizes on directories, invalidate up the parent chain on writes (`+delta` propagation) — trade write cost for O(1) reads; choose by read/write ratio.\n\n## Step 5 — Concurrency (say the layered answer)\n\n1. **Coarse:** one `ReadWriteLock` on the FileSystem — `ls/read/find` share read lock; `mkdir/write/rm` take write lock. Correct, simple, fine for an interview library. *Start here and say so.*\n2. **Finer:** lock per directory for structural ops (create/delete lock the parent), per-file locks for content; cross-directory ops (`mv`) lock both parents in canonical (path) order — deadlock discipline from Ch 3.3.\n3. Name the hazard fine-graining introduces: resolve walks the tree while another thread deletes an ancestor → resolved node may be detached; fix by validating parent chain under the op's lock or tolerating detached-node ops (Unix semantics: open file handles survive unlink — a delightful aside).\n\n## Step 6 — Extensions\n- **Permissions:** `Permissions(owner, mode)` on Node; checks in resolver (execute-on-directories to traverse) — a `PermissionPolicy` seam keeps Unix/ACL swappable.\n- **Symlinks:** `SymlinkNode(targetPath)` — resolver follows with a hop-limit (cycle guard: `/a → /b → /a`); this is why resolution lives in one class.\n- **Watches (inotify):** Observer on Directory mutation events.\n- **Quotas:** size-cache propagation + limit check on write path.\n- **Persistence/journaling:** operations as Commands → write-ahead log → replay on start (event sourcing in miniature).\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Outcome — what a complete answer includes\nComposite with Map-children and mutation-on-Directory (trade-off cited) → single PathResolver, every op = resolve + local action → `mkdir -p` walk-and-create → recursive size/rm/find via polymorphism or Visitor → RW-lock baseline with per-directory upgrade + mv lock ordering → symlink cycle guard + permissions + watch seams.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- `Map<String, Node>` children is the single highest-signal choice — it encodes name uniqueness and O(1) lookup silently.\n- \"Every operation is resolve + one local step\" — say it; it's the sentence that makes your design look inevitable.\n- Guard the root, reject `/file/x` traversal, and reject creating over an existing name — the three edge cases graders check first.\n- LeetCode-588 experience helps but LLD wants classes and seams, not a nested HashMap in one function — mention the difference if relevant.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. Why put addChild on Directory instead of Node (uniform Composite)?**\n> Uniform Composite forces FileNode to implement addChild — throwing UnsupportedOperationException, an LSP violation: code holding a Node can't trust the interface. Putting mutation on Directory makes \"files can't contain things\" a compile-time fact; the cost is an instanceof/pattern-match at mutation sites — but those sites already resolved a *parent directory*, so in practice the cast lives in one place (resolveParentOf). Read operations (size, name, accept) stay uniform on Node — I keep uniformity exactly where it's true.\n\n**Q2. How does your design handle `mv /a/b /c/d` atomically?**\n> Resolve source node and both parent directories; validate d doesn't exist under /c (or overwrite policy); then under locks on both parents taken in canonical path order (deadlock discipline): remove from old parent's map, rename, add to new parent's map, update parent pointer. The two-map mutation is the atomicity concern — with per-directory locking, holding both parent locks makes the move invisible in any intermediate state. Also guard the classic corruption: moving a directory *into its own subtree* (/a into /a/b) — check ancestry before mutating.\n\n**Q3. Implement `find(path, predicate)` — where does Visitor genuinely help over a simple DFS?**\n> For one predicate, a DFS with `Consumer<Node>` is simpler and I'd write that. Visitor earns its ceremony when operations multiply *and* need type dispatch: `find` matching on file attributes vs directory attributes, `du` formatting differently per type, permission-audit, serialization — each becomes a NodeVisitor with visit(FileNode)/visit(Directory), no instanceof ladders, added without touching Node classes. Fixed hierarchy (File/Directory/Symlink — stable) + growing operations = exactly Visitor's precondition from Ch 2.4; I'd also mention sealed interfaces + pattern-matching switch as the modern equivalent.\n\n**Q4. A write is happening to /a/f while another thread does rm -r /a. What should happen?**\n> Depends on the granularity I chose — and I must be consistent. Coarse RW-lock: impossible interleaving — rm waits for the write lock; done. Per-node locks: the write holds f's lock but rm detaches /a from its parent without touching f — the writer completes against a detached subtree, its data GC'd after. That's actually coherent (Unix unlink semantics: existing handles keep working), and I'd document it as the contract: *deletion detaches; in-flight operations on the subtree complete harmlessly*. What's unacceptable is a third behavior appearing by accident — the answer is choosing semantics, stating them, and testing them.\n\n**Q5. Symlinks introduce cycles. Show the resolution algorithm's guard.**\n> Follow-with-budget: resolution walks components; on hitting a SymlinkNode, splice the target path into the remaining components and decrement a hop counter (start ~40, like Linux's ELOOP limit); zero → throw TooManyLinksException. The budget handles both direct cycles (/a→/a) and long chains uniformly, without tracking a visited-set (which would miss cycles through *different* paths to the same node less cheaply). Guard placement matters: inside PathResolver — the single chokepoint — so every operation (ls, write, rm) inherits cycle safety with zero duplication. That's the payoff of centralizing resolution from the start.\n"}, {"id": "06-interview-prep/01-interview-framework", "path": "06-interview-prep/01-interview-framework.md", "part": "Part 6 · Interview Prep", "title": "6.1 The LLD Interview Framework — A 45-Minute Script", "md": "# 6.1 The LLD Interview Framework — A 45-Minute Script\n\n> Interviewers grade *process* as much as design. This is the repeatable script: what to say, in what order, with time stamps. It works for whiteboard LLD rounds; the machine-coding variant is Ch 4.1.\n\n---\n\n## The 5-phase script\n\n```mermaid\ngantt\n    dateFormat mm\n    axisFormat %M\n    section 45-min LLD round\n    1. Requirements & scope        :00, 5m\n    2. Entities & relationships    :05, 7m\n    3. Class diagram + patterns    :12, 10m\n    4. Core code (1-2 flows)       :22, 15m\n    5. Concurrency & extensions    :37, 8m\n```\n\n### Phase 1 — Requirements & scope (5 min)\n\nOpen with the same four moves every time:\n\n1. **Restate the problem** in one sentence: *\"So: a system where users X and the system does Y.\"*\n2. **Enumerate core use cases** (3–5 max): *\"I see: park/unpark/fee. Am I missing a must-have?\"*\n3. **Cut scope explicitly:** *\"I'll leave payments-gateway internals and persistence out, behind interfaces — OK?\"*\n4. **Fish for the hidden axis:** *\"Which parts do you expect to vary — pricing rules? vehicle types?\"* (Their answer tells you where the Strategy seams go — they're literally handing you the rubric.)\n\n**Red flag avoided:** starting to draw classes 30 seconds in.\n\n### Phase 2 — Entities & relationships (7 min)\n\nRun the Ch 4.3 method aloud: nouns → entity / value object / plain field; verbs → methods on the object whose data they use; find the aggregate root; state lifecycles (\"Ticket: ACTIVE→PAID — I'll enforce transitions\").\n**Checkpoint sentence:** *\"Core model: ParkingLot as the root, Spots as entities with state, Money/SpotId as immutable values, Ticket as its own aggregate. Sound right before I diagram?\"* — invite correction *early*, when it's cheap.\n\n### Phase 3 — Class diagram + pattern seams (10 min)\n\nDraw boxes with only decision-relevant members; arrows with the 5 relationships (Ch 1.4). As you place each interface, use the 4-beat pattern rhythm (Ch 2.5): **recognize → justify → seam → cost**.\n*\"Fee rules vary → FeePolicy interface → new rules become new classes → costs an interface, worth it per your requirement.\"*\n**Checkpoint:** walk one end-to-end flow across the diagram as a sequence (\"car arrives → gate asks lot → strategy finds spot → ticket issued\"). If an arrow is awkward, fix the design now — cheapest moment.\n\n### Phase 4 — Core code (15 min)\n\nRules of engagement:\n- **Ask what they want coded:** *\"Shall I code the park/unpark flow with fee calculation, and stub the rest?\"* — coding the wrong thing is the biggest time-loss.\n- Write **entity invariants and the interesting method** (tryAssign, addExpense, makeMove) — not getters, not constructors-by-the-dozen.\n- Narrate while typing: name the invariant each guard protects.\n- Use the polish signals from Ch 4.2: final fields, domain exceptions, Optional returns, injected Clock.\n\n### Phase 5 — Concurrency & extensions (8 min)\n\n**Bring up concurrency yourself** — it's the phase most candidates wait to be pushed into:\n*\"Concurrency: the shared mutable state is spot status. Lock lives on the Spot; the check-and-assign is one critical section; different spots don't contend. Multi-JVM would move this invariant to the DB — out of today's scope.\"* (The Ch 3.3 triage: what's shared → what's the invariant → smallest scope.)\n\nThen volunteer two extensions with one-liners: *\"EV spots = new SpotType + fits(); dynamic pricing = new FeePolicy.\"* Extensions you *predicted* prove the seams were right.\n\n---\n\n## The phrase bank (memorize these — they carry the round)\n\n| Moment | Say |\n|---|---|\n| Scoping | \"I'll build X and Y fully; Z stays behind an interface — tell me if you'd rather see Z.\" |\n| Choosing a pattern | \"This varies per your requirements, so it goes behind an interface — new variants become new classes.\" |\n| Rejecting a pattern | \"Only two fixed variants, so an if is honest; I'll extract Strategy when a third appears.\" |\n| Concurrency | \"The invariant is ___; the smallest lock that protects it is on ___; contention scope is per-___.\" |\n| Trade-off | \"I'm trading ___ for ___ here because the requirement says ___.\" |\n| A mistake you catch | \"Actually, this belongs on the entity, not the service — moving it. The invariant should live with the data.\" |\n| Unknown question | \"I haven't built that; reasoning from first principles: ___. Correct me if the standard approach differs.\" |\n| Interviewer pushes back | \"You're right / interesting — let me follow that: ___\" (never dig in ego-first) |\n\n## Calibration by seniority (what they expect from you)\n\n- **SDE-1:** correct model, working code, basic patterns applied when pointed at. Follows the script.\n- **SDE-2:** everything above, unprompted — plus concurrency raised proactively, trade-offs stated, extension seams pre-planned. **Owns the script.**\n- **Senior/Staff:** everything above, plus calibration — knows what *not* to build, negotiates scope, connects LLD choices to system context (\"this in-JVM lock becomes a DB constraint at scale\"), and teaches while designing. **Bends the script to the conversation.**\n\n## The failure modes (ranked by frequency)\n\n1. **Coding immediately** — no requirements, no model → mid-round rewrites.\n2. **Over-engineering** — 6 patterns for a Tic-Tac-Toe; abstraction with one implementation and no stated axis.\n3. **Anemic model** — all logic in a Service class, entities as field bags; the #1 *design* fail.\n4. **Silent designing** — perfect diagram, zero narration; the interviewer grades what they hear.\n5. **Ignoring hints** — interviewer says \"what if fees change?\" and you don't build the seam. Hints are the rubric leaking; **always take the hint.**\n6. **No concurrency story** in a problem that screams for it (booking, inventory, parking).\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- 5 phases: scope → entities → diagram+seams → code the interesting 20% → concurrency+extensions. Timestamps keep you honest.\n- Checkpoint after every phase — early correction is cheap, late correction is a rewrite.\n- The phrase bank turns good design into *visible* good design.\n- Interviewer hints = the rubric leaking. Take every hint, build every hinted seam.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Practice the script on all 13 case studies until phase transitions are automatic — the content changes, the skeleton never does.\n- If you blank, return to nouns-and-verbs — it restarts the engine every time.\n- Watch the clock at phase boundaries, not continuously; announce budget honestly: \"10 minutes left — I'll finish this flow and talk extensions.\"\n- Record yourself doing one mock: silent stretches and missing narration are invisible from the inside.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. The interviewer gives a problem you've literally memorized (parking lot). Play it safe or show off?**\n> Run the script *genuinely* — ask the scoping questions even though you know the usual answers, because their variant may differ (EV-only lot, valet flow), and mechanical recitation of a memorized design is visible and discounted. Where preparation legitimately shows: speed and depth — you reach concurrency and extensions with 15 minutes to spare, and spend them on the follow-ups where the real evaluation happens. Memorized structure + live adaptation is the winning combination.\n\n**Q2. You realize at minute 25 that your model is wrong. What's the recovery?**\n> Say it immediately and specifically: \"This is wrong — Booking shouldn't own Seats; Show should, or two shows share seat state incorrectly. Let me fix the boundary.\" Then do surgical repair, not a restart: move the relationship, patch the affected methods, re-walk the core flow to confirm. A caught-and-fixed modeling error with clear reasoning often scores *higher* than a lucky first draft — it demonstrates the review instinct they actually hire for. What kills you is defending the broken model or silently rewriting everything.\n\n**Q3. How do you handle \"why not just use a database/Spring/library X for this?\"**\n> Agree with reality, then re-anchor scope: \"In production, absolutely — this uniqueness check is a DB constraint and the singleton is a Spring bean. The exercise is the design underneath: if the interfaces are right, the library slots into the seam — this repository becomes JPA, this lock becomes SELECT FOR UPDATE.\" Then offer to map any part onto the real stack. This shows you're simplifying deliberately, not from ignorance — the exact opposite of the candidate who *only* knows the framework.\n\n**Q4. What do you do in the last 2 minutes?**\n> Deliver the closing summary unprompted: requirements covered vs stubbed, the two or three design decisions that matter (\"per-seat locking, immutable ledger, strategy-based pricing\"), and the named debts (\"with more time: percent-split rounding tests, hold-expiry sweeper\"). Thirty seconds, rehearsed shape. It converts a fading session into a strong last impression, and the debts list preempts the interviewer's private criticisms by making them your own observations.\n\n**Q5. The interviewer stays silent the whole round — no hints, no reactions. How do you adapt?**\n> Keep the checkpoints but convert questions to statements-with-pauses: \"I'm treating fee rules as the variation axis — designing for that\" (pause; silence = proceed). Self-generate the pressure the interviewer isn't providing: raise the concurrency scenario, the edge cases, and one what-if per phase yourself. The script is designed to be self-driving — silent interviewers are testing exactly whether you have an internal process or need to be steered. End with the same closing summary; it gives a silent room something concrete to score.\n"}, {"id": "06-interview-prep/02-pattern-problem-cheatsheet", "path": "06-interview-prep/02-pattern-problem-cheatsheet.md", "part": "Part 6 · Interview Prep", "title": "6.2 The Cheat Sheet — Problems × Patterns × Traps", "md": "# 6.2 The Cheat Sheet — Problems × Patterns × Traps\n\n> One-screen revision before an interview. Every classic problem: its pattern sentence, its concurrency moment, and its trap. If you can reproduce this table from memory, you're ready.\n\n---\n\n## The master table\n\n| Problem | Pattern sentence | Concurrency moment | The trap (avoid it) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Parking Lot** (5.1) | Strategy (assignment, fee) + Factory (vehicle) + per-spot state | Two gates, last spot → `tryAssign` per-spot lock + retry | Fee logic in ParkingLot instead of FeePolicy |\n| **Elevator** (5.2) | State (car) + Strategy (scheduling) + LOOK on two TreeSets | Confine cars to control thread; queues in | FCFS serving → zigzag; thread-per-elevator |\n| **Vending Machine** (5.3) | State pattern, pure delegation + CoR-ish change | Single-user machine — say it, coarse lock if shared | Dispensing without `canMakeChange` check first |\n| **LRU Cache** (5.4) | HashMap + DLL; eviction as Strategy; TTL lazy+sweep | get() mutates → coarse lock, then shard/approximate | O(n) eviction via timestamps; forgetting map+list dual removal |\n| **Tic-Tac-Toe / S&L** (5.5) | Small clean model; Dice/Player interfaces; jumps = one Map | Turn validation is the serializer | Snake and Ladder as separate class hierarchies |\n| **Chess** (5.6) | Piece polymorphism + slide() reuse; simulate-undo legality | Game is the serialization point | Pieces knowing their position; coding all 6 pieces |\n| **Splitwise** (5.7) | Strategy (splits) + immutable ledger + pairwise balance sheet | Per-group consistency | double for money; losing paise in ₹100/3 |\n| **BookMyShow** (5.8) | HOLD→PAY→CONFIRM + per-seat lock, sorted multi-acquire | *The* seat race — bring it up yourself | Book-then-pay; holds that never expire |\n| **Ride Hailing** (5.9) | Trip state machine + matching Strategy + pricing Decorators | CAS driver AVAILABLE→OFFERED + offer timeout | Re-pricing at drop-off (freeze the quote) |\n| **Logger** (5.10) | Appenders + Formatter Strategy + AsyncAppender decorator | Bounded queue, one writer thread, overflow policy | Logger that throws; unbounded queue |\n| **Rate Limiter** (5.11) | Strategy per algorithm; token bucket lazy refill | check+consume in one critical section / CAS | Fixed-window boundary burst unmentioned |\n| **Pub-Sub** (5.12) | Log+offsets; groups own partitions; commit-after-process | Per-partition append lock; pull loops | Per-subscriber copies; \"exactly-once\" without idempotency |\n| **File System** (5.13) | Composite + Map children + single PathResolver | RW-lock coarse → per-directory, mv in path order | addChild on Node (LSP); List children |\n\n## Requirements checklists (ask-these-first, per problem family)\n\n- **Booking/inventory** (BookMyShow, hotel, cab): hold TTL? partial success on multi-item? cancellation/refund rules? overbooking allowed?\n- **Machines** (vending, ATM, kiosk): denominations? change availability? cancel mid-flow? admin/restock?\n- **Games**: variants of rules? undo? N players? bot? board size generic?\n- **Money** (Splitwise, wallet, payment): currency? rounding rule? edit history? settlement?\n- **Infra libraries** (cache, limiter, logger, queue): capacity/bounds? eviction/overflow policy? thread-safety contract? metrics hooks?\n\n## The universal seams (pre-decide these before any interview)\n\n1. **Repository interfaces** over in-memory maps — persistence-ready by construction.\n2. **Clock injected** — every time-based rule becomes testable.\n3. **Strategy for the axis the problem statement says varies** — pricing, matching, splitting, eviction, scheduling.\n4. **State machine (explicit transitions) for any entity with a lifecycle** — ticket, order, trip, booking, machine.\n5. **Observer for \"notify/update X when Y\"** — boards, alerts, analytics.\n6. **Domain exceptions** — one small hierarchy, thrown at invariant violations.\n\n## Java snippets you should be able to type from memory\n\n```java\n// 1. The atomic claim (parking, seats, drivers)\npublic synchronized boolean tryClaim(String owner) {\n    if (state != FREE) return false;\n    state = HELD; this.owner = owner; return true;\n}\n\n// 2. Get-or-create per key (sessions, buckets, locks)\nmap.computeIfAbsent(key, k -> factory.create(k));\n\n// 3. Sorted multi-lock (multi-seat, transfer)\nitems.sort(comparing(Item::id));\nfor (...) if (!item.tryClaim(o)) { releaseAll(claimed); return false; }\n\n// 4. Strategy + factory selection\nMap<Type, Strategy> registry; registry.get(type).execute(...);\n\n// 5. State transition table\nenum Status { ...; boolean canGoTo(Status next) { return LEGAL.get(this).contains(next); } }\n\n// 6. Deterministic money split\nlong base = total / n, rem = total % n;   // first rem shares get +1\n\n// 7. Bounded handoff\nBlockingQueue<T> q = new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(cap); q.offer(x) /*policy*/; q.take();\n```\n\n## Red-flag phrases to never say (and their replacements)\n\n| ❌ Don't say | ✅ Say instead |\n|---|---|\n| \"I'll just make it a singleton\" | \"One instance, injected from the composition root\" |\n| \"double for the price\" | \"long minor-units / BigDecimal — money is never floating point\" |\n| \"I'll synchronize everything\" | \"The invariant is X; the smallest lock protecting it is on Y\" |\n| \"We can add getters/setters for all fields\" | \"I'll expose behavior; fields stay private\" |\n| \"This pattern is best practice\" | \"This axis varies per the requirements, so it gets an interface\" |\n| \"That edge case won't happen\" | \"Guarding it here: throw DomainException with context\" |\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- 13 problems, one table: pattern sentence + concurrency moment + trap. Reproduce it from memory as your final revision.\n- Six universal seams cover every problem; seven snippets cover every coding moment.\n- Family checklists make your Phase-1 questions instant and impressive.\n\n## 💡 Tips & Tricks\n- Night before: rewrite the master table from memory, check gaps, reread only the chapters where you blanked.\n- Each problem's \"trap\" is usually the interviewer's favorite probe — prepare the dodge sentence.\n- The snippets are finger-memory: type each 3 times today; they cost zero thought under pressure.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. You get a problem not in any list — \"design a smart irrigation controller.\" First 60 seconds?**\n> Same machine, new nouns: it's a *machine* family problem (state machine: idle/watering/paused) + *scheduling* (strategy: timer, moisture-threshold, weather-aware) + *observer* (sensors publish readings) + limited shared resource (water pressure/zones → semaphore thinking). I'd say the family mapping aloud, run Phase 1 with the machines checklist, and trust the universal seams. Novel problems are recombinations — the confidence to say \"this decomposes into shapes I know\" is exactly what the unusual prompt is testing.\n\n**Q2. Which single problem is the best all-round practice, and why?**\n> BookMyShow. It exercises the modeling insight (Movie/Show/ShowSeat lifecycle split), the hardest common concurrency (multi-entity atomic claim with TTL holds), a state machine (booking), strategies (pricing), external-system discipline (idempotent payments), and clean extension pressure (waitlist, cancellation). If you can do it end-to-end in 45 minutes with the concurrency narrated unprompted, every other problem is a subset. Parking lot second — it's the gentler version of the same skeleton.\n\n**Q3. How do you revise these in the final week?**\n> Spaced retrieval, not rereading: day 1 — master table from memory + fix gaps. Days 2–5 — two problems daily as *timed whiteboard runs* (20-min diagram + narration, no code), pairing one heavy (chess, pub-sub) with one light (S&L, limiter). Day 6 — the seven snippets typed cold + the phrase bank (6.1) out loud. Day 7 — one full 45-minute mock with a friend or recorder, then rest. Producing beats reviewing 10:1 for this material.\n\n**Q4. The table says \"bring up the seat race yourself\" — what if the interviewer wanted to ask it as their big question?**\n> Even better: you answer it before it's asked, and they upgrade to the harder follow-ups (multi-JVM, payment-after-expiry) — which is where differentiation actually happens. There's no scenario where volunteering the core risk hurts: either it was their question (you look proactive) or it wasn't (you added a dimension they now probe). The only mistake is raising it and hand-waving the solution — volunteer it *with* the per-seat lock and TTL story ready.\n\n**Q5. How much of this transfers to the HLD interview?**\n> The spines are shared: strategy seams become pluggable services; state machines become workflow/saga states; per-entity locks become DB row locks or optimistic versions; the pub-sub log IS Kafka; rate limiter and cache appear verbatim in both rounds; idempotency and immutable ledgers are the same idea at both scales. What changes is the unit of composition — classes vs services — and the failure model (partial failure, networks). Saying \"this JVM lock is that DB constraint at scale\" in either round signals you see the continuum, which is the strongest architectural signal there is.\n"}, {"id": "06-interview-prep/03-qa-bank", "path": "06-interview-prep/03-qa-bank.md", "part": "Part 6 · Interview Prep", "title": "6.3 Rapid-Fire Q&A Bank — 60 Questions With Crisp Answers", "md": "# 6.3 Rapid-Fire Q&A Bank — 60 Questions With Crisp Answers\n\n> Screening rounds and interview warm-ups fire these directly. Answers here are the 15–30 second versions — expand on request. Grouped by topic; the ⭐ ones are near-guaranteed.\n\n---\n\n## OOP & Principles\n\n**1. ⭐ Four pillars in one line each?**\n> Encapsulation: state hidden behind behavior. Abstraction: expose what, hide how. Inheritance: is-a reuse of contract+code. Polymorphism: one call site, runtime-chosen behavior.\n\n**2. ⭐ Composition over inheritance — why?**\n> No fragile base class, runtime-swappable behavior, testable via injection, survives multi-axis change. Inherit only for true, stable is-a with substitutability.\n\n**3. ⭐ Explain each SOLID letter in a sentence.**\n> S: one reason to change. O: extend by adding code, not editing. L: subtypes must honor the base contract. I: many client-shaped interfaces over one fat one. D: depend on abstractions, inject the concretes.\n\n**4. Abstract class vs interface?**\n> Interface = contract, multiple inheritance of type, default methods, no state. Abstract class = partial implementation + state + constructors, single slot. Capability → interface; shared skeleton → abstract class.\n\n**5. ⭐ Why override equals and hashCode together?**\n> Hash collections bucket by hashCode then confirm by equals; breaking the pact makes contains/get silently fail or duplicate keys.\n\n**6. What makes a class immutable?**\n> final class, private final fields, no setters, defensive copies in/out, no `this` escape in constructor. Free thread-safety, safe map keys.\n\n**7. Law of Demeter in practice?**\n> One dot of behavior: `customer.canPay(x)`, never `customer.getWallet().getBalance()...` — tell the object holding the data to decide.\n\n**8. Anemic domain model — why is it bad?**\n> Entities as field-bags with logic in services → invariants unenforced and duplicated, check-then-act races, procedural code wearing OO syntax. Put behavior with data.\n\n**9. When is a `switch` acceptable in good design?**\n> In the one factory/creation site over a sealed/enum domain (compiler-checked exhaustive), or two honest variants with no third coming. Everywhere else on type → polymorphism.\n\n**10. Cohesion vs coupling?**\n> Cohesion: how much a class's parts belong together (high = one concept). Coupling: how much classes know each other (low = narrow, abstract dependencies). Aim high/low; every principle serves these two dials.\n\n## Design Patterns\n\n**11. ⭐ Strategy vs State?**\n> Same UML, different intent: Strategy — client picks an algorithm, variants independent, stable over lifetime. State — object switches its own mode, states know successors, transitions constant.\n\n**12. ⭐ Factory Method vs Abstract Factory?**\n> One product from a type → factory method. A consistent *family* of related products → abstract factory.\n\n**13. ⭐ Decorator vs Proxy?**\n> Same wrapping shape. Decorator: client composes it to add visible behavior, stacks. Proxy: controls access (lazy/auth/cache/remote), client usually unaware, single layer.\n\n**14. Adapter vs Facade?**\n> Adapter translates one alien interface into yours. Facade invents a simpler interface over many subsystems. Translate vs simplify.\n\n**15. When is Singleton legitimate, and what's the safest Java form?**\n> Genuinely process-wide, stateless-or-thread-safe service (config, ids, logging context); enum or holder idiom; and prefer container-managed single instances — inject, don't `getInstance()` everywhere.\n\n**16. Why does double-checked locking need volatile?**\n> `new` = allocate→construct→publish can reorder; without volatile another thread can see a non-null, half-built object. Volatile fences publication.\n\n**17. Builder vs telescoping constructors?**\n> Named steps, optional params with defaults, cross-field validation in build(), immutable product. Use at 4+ params or any optionality.\n\n**18. Observer's two failure modes and fixes?**\n> A throwing listener breaking the loop → try/catch per listener. Slow listener blocking publish → async delivery via per-listener queue/executor.\n\n**19. Command pattern's superpower?**\n> Actions become objects → storable, queueable, loggable, undoable (inverse), replayable. Undo/redo = two stacks.\n\n**20. Template Method vs Strategy for varying steps?**\n> Template: fixed skeleton, inheritance fills blanks, parent controls order. Strategy: whole algorithm swapped by composition. Prefer composition; protect an invariant sequence with a final template.\n\n**21. Chain of Responsibility's two flavors?**\n> First-match-wins (ATM denominations, approvals) vs process-and-continue (loggers, filters). Say which you're building.\n\n**22. Visitor — when, and its cost?**\n> Stable hierarchy + growing operations; double dispatch via accept/visit. Cost: new element type touches every visitor. Modern Java: sealed + pattern-matching switch often replaces it.\n\n**23. Flyweight in one line + JDK example?**\n> Share the immutable intrinsic part among millions of instances; extrinsic state passed in. `Integer.valueOf` cache, string interning.\n\n**24. Null Object pattern?**\n> A do-nothing implementation replacing null checks (NoDiscount, no-op logger). Don't use where absence is an error to surface.\n\n## Java & Language\n\n**25. ⭐ Why is money never double?**\n> Binary floats can't represent 0.1; errors compound across arithmetic. Use long minor-units or BigDecimal — exactness is the requirement.\n\n**26. Records — what do they give and when not to use?**\n> Immutable value carriers: final fields, equals/hashCode/toString, compact validation constructors. Not for entities with identity+lifecycle or mutable state.\n\n**27. Optional — the rules?**\n> Return type for expected absence; never fields or params; never `.get()` bare; empty collections instead of Optional-of-collection.\n\n**28. ⭐ HashMap vs ConcurrentHashMap vs synchronizedMap?**\n> HashMap: none-safe, corrupts under concurrent write. synchronizedMap: one global lock, compound ops still race. CHM: per-bin concurrency + atomic compound ops (putIfAbsent/compute) — the default under threads.\n\n**29. ArrayList vs LinkedList — real answer?**\n> ArrayList almost always: O(1) index, cache-friendly; amortized O(1) append. LinkedList only for heavy head/mid insert-delete via iterator — rare. (Deque → ArrayDeque.)\n\n**30. fail-fast vs weakly-consistent iterators?**\n> ArrayList etc. throw ConcurrentModificationException on structural change mid-iteration (best-effort). CHM/COW iterate a safe view: no exception, may miss concurrent updates.\n\n**31. Comparable vs Comparator?**\n> Comparable: the type's one natural order (`compareTo`). Comparator: external, many orders, composable (`comparing().thenComparing()`), keeps the entity clean.\n\n**32. Sealed interfaces — why care in LLD?**\n> Closed hierarchies the compiler knows: exhaustive switch without default, safer domain sums (PaymentMethod = Card|Upi|Wallet). Pattern-matching makes visitor-free dispatch clean.\n\n**33. String immutability — design consequences?**\n> Safe sharing/interning/map keys, no defensive copies; concatenation in loops → StringBuilder.\n\n**34. Why prefer enums to string/int constants?**\n> Type safety, exhaustive switches, namespaced values, attachable behavior/fields — a mini strategy per constant if needed.\n\n## Concurrency\n\n**35. ⭐ synchronized guarantees?**\n> Mutual exclusion on the monitor + memory visibility: release flushes, acquire re-reads — happens-before between exiting and entering threads.\n\n**36. ⭐ volatile vs synchronized?**\n> volatile: visibility + ordering for a single variable; no atomicity for compound actions. synchronized: atomicity + visibility for a block. Flag → volatile; count++ → atomic/lock.\n\n**37. ⭐ What is check-then-act and why does it break?**\n> Decision on state that can change between the check and the act (if free → assign). Fix: one atomic operation covering both — lock spanning check+act, CAS, or CHM compute methods.\n\n**38. Deadlock's four conditions and the practical fix?**\n> Mutual exclusion, hold-and-wait, no preemption, circular wait. Fix: global lock ordering (sorted IDs); or tryLock-with-timeout-and-retry; or one coarser lock.\n\n**39. wait/notify rules?**\n> Only while holding the monitor; wait in a while(predicate) loop (spurious/stolen wakeups); prefer separate Conditions (notFull/notEmpty) over notifyAll storms; prefer BlockingQueue over all of it.\n\n**40. CAS — what and where?**\n> Compare-and-set: write only if the value is still what I read; retry loop otherwise. Atomics, lock-free counters, single-field state machines (FREE→HELD). Fails under multi-field invariants → lock or immutable-snapshot-in-AtomicReference.\n\n**41. ⭐ How do you make seat/spot booking thread-safe in one JVM?**\n> Lock on the entity: synchronized tryHold checking-and-setting in one section; multi-seat → sorted acquisition all-or-release; TTL holds with lazy + swept expiry.\n\n**42. ReadWriteLock — when does it pay?**\n> Read-heavy shared structures (catalogs, configs, file trees): parallel readers, exclusive writers. Useless when \"reads\" mutate (LRU recency!).\n\n**43. ThreadLocal — use and hazard?**\n> Per-thread context (request ids, formatters) without passing params. Hazards: leaks on thread pools (remove() in finally), invisible coupling; scoped values on virtual threads.\n\n**44. Virtual threads' one-line pitch?**\n> Cheap JVM-scheduled threads → thread-per-request survives 100K blocking tasks with simple code; not faster CPU, just cheaper waiting.\n\n**45. Why bounded queues?**\n> Backpressure: producers feel the overload (block/fail) instead of OOM. The bound + overflow policy is a design decision, not a tuning knob.\n\n## Modeling & Architecture\n\n**46. ⭐ Entity vs value object?**\n> Entity: identity + lifecycle + mutable state (equality by id). VO: is its attributes, immutable, freely shared (equality by value). Push everything possible to VO.\n\n**47. Aggregate root — why does it matter?**\n> Single entry point owning a consistency boundary: invariants enforced once, natural lock scope, small API. Reference other aggregates by ID.\n\n**48. Repository pattern in one line?**\n> Collection-like interface for aggregate roots, domain-named queries; in-memory today, DB tomorrow, tests forever.\n\n**49. Why constructor injection over field injection/statics?**\n> Dependencies explicit and final, object valid at construction, trivially testable with fakes, no framework needed — a main() wiring is enough.\n\n**50. ⭐ How do you make a design testable?**\n> Inject seams: repositories, Clock, external gateways as interfaces; pure logic in entities/VOs; side effects at edges. Then tests need no infra and no mocks-of-everything.\n\n**51. Idempotency — define and give the standard mechanism.**\n> Same operation applied twice = applied once. Mechanism: caller-supplied idempotency key, dedupe store checked atomically before effect; or naturally idempotent writes (set-status).\n\n**52. Event sourcing in miniature (LLD scale)?**\n> Store the operations (moves, expenses, commands), derive state by folding; gives audit, undo, replay. Chess history and Splitwise ledger are exactly this.\n\n**53. When do you introduce an interface?**\n> Stated variation axis, external system boundary, or test seam. Not for \"just in case\" — one forever-implementation is ceremony.\n\n**54. State machine: enum+switch vs State pattern — the boundary?**\n> 2–3 states with trivial transitions → enum + transition table. Rich per-state behavior across many events → state classes (matrix completeness by construction).\n\n## Curveballs\n\n**55. \"Design anything you like in 10 minutes\" — what do you pick?**\n> A rate limiter or LRU cache: small enough to finish, deep enough to show strategy seams, algorithms, and concurrency in one arc.\n\n**56. \"What's the worst code you've written?\"**\n> Have a real story: the smell, the consequence, the refactor, the lesson (e.g., god service → split by reason-to-change). Self-awareness is the point.\n\n**57. \"Estimate classes needed for X\" (weird but asked)?**\n> Refuse the number gracefully: \"entities + VOs + one service per flow + interfaces per variation axis — for this problem that lands around N; the count follows the model, not vice versa.\"\n\n**58. \"Is Java OOP done right?\"**\n> Diplomatic depth: great — interfaces, strong typing, now records/sealed/patterns for data modeling; warts — primitives split, arrays covariance, checked exceptions, pre-record boilerplate. Modern Java closes most gaps.\n\n**59. \"Convince me NOT to use microservices for this.\"**\n> One process, one team, entangled invariants (seat + payment + booking) → a modular monolith with clean aggregates wins: transactions instead of sagas, refactoring instead of API versioning. Distribution is a cost you pay when team/scale forces it.\n\n**60. ⭐ \"Any questions for me?\" — the LLD-round version.**\n> Ask about *their* design reality: \"What's the most contended piece of state in your system and how do you protect it?\" or \"What does code review push back on most here?\" — signals you think about the craft, and their answer teaches you about the team.\n\n---\n\n## 💡 Tips\n- Practice these out loud, 20 per day for 3 days — verbal fluency is the whole game in rapid-fire rounds.\n- Every ⭐ deserves a written-from-memory version in your own words.\n- If an answer draws a follow-up you can't handle, the chapter reference is your study pointer: patterns → Part 2, concurrency → Part 3, modeling → Ch 4.3.\n"}, {"id": "06-interview-prep/04-tips-and-red-flags", "path": "06-interview-prep/04-tips-and-red-flags.md", "part": "Part 6 · Interview Prep", "title": "6.4 Tips, Tricks & Red Flags — What Separates Senior Candidates", "md": "# 6.4 Tips, Tricks & Red Flags — What Separates Senior Candidates\n\n> The last-mile chapter: behaviors that flip borderline rounds, the red flags that quietly end them, and the exact prep plan. Read this the night before every LLD interview.\n\n---\n\n## The 10 highest-leverage behaviors\n\n1. **Narrate decisions, not code.** \"I'm locking on the seat so contention is per-seat\" > silently typing `synchronized`. The interviewer scores what they hear.\n2. **Volunteer the hard part first.** Raise the double-booking race, the rounding paisa, the boundary burst — before they do. It flips you from examinee to co-designer.\n3. **Take every hint instantly.** \"What if fees change?\" = build the seam *now*, thank them implicitly by using it.\n4. **Say trade-offs in pairs.** Never \"I'll use X\" — always \"X over Y because Z.\" One sentence, every decision.\n5. **Fix your own mistakes out loud.** Caught-and-corrected beats lucky-and-silent. The review instinct is the hire signal.\n6. **Calibrate abstraction.** Interface on the stated variation axis; honest `if` on the two-variant case; and *say which is which*.\n7. **Keep one flow fully alive.** A demoable park→unpark beats five half-built features — always.\n8. **Use domain vocabulary.** Hold, ledger, idempotency key, aggregate, happens-before — precise words are compressed proofs of experience.\n9. **State your assumptions as assumptions.** \"Assuming single currency — flag me if not\" leaves doors open instead of walls.\n10. **Land the closing summary.** Coverage, key decisions, named debts — 30 rehearsed seconds (Ch 6.1 Q4).\n\n## Red flags that end rounds (interviewers' actual list)\n\n| Red flag | What the interviewer writes down |\n|---|---|\n| Codes for 10 minutes before any question | \"Doesn't gather requirements\" |\n| God class / anemic entities | \"Weak OO fundamentals\" |\n| `double` for money, string statuses, public fields | \"Production-unready instincts\" |\n| Pattern-bingo (6 patterns, no reasons) | \"Cargo cult, over-engineers\" |\n| Concurrency never mentioned in a booking problem | \"Misses the core risk\" |\n| Defends a broken design after a clear counterexample | \"Uncoachable\" |\n| \"That edge case won't happen\" | \"Ships bugs\" |\n| Silent for long stretches | \"Can't collaborate\" |\n| Rewrites everything at minute 30 | \"No incremental instincts\" |\n| Claims exactly-once/thread-safe without justification | \"Hand-waves hard problems\" |\n\n## Tricks of the format\n\n- **Whiteboard rounds:** diagram quality matters more than code volume — invest in the class diagram + one sequence; code only the 2–3 methods with invariants.\n- **Machine coding:** the Ch 4.1 budget; demo main is non-negotiable; commit history (if git) tells your story — commit per working feature.\n- **Follow-up-heavy rounds (senior):** answers should *end with a hook* — \"…and that's also where I'd plug pricing experiments\" invites the next question onto your strong ground.\n- **Pair-style rounds:** verbalize options and *ask which they prefer* at genuine forks — collaboration is the rubric line.\n- **Take-home LLD:** README with decisions + trade-offs is worth as much as the code; include the tests they'll try to break you with (concurrency, rounding, expiry).\n\n## The 4-week preparation plan\n\n```\nWeek 1 — Foundations: Part 1 + Part 4 (modeling & clean code).\n          Daily: 1 chapter + rewrite one past project's class in this style.\nWeek 2 — Patterns: Part 2. Daily: 1 chapter + implement 2 patterns from memory in Java.\n          Weekend: reproduce the Ch 2.5 map from memory.\nWeek 3 — Concurrency + first case studies: Part 3, then 5.1/5.3/5.4/5.5.\n          Daily: 1 thread-safe kata (bounded buffer, tryClaim, computeIfAbsent patterns).\nWeek 4 — Case studies at interview speed: remaining 5.x as timed 45-min runs (diagram\n          + narration + core code), 2/day. Finish with Part 6 + one recorded full mock.\n```\n\n**Golden rule (same as HLD):** after each chapter, close the notes and *design it aloud from scratch*. If you can't narrate it, you don't own it yet — reread. LLD is learned by designing, not by reading.\n\n## The night-before checklist\n\n- [ ] Reproduce the Ch 6.2 master table from memory (problems × patterns × traps).\n- [ ] Type the 7 snippets cold (atomic claim, computeIfAbsent, sorted multi-lock, strategy map, transition table, money split, bounded queue).\n- [ ] Read the phrase bank (Ch 6.1) aloud once.\n- [ ] Rehearse the 30-second closing summary shape.\n- [ ] Pick your default stack answers: money=long paise; ids=typed records; time=injected Clock; storage=repository interfaces.\n- [ ] Sleep. A rested candidate who knows 80% beats an exhausted one who knows 95%.\n\n---\n\n## ✅ Key Takeaways\n- Rounds are won by narration, volunteered hard parts, taken hints, and paired trade-offs — behaviors, not knowledge.\n- The red-flag list is short and avoidable; know it cold.\n- Prep = timed designing aloud, not reading. The night-before checklist is 40 minutes.\n\n## 💡 Final Tips\n- Interviewers remember how the round *felt*: momentum, honesty, and recovery from mistakes weigh more than any single decision.\n- When in doubt, return to the triad: **what varies (seam it), what's invariant (guard it), what's shared (lock it).** Nearly every LLD question is one of the three.\n- You've built all 13 systems in this app. Walk in knowing you've already done the job's hard part — repeatedly.\n\n## 🎯 Interview Q&A\n\n**Q1. What single habit most reliably upgrades an LLD performance?**\n> Decision narration with reasons — \"X over Y because Z\" on every choice. It converts invisible thinking into scoreable evidence, catches your own errors as you hear them, invites course-correcting hints early, and produces the collaborative feel interviewers rate. Candidates with identical designs score a full band apart on narration alone.\n\n**Q2. How do you recover when the interviewer disproves your design with a counterexample?**\n> Concede fast and specifically — \"right: two holds can interleave there\" — then repair in place with the smallest change that restores the invariant, and add the test/scenario that would have caught it. What's being measured in that moment is coachability and debugging instinct, not the original bug. The only losing moves are defending, deflecting, or full rewrites.\n\n**Q3. Is it better to appear knowledgeable or honest when you don't know something?**\n> Honest, structured, and curious — \"I haven't implemented that; reasoning from what I know: …\" — then reason visibly and invite correction. Interviewers calibrate against bluffing constantly and it's easy to detect with one follow-up. A principled guess labeled as a guess scores; a confident wrong answer un-labeled ends trust for the whole round.\n\n**Q4. How different is LLD prep for a 3-YOE vs a 10-YOE candidate?**\n> Same material, different emphasis. At 3 YOE: execution — clean code fast, patterns applied correctly, the script followed; interviewers forgive scope narrowness. At 10 YOE: judgment — scope negotiation, what *not* to build, trade-off fluency, connecting class design to system consequences and team realities (\"this seam is where two teams' code meets\"). Seniors fail by over-proving coding speed and under-showing judgment; juniors fail the reverse. Calibrate to the altitude they're hiring for.\n\n**Q5. Final question: how do you keep these skills after the interview?**\n> Practice on the job deliberately: every feature is a mini-LLD round — write the two-line design note (what varies/invariant/shared) before coding; refactor one smell per week toward the principles here; and re-derive one case study monthly from memory. Interview skills and engineering skills converge at exactly this material — that's why it's worth owning, not memorizing.\n"}];
